t is welcome to attend.\n\nGoToWebinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 15:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://register.gotowebinar.com/register/7233606093122871391\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":257,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":true},{"id":267,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":true},{"id":265,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":true},{"id":266,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2757,"created":1790349767000,"updated":1790350334000,"title":"Monitoring Surface Water with SAR for Water Resource Management","titleNative":"Monitoring Surface Water with SAR for Water Resource Management","startDate":1792508400000,"endDate":1793120400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov ","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/monitoring-surface-water-sar-water-resource-management","description":"Monitoring surface water is critical for supporting agriculture, sustaining ecosystem health, and managing water resources. The recent availability of Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) data from the NASA-ISRO Synthetic Aperture Radar (NISAR) mission provides users with high-resolution, consistent observations of Earth's surface every 12 days. These observations are particularly valuable in tropical and flood-prone regions, where frequent cloud cover and varying illumination often limit the effectiveness of optical remote sensing. \n\nComplementing these observations, NASA's Observational Products for End-Users from Remote Sensing Analysis (OPERA) project is producing global, analysis-ready surface water products that provide readily accessible information for researchers, resource managers, and decision-makers.\n\nThis intermediate-level training will introduce participants to complementary approaches for monitoring surface water using NISAR and OPERA. Participants will learn how to use NISAR SAR amplitude data to generate their own flood maps by identifying open water and inundated vegetation, as well as how to use the analysis-ready OPERA Surface Water Extent product for monitoring surface water and to support water management activities. The training will highlight the strengths, limitations, and appropriate applications of each approach.\n\nThe training will cover the fundamental principles of how radar detects surface inundation, the characteristics of NISAR and OPERA datasets, and considerations for selecting and interpreting these data for different applications. Through hands-on demonstrations, participants will learn how to access, visualize, and analyze NISAR and OPERA data using open-source software, gaining practical experience with workflows for surface water monitoring and water resource management.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, participants will be able to:\n\nDifferentiate radar backscatter characteristics in order to identify open water and inundated vegetation.\n\nGenerate a flood map from NISAR SAR amplitude data using open-source software.\n\nCompare the workflows for deriving a flood map from NISAR observations and using the analysis-ready OPERA Surface Water Extent product.\n\nEvaluate when it is more appropriate to generate a flood map from NISAR observations versus using the OPERA Surface Water Extent product.\n\nAccess, visualize, and interpret OPERA Surface Water Extent products for flood and surface water monitoring applications.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Water resource managers, flood managers, and disaster response managers from federal, state, and local government agencies, NGOs, and private sector organizations.\n\nStudents and academics who are interested in radar remote sensing for water resources applications.","registrationHowto":"Please register here: https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/monitoring-surface-water-sar-water-resource-management\n\nCourse Format\nThe complete course consists of two 2-hour parts, with Part 1 offered on October 20 and Part 2 on October 27.\n\nOn each day, there are two opportunities to take the course (one in English, one in Spanish):\nEnglish: 11:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nSpanish: 2:00 p.m. to 4:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\n\nEach part will include a 30-minute live Q&A.\n\nThose who attend Parts 1 and 2 and complete the homework by the due date will receive a certificate of attendance.","registrationEndDate":1793113200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":31,"title":"Surface features","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":10,"title":"Floods","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2758,"created":1791190460000,"updated":null,"title":"From Product to Process: How Universities and Training Teams Are Rethinking Assessment in the Age of Generative AI","titleNative":"From Product to Process: How Universities and Training Teams Are Rethinking Assessment in the Age of Generative AI","startDate":1792504800000,"endDate":1792508400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":"EUMETCAL","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/ac857d95-fed5-49eb-8f59-78a1a0a826a4@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","description":"Generative AI can now write a polished paper and successfully complete a multiple-choice test. This raises an important question for educators and trainers: If the final product no longer tells us whether a learner did the thinking or the work, what should we assess instead?\n\nIn this webinar, we will explore how universities and corporate training departments are starting to rethink assessment in response to Generative AI.\nWe will look at approaches ranging from AI-conducted oral exams to assessing the learning process rather than only the final product, and review practical examples of what trainers can already do to apply some of these approaches in their own work.\n\nWhether you work in education, training, instructional design, or are simply interested in how GenAI is changing the way we assess learning, you are very welcome to join us.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/ac857d95-fed5-49eb-8f59-78a1a0a826a4@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGro