","contactEmail":"Ibrahim.Abdulsalam@caa.gov.om","contactUrl":null,"description":"This three-day online event takes participants on a virtual journey across “forecasting ports” in Africa, the Middle East, and the Mediterranean.\n\nIt aims to strengthen the operational use of satellite data for oceanic and marine–atmospheric monitoring, supporting early warning systems, climate services, and marine safety through discussions on NHMS services and satellite applications along the journey.\n\nThe event also promotes regional networking and knowledge exchange among WMO Centres of Excellence (CoEs), forecasting centers, and experts.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"* Apply satellite data more effectively for marine and oceanic monitoring.\n* Improve understanding of marine weather services and forecasting practices across different regions.\n* Learn from operational case studies and NMHS experiences.\n* Support marine safety, climate services, and early warning systems through satellite applications.\n* Strengthen professional networks and collaboration among WMO CoEs, forecasting centers, and experts.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"* Marine Weather Forecasters and Oceanographers\n* Operational Weather Forecasters\n* Satellite Meteorologists\n* Tropical Cyclone Forecasters and Coastal Hazard Specialists\n","registrationHowto":"You will receive an email upon registering using the link below: \nhttps://eumetsat.zoom.us/meeting/register/zyvQYc0rSJCSAQTW77Sp6g","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5879,"name":"Ibrahim Al Abdulsalam","email":"Ibrahim.AbdulSalam@caa.gov.om","agency":"","country":"OM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":7,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":68,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":262,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":4,"value":"BIP-H: Basic Instructional Package for Hydrologist"},{"id":11,"value":"Marine: Marine Weather Forecaster"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2722,"created":1781528977000,"updated":1781529108000,"title":"Estimating Surface PM2.5 Using Satellite Data and Other Information Sources","titleNative":"Estimating Surface PM2.5 Using Satellite Data and Other Information Sources","startDate":1783519200000,"endDate":1784736000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/estimating-surface-pm2.5-using-satellite-data-other-information-sources","description":"This training covers the theoretical approaches and key datasets for estimating the near-surface concentration of particulate matter smaller than 2.5 micrometers in diameter (PM2.5) by using satellite aerosol optical depth (AOD) and other data sources (including ground-based monitors and model estimates). \n\nParticipants will acquire knowledge about different methodologies, and their strengths and limitations, for inferring surface PM2.5 from satellite AOD. They will be able to identify access methods for using existing datasets of surface PM2.5 estimated from satellite sensors and other data sources. They will also learn how to compare satellite-based estimates of PM2.5 to in situ measurement data through hands-on coding exercises using a generic code template and suitable co-location methodology. This would be the first step in evaluating the suitability of these datasets for the users’ applications and regions.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nMatch the required inputs, strengths, and limitations with different basic methods of inferring surface PM2.5 from satellite AOD.\n\nDifferentiate between available data products for surface PM2.5 from NASA and Washington University in St. Louis based on their methodologies, strengths, and weaknesses.\n\nUse NASA Earthdata and the SatPM website to access these surface PM2.5 data products for a location and time period of interest.\n\nCompare these satellite-derived PM2.5 estimates to in situ measurements using appropriate co-location strategies and evaluation metrics.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Researchers looking to use surface air quality estimates derived from satellites and looking to better understand the methodologies and limitations\n\nAir quality managers seeking to understand the potential for satellites to supplement ground-based monitors in remote areas","registrationHowto":"Register on the training webpage:\nhttps://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/estimating-surface-pm2.5-using-satellite-data-other-information-sources\n\nThe complete course consists of three 2-hour parts, with Part 1 offered on July 8, Part 2 on July 15, and Part 3 on July 22.\n\nOn each day, there are two opportunities to take the course (one in English, one in Spanish):\n\nEnglish Session: 10:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nSpanish Session: 3:00 p.m. to 5:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\n\nEach part includes a 30-minute live Q&A.\n\nThose who attend Parts 1, 2, and 3 and complete the homework by the due date will receive a certificate of attendance.","registrationEndDate":1784728800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"title":"Aerosols","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":22,"title":"Air quality","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":17,"name":"Aqua/Terra"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":189,"name":"VIIRS","uniqueName":"VIIRS (JPSS)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":46,"name":"MODIS","uniqueName":"MODIS (Aqua/Terra)"}]},{"id":2723,"created":1781614678000,"updated":1781614907000,"title":"Species Distribution Modeling with Google Earth Engine","titleNative":"Species Distribution Modeling with Google Earth Engine","startDate":1783429200000,"endDate":1784039400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/species-distribution-modeling-google-earth-engine","description":"Species distribution models (SDMs), sometimes referred to as ecological niche or habitat suitability models, are a commonly used analytical technique to link known species locations with environmental predictor variables and assess patterns of species occurrence and habitat suitability. SDMs have been successfully used to model the distributions of various species, from butterflies to baleen whales, with a goal of using associations with habitat variables — such as elevation, vegetation greenness, or distance to human settlements — to map the potential distribution to a species across a landscape or seascape. \n\nTo implement SDMs, we will fit models using Google Earth Engine (GEE), a cloud-based spatial analysis platform that can simplify the process of accessing and analyzing huge quantities of remotely sensed data.  \n\nThis course begins by introducing key SDM concepts for participants working in ecology, conservation, or wildlife biology. The course then provides a basic introduction to Google Earth Engine (GEE) and JavaScript coding, before moving on to spatial data manipulation and example workflows for species distribution mapping in GEE. \n\nParticipants need no prior experience working with GEE, but we assume participants have a basic understanding of GIS data and concepts, such as rasters (including stacks or multi-band images), vectors, projections, and spatial resolution.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nConduct basic operations and functions in the Google Earth Engine (GEE) web interface using JavaScript code.\n\nIdentify core concepts and applications of species distribution modeling (SDM).\n\nAssess key SDM workflow decisions to ensure occurrence point independence, to select background points, and to choose ecologically relevant predictors.\n\nAccess and manage analysis-ready spatial data from the GEE catalog as predictor variables.\n\nFit species distribution models using machine learning models (e.g., Random Forest) in GEE using a reproducible code-based workflow.\n\nCritically evaluate the appropriateness of the model design and interpret predicted distribution and habitat suitability results.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"NGOs and technicians, GIS officers, and academics\n\nEarly career scientists and postgraduate students","registrationHowto":"Please register on the training webpage: \n\nhttps://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/species-distribution-modeling-google-earth-engine\n\nCourse Format\nThe complete course consists of two 1.5-hour parts, with Part 1 offered on July 7 and Part 2 on July 14.\n\nOn each day, there are two opportunities to take the course (identical offerings):\n\nSession A: 9:00 a.m. to 10:30 a.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nSession B: 4:00 p.m. to 5:30 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\n\nEach part will include a 30-minute live Q&A.\n\nThose who attend Parts 1 and 2 and complete the homework by the due date will receive a certificate of attendance.","registrationEndDate":1784034000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":42,"name":"Landsat"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":17,"name":"Aqua/Terra"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":46,"name":"MODIS","uniqueName":"MODIS (Aqua/Terra)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":110,"name":"Thematic Mapper","uniqueName":"Thematic Mapper (Landsat)"}]},{"id":2670,"created":1767950594000,"updated":1775031972000,"title":"IOCCG Summer Lecture Series","titleNative":"IOCCG Summer Lecture Series","startDate":1783238400000,"endDate":1784480400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Venice","host":"IOCCG","contactPerson":"Raisha Lovindeer","contactEmail":"raisha@ioccg.org","contactUrl":"https://ioccg.org/what-we-do/training-and-education/ioccg-sls/","description":"The 7th edition of the advanced IOCCG Summer Lecture Series is scheduled for 5-19 July 2026, in Venice, Italy, hosted by the Institute of Marine Sciences (ISMAR) from the Italian National Council of Research (CNR) and the **[Venice International University (VIU)](https://www.univiu.org/)**. The course will be held at the VIU campus, on the island of San Servolo, located in the Venetian lagoon between the Venice city centre and the island of Lido.\n\nAs in **[previous years](https://ioccg.org/what-we-do/training-and-education/lectures/)**, this high-level training course will be dedicated to the fundamentals of ocean optics, bio-optics and ocean colour remote sensing. Several experienced research scientists will provide lectures on cutting edge research, with focus on current critical issues in ocean colour science. Students will have ample opportunity to meet with lecturers for in-depth discussions on various pre-selected topics, as well as on their own scientific research.\n\nStudents will learn methods for measuring optical properties and interpreting the results. Practical sessions will also be conducted to help students understand absorption, scattering and the colour of the ocean.\n\nThe Summer Lecture Series is an advanced training course targeted at students conducting ocean color research, and is not suitable for beginners with limited knowledge of remote sensing.  The course will address theoretical aspects and advanced science questions, rather than practical applications.","country":{"id":"IT","name":"ITALY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"The emphasis of the course will be on current critical issues in ocean colour science, and will consist of lecture-style discussions, with a few hands-on practical and/or field sessions. A range of topics will be covered in the course and will depend upon the expertise of the invited lecturers. Following subject areas will most likely be included (to be updated, once lecturers have been confirmed):\n\n* Inherent optical properties (inversion and applications in coastal and open ocean waters).\n* Errors and uncertainties in ocean colour remote sensing (emphasis on inverse modelling).\n* Radiative transfer in the ocean: shallow water remote sensing and HydroLight training.\n* Ocean colour remote sensing in optically-complex coastal waters.\n* Atmospheric correction.\n* Retrieving phytoplankton biomass and functional groups.\n* In situ measurements.","summary":"The 7th edition of the advanced IOCCG Summer Lecture Series is scheduled for 5-19 July 2026, in Venice, Italy, hosted by the Institute of Marine Sciences (ISMAR) from the Italian N","content":null,"targetAudience":"The lecture series is open to students from all countries, with no preference for geographical location, however, fluency in English is required. Participants should be familiar with downloading and processing satellite ocean colour imagery, since this is not an introductory training course. The course is primarily intended for PhD students and post-doctoral fellows involved in any aspect of ocean colour research. However, outstanding graduate students with a Masters degree (or studying for their Masters degree), with a strong understanding of ocean colour remote sensing, are also encouraged to apply. Research scientists with more than 10 years of ocean colour research, who are hoping to broaden their knowledge, will also be considered. Once selected, all students are required to attend in person for the full two weeks of the lecture series.","registrationHowto":"Applications for the 7th IOCCG Summer Lecture Series are now open. The deadline for applications is 15 February 2026. Applicants will be notified about the success of their application by the end of March 2026. Please see the **[course page](https://ioccg.org/what-we-do/training-and-education/ioccg-sls/)** for more details on how to apply.","registrationEndDate":1771718340000,"fees":null,"awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5877,"name":"Ben Loveday","email":"Ben.Loveday@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":237,"value":"IOCCG","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":12,"title":"Ocean biogeochemistry","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":162,"name":"OLCI","uniqueName":"OLCI (Sentinel-3)"}]},{"id":2721,"created":1781528543000,"updated":1781528832000,"title":"Harnessing NISAR: Next-Generation Radar Observations for Earth Applications","titleNative":"Harnessing NISAR: Next-Generation Radar Observations for Earth Applications","startDate":1783006200000,"endDate":1784223000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/harnessing-nisar-next-generation-radar-observations-earth-applications","description":"Synthetic aperture radar (SAR) data is becoming increasingly accessible through open datasets, including those from the newly launched NISAR mission. One of SAR’s main advantages is its ability to observe Earth's surface by day or night and under most weather conditions, making it especially valuable in persistently cloudy regions like the tropics or in high-latitude areas with long periods of darkness.\n\nNISAR provides a wealth of data for both scientific research and practical applications, but it can be challenging for users to interpret and apply. This course is designed to help users understand the unique characteristics of NISAR data and learn how to access, visualize, and analyze them for various scientific and applied purposes.\n\nThis training consists of three sessions:\n\nIntroduction to the NISAR mission — covering its capabilities and data products, with a focus on amplitude data and its relevance for ecosystem monitoring. This session includes a demonstration of the use of NISAR data for flood detection and monitoring.\n\nData access and analysis tools — practical guidance on accessing, visualizing, and analyzing NISAR data by using resources available from Earthdata and the Alaska Satellite Facility, as well as ISRO's Bhoonidhi portal.\n\nInterferometric SAR (InSAR) data — for identifying disaster risk areas and assessing damage following extreme events. The demonstration portion of this session focuses on one case study related to identifying areas at risk for landslides and another for assessing earthquakes.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, participants will be able to:\n\nIdentify the characteristics, capabilities, and limitations of NISAR data.\n\nRecognize how NISAR data can be applied to decision-making related to flooding, agriculture, earthquakes, and landslides.\n\nDifferentiate between the various NISAR data products and their applicability for different real-world use cases.\n\nDemonstrate how to access NISAR data and tools via the ASF DAAC platform to search, visualize, and analyze data for case studies related to floods, earthquakes, landslides, and agriculture.\n\nGenerate a flood map, landslide risk map, and earthquake deformation map for an event of interest using provided Jupyter Notebooks and exporting them into a GIS framework (e.g. QGIS).\n\nDemonstrate how to access NISAR data via the ISRO Bhoonidhi platform, and use available tools for basic visualization and analysis.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Remote sensing end users, geospatial analysts, academics, and students\n\nManagers from local, state, and federal government and NGOs\n\nPractitioners of remote sensing data","registrationHowto":"Register on the training webpage:\nhttps://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/harnessing-nisar-next-generation-radar-observations-earth-applications\n\nThe complete course consists of three 2-hour sessions, with Session 1 offered on July 2, Session 2 on July 9, and Session 3 on July 16.\n\nOn each day, there are two opportunities to take the course (one in English, one in Spanish):\n\nEnglish Session: 11:30 a.m. to 1:30 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nSpanish Session: 2:30 p.m. to 4:30 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\n\nEach session will include a 30-minute live Q&A.\n\nThose who attend Sessions 1, 2, and 3 and complete the homework by the due date will receive a certificate of attendance.","registrationEndDate":1784215800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":31,"title":"Surface features","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":10,"title":"Floods","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2720,"created":1781257332000,"updated":1782994908000,"title":"African Quarterly Weather Briefing ","titleNative":"African Quarterly Weather Briefing ","startDate":1782723600000,"endDate":1782727200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"South African Weather Service ","contactPerson":"Matshidiso Mogale","contactEmail":"met.training@weathersa.co.za","contactUrl":"https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=615#coursecontentcollapse1","description":"The South African Weather Service (SAWS), in collaboration with EUMETSAT, invites you to join the African Weather Briefing. Open to all, this regular forum explores significant weather events across Africa using a range of satellite products and numerical weather prediction (NWP) models.\n\nLearning outcomes: \n* Interpret various MSG and MTG satellite products\n* Analyse and diagnose the current weather situation\n* Assess potential weather hazards","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"The South African Weather Service (SAWS), in collaboration with EUMETSAT, invites you to join the African Weather Briefing. Open to all, this regular forum explores significant wea","content":null,"targetAudience":"Meteorologists","registrationHowto":"To join the first briefing of this year, please\n[register](https://eumetsat.zoom.us/meeting/register/I0BAmXnUR0i9qyzvfg664w).","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":454,"name":"Jannie Stander","email":"jannie.stander@weathersa.co.za","agency":" South African Weather Service","country":"ZA","programme":" ","trainingPartner":{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":258,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2714,"created":1781034604000,"updated":1781097304000,"title":"Processing EUMETSAT Data for Operational Weather Applications","titleNative":"Traitement des données EUMETSAT pour la météo opérationnelle","startDate":1782691740000,"endDate":1783083600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Casablanca","host":"General Directorate of Meteorology of Morocco ","contactPerson":"Carla Barroso","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"Cette formation vise à renforcer les capacités des professionnels de la météorologie dans l’accès, le traitement, la visualisation et l’interprétation des données issues du système satellitaire Meteosat Troisième Génération (MTG).\n\nAnimée par des experts de la Direction de la Météorologie Nationale (DGM) du Maroc, de Météo-France, de l’EAMAC et d’EUMETSAT, cette formation permettra aux participants d’acquérir une vue d’ensemble des programmes satellitaires d’EUMETSAT et des services de diffusion de données, notamment EUMETView, EUMETCast et l’EUMETSAT Data Store. Le cours présentera les derniers produits du Flexible Combined Imager (FCI) de MTG ainsi que les observations du Lightning Imager (LI), en mettant l’accent sur leurs applications pour la surveillance du temps et la prévision météorologique en Afrique.\n\nLes participants apprendront à accéder aux données satellitaires et à les traiter à l’aide de Python et des notebooks Jupyter. Ils exploreront également la génération et l’interprétation de produits satellitaires tels que les composites RGB, les images « sandwich » et d’autres visualisations dérivées à l’aide d’outils modernes comme Satpy. Grâce à des exercices pratiques guidés, ils produiront des images satellitaires personnalisées, sélectionneront des zones d’intérêt et développeront des chaînes de traitement adaptées à une utilisation opérationnelle des données satellitaires.\n\nAfin d’illustrer la valeur opérationnelle des observations MTG, les participants analyseront des études de cas météorologiques et mettront en pratique les compétences acquises pour soutenir les activités de prévision météorologique et de surveillance environnementale.\n","country":{"id":"MA","name":"MOROCCO","region":"RA-I","type":"RA-I"},"learningOutcome":"Au terme de cet atelier, les participants seront capables de :\n\n* Comprendre les mécanismes généraux de diffusion des données satellitaires d'EUMETSAT.\n\n* Maîtriser et utiliser de manière autonome les outils dédiés à l'accès, au traitement et à la visualisation des données satellitaires pour un usage météorologique opérationnel.\n\n* Manipuler et exploiter efficacement les données satellitaires afin de mener des analyses météorologiques précises et de soutenir la recherche appliquée.\n\n* Concevoir et développer des supports pédagogiques en météorologie satellitaire, notamment à travers l'élaboration d'études de cas concrètes pour la formation.\n\n* Intégrer de façon opérationnelle les méthodes et les produits satellitaires au sein de leurs activités météorologiques quotidiennes.","summary":"Cette formation vise à renforcer les capacités des professionnels de la météorologie dans l’accès, le traitement, la visualisation et l’interprétation des données issues du système","content":null,"targetAudience":"L'atelier s'adresse aux participants africains spécialisés dans l'exploitation des données satellitaires.","registrationHowto":"Les candidats sont désignés par les représentants permanents.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5848,"name":"Carla Barroso ","email":"carla.barroso@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency"