l areas, polar seas, and inland waters.\n\nIn addition, and following on from the successful launch of the Copernicus Sentinel-6B satellite in November 2025, this OSTST meeting will place a strong focus on the commissioning status and activities of the mission. \n\nIt will take place jointly, with meetings dedicated to the Sentinel-6B validation team and the international DORIS service, where teams will report on the findings from the satellite’s commissioning.","country":{"id":"DE","name":"GERMANY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"The Ocean surface topography science team meeting (OSTST) is a joint endeavour between EUMETSAT, the European Space Agency, CNES (France’s space agency), NASA, NASA Jet Propulsion ","content":null,"targetAudience":"The meeting is open to international partners, global users of Copernicus altimeter data such as from Jason-3, Sentinel-3 and Sentinel-6, users of altimeter data provided by other space agencies, eg, CryoSat-2, SARAL/AltiKa, SWOT, HY-2, as well as altimetry specialists from EUMETSAT ESA, NASA, NOAA, CNES, ISRO etc., and those working on future altimetry missions.","registrationHowto":"For full event information, including abstract submission and registration please visit the **[Ocean Surface Topography Science Team Meeting 2026 event webpage](https://www.eventsforce.net/eumetsat/frontend/reg/thome.csp?pageID=31367&ef_sel_menu=510&eventID=81)**.\n\nPlease note that **abstract submission closes on 3 April**.\n","registrationEndDate":1782115200000,"fees":"Yes","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5877,"name":"Ben Loveday","email":"Ben.Loveday@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":110,"value":"NASA","visible":true},{"id":223,"value":"ESA","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":272,"value":"JPL","visible":true},{"id":273,"value":"CNES","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[{"id":3,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":6,"title":"Marine","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":20,"name":"WEkEO"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":69,"name":"SWOT"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":9,"name":"Sentinel-6"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"name":"Jason"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":190,"name":"Poseidon-3C","uniqueName":"Poseidon-3C (SWOT)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":164,"name":"SRAL","uniqueName":"SRAL (Sentinel-3)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":102,"name":"Poseidon-3","uniqueName":"Poseidon-3 (Jason)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":103,"name":"Poseidon-3B","uniqueName":"Poseidon-3B (Jason)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":167,"name":"Poseidon-4","uniqueName":"Poseidon-4 (Sentinel-6)"}]},{"id":2710,"created":1780428473000,"updated":1780428501000,"title":"Monitoring and Predicting Floods Using Earth Observations for Planning and Preparedness","titleNative":"Monitoring and Predicting Floods Using Earth Observations for Planning and Preparedness","startDate":1781787600000,"endDate":1782408600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"broc.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/monitoring-predicting-floods-using-earth-observations-planning-preparedness","description":"Floods are the most common and widespread of all weather-related natural disasters. Lasting from minutes to weeks, floods can place a huge burden on a community, including loss of life, damage to infrastructure and economies, and long-term impacts on human health and well-being. Remote sensing observations and Earth system models, when combined with hydrologic models, can support both flood monitoring and prediction efforts to significantly improve preparedness and response planning.\n\nThis three-part training includes an overview and demonstration of flood monitoring tools based on remotely sensed optical observations, NASA's near real-time Global Flood Product, and several Observational Products for End-users from Remote Sensing Analysis (OPERA). Additionally, the training introduces a streamflow prediction tool — the Group on Earth Observations Global Water Sustainability (GEOGLOWS) model — which provides global, historical, and 15-day streamflow predictions. Trainers guide participants through hands-on exercises to access and visualize selected flood data.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nIdentify datasets in NASA's Global Flood Product, which is based on remotely sensed optical observations.\n\nUse the webtool, NASA Worldview, to access and visualize flooded regions from Global Flood Product, available from multiple satellites, from 2021 to near-real time.\n\nIdentify OPERA dynamic surface water extent data for flood detection derived from optical and SAR observations.\n\nAccess and visualize OPERA dynamic surface water extent data for flood events using NASA Worldview.\n\nIdentify the capabilities of GEOGLOWS River Forecast System (RFS) for global streamflow prediction.\n\nUse GEOGLOWS Hydroviewer to access globally available retrospective and predicted streamflow for selected rivers.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Local, regional, federal, and non-governmental organizations, as well as companies, involved in flood and water resources management, emergency response, disaster relief and rescue operations, flood insurance, and real estate and infrastructure building. \n\nStudents and academics who are interested in learning about remote sensing of flood monitoring and predictions.","registrationHowto":"Register on the training webpage: https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/monitoring-predicting-floods-using-earth-observations-planning-preparedness\n\nCourse Format\nThe complete course consists of three 1.5-hour parts, with Part 1 offered on June 18, Part 2 on June 23, and Part 3 on June 25.\nOn each day, there are two opportunities to take the course (identical offerings):\nSession A: 11:00 a.m. to 12:30 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nSession B: 2:00 p.m. to 3:30 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nEach part includes a 30-minute live Q&A.\nThose who attend Parts 1, 2, and 3 and complete the homework by the due date will receive a certificate of attendance.","registrationEndDate":1782392400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US