sse (Klassenphase).\n- Austausch der spezifischen Arbeitsweisen in den einzelnen Wetterdiensten.","summary":"Im DACH-Seminar 2026 liegt der Schwerpunkt auf:\n\n- Zyklogenese und Frontenanalyse sowie Tiefdruckgebiete.\n- Mechanismen bei Starkniederschlagsereignissen, Hagel und konvektiven Böe","content":null,"targetAudience":"Meteorologinnen und Meteorologen sowie Wetterberaterinnen und Wetterberater im Vorhersagedienst der beteiligten D-A-CH - Organisationen.","registrationHowto":"Anmeldung via lokaler Kontaktpunkt bis spätestens 31. Januar 2026.","registrationEndDate":1770595140000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":106,"value":"Seminar"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"BLENDED","attendeeCount":18,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":29,"value":"German","iso639":"de"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":7,"title":"Large-scale and synoptic systems","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":10,"title":"Floods","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"title":"Mesoscale systems and convective storms","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2700,"created":1776855055000,"updated":null,"title":"EUMeTrain Monthly Weather Briefing ","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Monthly Weather Briefing ","startDate":1777032000000,"endDate":1777034700000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMetrain","contactPerson":"Dominik Rukavina","contactEmail":"drukavina@dhz.hr ","contactUrl":"https://resources.eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Once a month EUMeTrain organizes a Weather Briefing open to everyone to discuss the present weather situation. The basis of the discussions are todays satellite images and the corresponding numerical weather prediction data. A basic knowledge on conceptual models used in satellite meteorology is of advantage to follow the presentations.\n\nThis session will be done through Webex.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Participants will learn which RGB products to use in a particular weather situation as well as how to interpret the products and single channels. ","summary":"Once a month EUMeTrain organizes a Weather Briefing open to everyone to discuss the present weather situation. The basis of the discussions are todays satellite images and the corr","content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters\n\nStudents\n\nResearchers","registrationHowto":"To join, go to: https://resources.eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html and follow the instructions.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9945,"name":"Dominik Rukavina","email":"drukavina@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"nan","country":"HR","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":17,"value":"EuMeTrain"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2691,"created":1774298318000,"updated":1774334256000,"title":"Monitoring Groundwater Changes for Water Resources Management","titleNative":"Monitoring Groundwater Changes for Water Resources Management","startDate":1776956400000,"endDate":1777572000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/monitoring-groundwater-changes-water-resources-management","description":"Groundwater is a vital resource, especially in arid regions where surface water is limited. The U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) reports approximately 82 billion gallons of groundwater are withdrawn daily in the U.S. for domestic, irrigation, industrial, mining, thermoelectric, livestock, and aquaculture uses. \n\nAccording to the National Groundwater Association (NGWA), groundwater accounts for about 29% of total U.S. water usage; about two thirds of that groundwater is used for irrigation. Aquifers in certain regions of the world are being depleted by unsustainable groundwater pumping to support irrigated agriculture, including in southern High Plains and Central Valley aquifers in the U.S., northern India, the North China Plain, and parts of the Middle East. \n\nThe Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE, 2002–2017) and GRACE Follow-On (GRACE-FO, 2018–present) satellite missions -- supported by NASA and the German Research Centre for Geosciences (GFZ) -- provide monthly estimates of changes in terrestrial water storage (the sum of groundwater, soil moisture, surface waters, snow, and ice) at spatial resolutions no better than about 150,000 km² at mid-latitudes. While useful for detection of large-scale changes, the coarse resolution limits regional-scale groundwater assessments. \n\nThere are, however, groundwater products with higher spatial resolutions, which can be useful for finer scale groundwater monitoring. A new product from Observational Products for End-Users from Remote Sensing Analysis (OPERA), provides surface displacement products (DISP) for North America indicating subsidence due to groundwater withdrawal based on Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) from Sentinel-1 at 30 m2 (and from NISAR as of next year). In addition, NASA's Global Land Data Assimilation System (GLDAS) assimilates GRACE/GRACE-FO data into a land surface model along with other, higher resolution inputs, producing daily outputs on a 0.25° (~25 km) grid. \n\nThis three-part training will focus on an overview of GRACE/GRACE-FO data, OPERA-DISP data, and GLDAS groundwater data for assessing seasonal to interannual groundwater changes at various spatial scales. The training will provide hands-on experience in accessing and analyzing these products for applications.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nIdentify characteristics of groundwater quantity datasets from GRACE/FO, OPERA, and GLDAS.\n\nAccess, analyze, and visualize GRACE/FO data to monitor seasonal to interannual changes in large-scale total terrestrial water storage.\n\nAccess, analyze, and visualize GLDAS data to monitor seasonal to interannual changes in groundwater using at regional scale.\n\nMap surface subsidence related to groundwater extraction with OPERA Displacement (DISP) data.\n\nUtilize groundwater products from these datasets at multiple spatial/temporal scales to make decisions in a variety of applications, such as monitoring drought and flood conditions, planning groundwater resources for irrigation.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Water resource, drought and flood managers, and irrigation managers from federal, state, and local government agencies, NGOs, and private sector organizations, both domestically and internationally.\n\nAcademic faculty and students, mining planners, groundwater recharge and water harvesting planners.","registrationHowto":"Please register on the training webpage: go.nasa.gov/4cw5Ogu \n\nFormat: \nThree, 2-hour parts\n\nTwo identical sessions will be hosted at two different times of day for each part:\nSession A: 11:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4): \nSession B: 2:00 p.m. to 4:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\n\nEach part will include a 30-minute live Q&A.\nThose who attend all parts and complete the homework by the due date will receive a certificate of attendance.","registrationEndDate":1774882800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":""