ions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"name":"EUMETCast Africa"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":138,"name":"FCI","uniqueName":"FCI (MTG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":140,"name":"LI","uniqueName":"LI (MTG)"}]},{"id":2687,"created":1773323694000,"updated":null,"title":"High Impact Weather webinar #1","titleNative":"High Impact Weather webinar #1","startDate":1775552400000,"endDate":1775556000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":"EUMETCAL","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/ui#/page/Newsitem81","description":"This “HIW course” is an online course for EUMETCAL and will focus on the use of a particular forecasting method based on Doswell’s ingredient-based forecasting method  IBM (1996). This method assumes that three ingredients are needed for convection: moisture, lift, instability (and vertical wind shear to take the modes in convection into account).\n\nThese convection modes each have their own distinctive characteristics as regards duration, radar images and, in particular, hazardous weather phenomena (lightning, wind gusts, hail and heavy precipitation). This method has been incorporated into a convection scheme that is currently used at the KNMI forecasting office; it leads, via a few steps, to one of four convection modes. This course e.g. explain the method and describe a number of typically synoptic situations that yield severe convection with some regularity.\n\nThe use of the convection prediction scheme in forecasting convection leads to a better physical understanding of convection, more objective forecasting methodologies and more accurate forecasts of hazardous weather phenomena. The convection prediction scheme can be used to make a good assessment of the probability of hazardous weather phenomena. The latter are rare in the case of single cells, for example, whereas pulse storms are frequently accompanied by strong downdrafts (wind gusts) and heavy precipitation and supercells are infamous for their prolonged duration, large hail, strong lightning activity and wind gusts.\n\nOn completion of this course, participants will be able to:\n- determine where and when deep convection will be initiated using the convection scheme (IBM),\n- find the overlapping regions of moisture, instability and lift,\n- determine the type of convection to expect: single cell/pulse cell, multi-cell, squall line or supercell,\n- determine the corresponding hazardous weather.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/8bcb4082-a1c2-48fe-b0b2-4115a83ef103@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2681,"created":1772192167000,"updated":1775814116000,"title":"Short Course: Introduction to Fire Products Pt 2 - Intermediate Users","titleNative":"Short Course: Introduction to Fire Products Pt 2 - Intermediate Users","startDate":1774962000000,"endDate":1774969200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Noemi Marsico","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_AjIhG4kATjSdWKw_OVRUjQ","description":"Building on the foundations introduced in the first session (10 March 2026), this second online session is designed as an expert-level course, focusing on more advanced scientific concepts and hands-on data analysis workflows for fire monitoring and impact assessment.\n\nThe course begins with a deeper theoretical overview of fire processes, atmospheric aerosols, and burned area dynamics. Participants will explore the physical principles behind Fire Radiative Power (FRP), aerosol transport, and post-fire landscape changes.\n\nFollowing the theoretical introduction, the session will move into a series of live demonstrations using Jupyter Notebooks within the WEkEO environment, showcasing practical workflows for accessing, analysing, and visualising Copernicus data.\n\nParticipants will:\n\n- Work with Sentinel-3 FRP data to monitor active fire intensity and temporal evolution.\n\n- Explore Sentinel-3 Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD) products to assess smoke plume dispersion and atmospheric impacts.\n\n- Use a dedicated Sentinel-2 notebook, applying the Normalized Burn Ratio (NBR) to map and analyse burned areas after a fire event.\n\nThrough these guided exercises, attendees will follow a complete workflow from data access to interactive analysis, gaining practical experience with advanced EO tools commonly used in fire science and operational monitoring.\n\nThis expert course complements the first basic session by moving beyond visualisation into quantitative analysis, enabling participants to better understand fire behaviour, atmospheric effects, and post-fire impacts using satellite data and cloud-based platforms.\n\nResources available here -> https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=600 ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of the session users should be able to:\n\n- Explain advanced concepts related to fire dynamics, aerosols, and burned area processes, and their impacts on the atmosphere and land surface.\n- Interpret Fire Radiative Power (FRP) data from Sentinel-3 to analyse active fire intensity and temporal evolution.\n- Analyse Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD) products from Sentinel-3 to assess smoke plume transport and atmospheric effects.\n- Apply Sentinel-2 Normalized Burn Ratio (NBR) techniques to map and evaluate burned areas following wildfire events.\n- Use Jupyter Notebooks within the WEkEO environment to access, process, and visualise EO fire-related datasets, following complete analysis workflows.","summary":"Building on the foundations introduced in the first session (10 March 2026), this second online session is designed as an expert-level course, focusing on more advanced scientific ","content":null,"targetAudience":"Intermediate users","registrationHowto":"Register on [Zoom](https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_AjIhG4kATjSdWKw_OVRUjQ).","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":11762,"name":"Noemi Marsico","email":"noemi.marsico@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null