 Briefing ","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Monthly Weather Briefing ","startDate":1772020800000,"endDate":1772023500000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMetrain","contactPerson":"Dominik Rukavina","contactEmail":"drukavina@dhz.hr","contactUrl":"https://resources.eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Once a month EUMeTrain organizes a Weather Briefing open to everyone to discuss the present weather situation. The basis of the discussions are todays satellite images and the corresponding numerical weather prediction data. A basic knowledge on conceptual models used in satellite meteorology is of advantage to follow the presentations.\n\nThis session will be done through MS Teams.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Participants will learn which RGB products to use in a particular weather situation as well as how to interpret the products and single channels. ","summary":"Once a month EUMeTrain organizes a Weather Briefing open to everyone to discuss the present weather situation. The basis of the discussions are todays satellite images and the corr","content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters\n\nStudents\n\nResearchers","registrationHowto":"To join, go to: https://resources.eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html and follow the instructions.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9945,"name":"Dominik Rukavina","email":"drukavina@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"nan","country":"HR","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":17,"value":"EuMeTrain"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2668,"created":1766521573000,"updated":1766559788000,"title":"Visualizing Land Cover and Land Use Change with NASA Satellite Imagery","titleNative":"Visualizing Land Cover and Land Use Change with NASA Satellite Imagery","startDate":1771948800000,"endDate":1772226000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/visualizing-land-cover-land-use-change-nasa-satellite-imagery","description":"Land cover and land use change (LCLUC) describes the many ways our world changes around us. Every new building, each vacant lot, and the expansion and retreat of forests or wetlands are all examples of LCLUC. These changes may be slow or sudden, but understanding the ways in which our landscape is changing gives us the opportunity to better anticipate and avoid problems that may arise. \n\nThis training, led by NASA's Applied Remote Sensing Training program (ARSET), covers some of the ways that we can use NASA data to determine what is happening on the ground and how that changes over time. Participants will see examples of supervised and unsupervised classification models implemented in the R statistical coding language, and improve their understanding of how these models can be used to produce maps and metrics of LCLUC.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\n\nAccess NASA Earth observation data (e.g., Harmonized Landsat and Sentinel-2 (HLS)) relevant to LCLUC mapping.\n\nConvert NASA Earth observation data into distinct land cover and land use (LCLU) classes using supervised and unsupervised machine learning classification methods in the R programming language.\n\nRecognize the role of classification methods as one part of a change monitoring strategy.\n\nCompute a change matrix representing the change in LCLU between two dates.\n\nCreate a map in RStudio visualizing the differences in LCLU between two dates.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Professionals interested in coding to quantify land cover change as it relates to phenomena such as forest composition change, deforestation, urban expansion, habitat loss, and hydro/cryosphere changes.\nOther scientists or analysts interested in leveraging automated tools for decision support around land use management, natural resource management, or other issues that have an impact on how our world is structured across space and time.","registrationHowto":"Please register here: https://go.nasa.gov/4s2XnyJ","registrationEndDate":1772121600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":31,"title":"Surface features","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":42,"name":"Landsat"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2675,"created":1769695115000,"updated":null,"title":"Latest on FCI in weather analysis and nowcast","titleNative":"Latest on FCI in weather analysis and nowcast","startDate":1771934400000,"endDate":1771938000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":"EUMETCAL","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/ui#/page/Newsitem79","description":"Applying FCI data in operational procedures, forecasters are able to follow, in near-real time, the development of storms, fog development and dissipation or track the large- and meso-scale weather systems affecting their area, with the aim to provide more precise and timely warnings about weather hazards. The webinar will give an overview of MTG FCI data and products and new findings on their utility, showcasing the applications through recent selected examples.\n\nThis webinar will be presented by Nataša Strelec Mahović and Ivan Smiljanić (EUMETSAT) on 24 February 2026 at 12:00 UTC / 13:00 CET.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/41e25b0a-9a74-46a9-a105-6d70628823ef@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2676,"created":1770818566000,"updated":1770824803000,"title":"Earth Sessions by WEkEO - Natural Disasters: Fire hotspots and smoke monitoring with Sentinel-3","titleNative":"Earth Sessions by WEkEO - Natural Disasters: Fire hotspots and smoke monitoring with Sentinel-3","startDate":1771891200000,"endDate":1772103600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":null,"contactPerson":"Noemi Marsico","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"As part of these events, the Atmospheric Composition team at EUMETSAT will host two sessions:\n\nSession 1: Fire hotspots and smoke monitoring with Sentinel-3: basic concepts\n- Date: 24 February\n- Session length: ~20 minutes \n- Session type: Theory\n\nThis first session provides a theoretical introduction to wildfire activity in Spain and Europe in 2025, setting the environmental context and explaining the role of satellite Earth Observation in detecting and monitoring fires. Participants will be introduced to Copernicus fire-related data products, with a focus on Sentinel-3 Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD) and Fire Radiative Power (FRP). This session lays the conceptual foundation for the hands-on demonstration that follows.\n\nSession 2: Fire hotspots and smoke monitoring with Sentinel-3: Tutorial\n- Date: 26 February\n- Session length: ~30 minutes \n- Session type: Jupyter Notebook demonstration\n\nThe second session is a practical, hands-on demonstration, focused on working with real wildfire data. Using a Jupyter Notebook, participants will follow a step-by-step walk-through, showing how to access, process, and visualise fire-related datasets. The session includes:\n- A live demonstration of data access using WekEO HDA through a Jupyter Notebook.\n- Practical processing and visualisation of S3 AOD and S3 FRP data through a Jupyter Notebook.\n\nParticipants will gain a clear understanding of how the WEkEO platform can be used to support wildfire analysis and monitoring.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"- Users will familiarise themselves with EUMETSAT in the general EO business.\n- Users will find out which fire-related data are available from EUMETSAT/Copernicus services.\n- Users will able to familiarise themselves with EUMETSAT Atmospheric Composition Jupyter Notebooks, and use WEkEO as a tool.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Beginners","registrationHowto":"Find more information at the [WEkEO event page](https://events.wekeo.eu/earth-sessions-natural-disasters).","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":11762,"name":"Noemi Marsico","email":"noemi.marsico@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":220,"value":"WEkEO","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":36,"title":"Fires","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2649,"created":1764590363000,"updated":1767951729000,"title":"Supporting Marine Earth Observation Applications","titleNative":"Supporting Marine Earth Observation Applications","startDate":1771804800000,"endDate":1773964800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Ben Loveday","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"In early 2026, EUMETSAT will be running the next iteration of the 'Supporting Marine Earth Observation Applications (SMA)' course. The course introduces our marine data catalogue and aims to help you develop workflows that support your own application-specific goals. If you wish to learn about, or work more extensively with our satellite-derived, ocean colour, sea surface temperature, altimetry, sea ice, or wind data, then we encourage you to apply. This year, the course will be delivered in partnership with the **Deutsches Geodätisches ForschungsInstitut (DGFI) at the Technical University of Munich**, and we particularly welcome applications from those who are interested in working with altimetry.\n\nDuring this course you will learn how to independently access and process data from the EUMETSAT product catalogue for your marine workflows and applications. The focus will be on data streams from <b>EUMETSAT Copernicus marine missions (Sentinel-3, Sentinel-6)</b> and derived downstream products, those derived from Copernicus supporting missions, and those made available by the <b>Ocean and Sea Ice Satellite Application Facility (OSI SAF)</b>.\n\nTraining will take place entirely online, in two phases, which will be supported by experts in the relevant fields:\n\n* The first, self-paced phase will take place between 23 February and 13 March 2026 and will take approximately nine-15 hours to complete. It will involve self-paced flexible learning through webinars, forums, and technical checks (particularly for software installations), documentation, and assignments.\n\n* The second, virtual classroom phase will be take place between 16 March and 20 March 2026, using GatherTown and other collaborative tools. This phase will be made up of interactive tutorials, live demos, group work, and 1:1 meetings. During this intensive week, which will require five-eight hours of participation each day, we will focus on co-creating a **mini-project** directly relevant to your application of interest.\n\nDuring the course, you will:\n* Learn about the specifics of the marine products that EUMETSAT provides (variables, timeliness, resolution, data formats).\n* Select appropriate products from the EUMETSAT marine catalogue and access them using GUI-based and automated services.\n* Use relevant software tools, such as SNAP, and scripting languages, such as Python, to acquire, visualise and process Earth observation data from multiple sources.\n* Work with trainers to design workflows around these products and tools, tailoring approaches to the specific needs of your participant-defined application.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Participants completing this course will:\n\n* Learn about the operational role EUMETSAT plays in marine Earth observation, understand the range of products it provides and appreciate how it supports downstream services.\n* Be able to select and download the appropriate data for their marine application of interest.\n* Be able to manipulate these products for their own purposes, using appropriate software packages and code-based approaches.\n* Produce a use case specific to their needs, in collaboration with other participants and trainers.","summary":"In early 2026, EUMETSAT will be running the next iteration of the 'Supporting Marine Earth Observation Applications (SMA)' course. The course introduces our marine data catalogue a","content":null,"targetAudience":"This workshop is a technical training event, designed to increase participants' abilities to access and use EUMETSAT marine products for their own applications. Training is aimed at, but not restricted to, the following:\n\n* Early career researchers (masters, PhD, PostDoc) who are new to marine remote sensing and/or the EUMETSAT marine product catalogue.\n* Marine scientists who may be working in a cross-disciplinary capacity and are looking to widen their skills into marine Earth observation.\n* Experienced professionals from any relevant sector (industry, government, environment, research) who have clear motivations for working with satellite-based ocean observation data for marine applications.","registrationHowto":"A limited number of places are available on this course. Visit the “Apply for courses” section of our course <b><a href=\"https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158\">Moodle page</a></b> for more information on how to apply.\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5877,"name":"Ben Loveday","email":"Ben.Loveday@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[{"id":3,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":6,"title":"Marine","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":20,"name":"WEkEO"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":9,"name":"Sentinel-6"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":163,"name":"SLSTR","uniqueName":"SLSTR (Sentinel-3)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":164,"name":"SRAL","uniqueName":"SRAL (Sentinel-3)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":162,"name":"OLCI","uniqueName":"OLCI (Sentinel-3)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":167,"name":"Poseidon-4","uniqueName":"Poseidon-4 (Sentinel-6)"}]},{"id":2642,"created":1763667190000,"updated":1763709878000,"title":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - February 2026 Weather and Climate Discussion","titleNative":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - February 2026 Weather and Climate Discussion","startDate":1770825600000,"endDate":1770831000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Erin Sanders","contactEmail":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"The monthly weather and climate discussions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and focus on WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). The sessions highlight the use and interpretation of satellite imagery and products for meteorological applications, using SLIDER (http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu) for imagery. Anyone with an interest is welcome to attend.\n\nGoToWebinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 16:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (15:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/578137959031748697\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":257,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":true},{"id":267,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":true},{"id":265,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":true},{"id":266,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2667,"created":1766521143000,"updated":1766559766000,"title":"Monitoring Water Quality in Lakes and Coastal Regions Using STREAM","titleNative":"Monitoring Water Quality in Lakes and Coastal Regions Using STREAM","startDate":1770735600000,"endDate":1771360200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/monitoring-water-quality-lakes-coastal-regions-using-stream","description":"Monitoring water quality is vital for managing drinking water treatment and public health and ecosystem advisories. In particular, monitoring harmful algal blooms and water transparency are crucial for assessing health and productivity of freshwater and saltwater fisheries. Conventional in situ measurements of water quality parameters are expensive and have limited spatial and temporal coverage. Remote sensing provides a cost-effective way to assess water quality in thousands of lakes and on coastal waters. \n\nNASA has developed the Satellite-based Tool for Rapid Evaluation of Aquatic Environments (STREAM) — an interactive web tool that enables high-resolution (20–30 meter) monitoring of water quality in inland lakes and coastal waters across the U.S. and selected other countries. STREAM provides both past (since 2018) and near real-time maps of chlorophyll-a concentration, Secchi disk depth, and total suspended solids based on data from the Landsat 8/9 Operational Land Imager (OLI) and the Sentinel 2 Multispectral Instrument (MSI). Additionally, an open-source machine learning model based on a Mixture Density Network (MDN) is available to estimate water quality parameters for any inland or coastal water body worldwide (minimum size: 100m x 100m). \n\nThis two-part training led by NASA's Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET) introduces the STREAM web tool to visualize maps of water quality parameters over lakes and coastal areas and how to use STREAM application programming interface (API) to discover and download satellite images and water quality data for a selected water body. The training demonstrates how to assess changes in water quality in a given lake using QGIS. The training also demonstrates the application of a MDN model to estimate water quality parameters from satellite images.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nIdentify the purpose, capabilities, and benefits of the STREAM tool for analyzing inland and coastal water bodies.\n\nIdentify the process to use STREAM to monitor chlorophyll-a concentration, Secchi disk depth, and total suspended solids in lakes and coastal waters.\n\nIdentify the steps to use STREAM API to search and download chlorophyll-a concentration, Secchi disk depth, and total suspended solids data for a specific time period.\n\nExamine time series of chlorophyll-a concentration, Secchi disk depth, and total suspended solids using QGIS.\n\nIdentify how an open-source machine-learning model based on Mixture Density Network (MDN) enables users to estimate water quality parameters for any inland or coastal water body (greater than 100m x 100m) worldwide.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Primary target audience: National and international water resources managers at local, state, and federal levels. Public and private water utilities, natural resources conservation organizations, and fisheries and aquaculture organizations.\nSecondary target audience: Academic faculty and students.","registrationHowto":"Please register here: https://go.nasa.gov/4oZSZOm","registrationEndDate":1771340400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":12,"title":"Ocean biogeochemistry","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"title":"Water quality","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":42,"name":"Landsat"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2661,"created":1765891283000,"updated":1775814235000,"title":"Short Course - Using EUMETView and Jupyter Notebooks to depict a Dust Event","titleNative":"Short Course - Using EUMETView and Jupyter Notebooks to depict a Dust Event","startDate":1770728400000,"endDate":1770733800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Noemi Marsico","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_d8ZdDBNzSfu4Cr-d_m6_vg","description":"Dust is one of the key components of atmospheric constituents, influencing air quality, climate, visibility, and human health. Its high spatial and temporal variability makes it difficult to monitor using surface-based observations alone, which is why spaceborne observations are essential to accurately detect, track, and monitor dust plumes across large areas.\n\nTo address these challenges, EUMETSAT provides a short course showing how its satellite-based tools respond to the current scientific needs for dust detection and visualisation.\n\nThe session begins with a brief introduction to dust, setting the scene by exploring its environmental relevance and highlighting the role of satellite Earth Observations in detecting, monitoring, and tracking dust transport.\n\nParticipants will then follow a guided demonstration on how to identify a dust event using EUMETView, EUMETSAT’s user-friendly visualisation service. This exercise will show how to generate a clear image or animation of the selected event.\n\nThe training continues with an overview of the available Jupyter Notebooks, which can be used to visualise and download Copernicus Sentinel-3 SLSTR Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD) and C3S (Copernicus Climate Change Service) IASI (MAPIR Algorithm) Dust Optical Depth (DOD). The notebooks will be adapted live during the session, to demonstrate how to reproduce the same event previously identified on EUMETView, showcasing a complete workflow from visual inspection to interactive data analysis.\n\nResources available here -> https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=596 ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"- Users will be familiar with EUMETSAT in the general EO industry.\n- Users will know which dust-related data are available from EUMETSAT/Copernicus services.\n- Users will independently find resources and guides related to atmospheric data, within the EUMETSAT User Portal.\n- Users will become familiar with the EUMETSAT visualisation tool EUMETView, and downloading tools such as EUMDAC, CDS and WEkEO (Jupyter Notebooks)\n- Users will become familiar with visualising dust on EUMETView and AOD and DOD using Jupyter Notebooks","summary":"Dust is one of the key components of atmospheric constituents, influencing air quality, climate, visibility, and human health. Its high spatial and temporal variability makes it di","content":null,"targetAudience":"From beginners to intermediate. This level refers to participants' experience using EUMETSAT data and tools, rather than their overall experience in the sector.","registrationHowto":"Please register at https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_d8ZdDBNzSfu4Cr-d_m6_vg.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":11762,"name":"Noemi Marsico","email":"noemi.marsico@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":101,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[{"id":3,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":20,"name":"WEkEO"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":163,"name":"SLSTR","uniqueName":"SLSTR (Sentinel-3)"}]},{"id":2664,"created":1766404738000,"updated":1767696763000,"title":"Applications in satellite meteorology","titleNative":"Applications en météorologie satellitaire","startDate":1770595200000,"endDate":1770940800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Dakar","host":"Ecole Africaine de la Météorologie et de l'Aviation Civile (EAMAC) ","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT Training","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"En association avec l'École Africaine de la Météorologie et de l'Aviation Civile (EAMAC) de l' Agence pour la Sécurité de la Navigation Aérienne en Afrique et à Madagascar (ASECNA), centre d'excellence de l'OMM, EUMETSAT organise une formation en présentiel sur les applications satellitaires à destination des prévisionnistes opérationnels d'Afrique de l'Ouest, d'Afrique centrale et de Madagascar.\n\nDispensée en français, cette formation vise à explorer les capacités de la mission Meteosat Troisième Génération (MTG). Elle mettra en évidence les avantages de MTG par rapport à MSG et montrera comment les données des instruments FCI et LI peuvent être utilisées pour améliorer la surveillance et les prévisions météorologiques. Les participants apprendront à intégrer ces données dans leurs routines opérationnelles, ce qui renforcera leurs compétences en analyse et en prévision météorologiques. Le programme comprendra également des exercices pratiques utilisant des logiciels professionnels de visualisation.\n","country":{"id":"SN","name":"SENEGAL","region":"RA-I","type":"RA-I"},"learningOutcome":"Les étudiants devraient être capables de :\n\n•\tMaîtriser l'interprétation des images satellitaires MSG et MTG (RVBs).\n•\tReconnaître la valeur ajoutée des données MTG par rapport aux données MSG.\n•\tAcquérir une expérience pratique avec les logiciels d'affichage pour les applications météorologiques.\n•\tApprendre à intégrer les données satellitaires dans le processus de prévision opérationnelle.\n","summary":"En association avec l'École Africaine de la Météorologie et de l'Aviation Civile (EAMAC) de l' Agence pour la Sécurité de la Navigation Aérienne en Afrique et à Madagascar (ASECNA)","content":null,"targetAudience":"Météorologistes opérationnels avec solides compétences en langue française.","registrationHowto":"La sélection des participants au cours par EUMETSAT et l’EAMAC se fait selon les critères suivants: la priorité sera accordée aux météorologistes opérationnels ayant suivi et validé avec succès une formation en ligne récente sur les applications satellitaires organisée par EUMETSAT et ses partenaires africains, et ayant été nommés par leur Représentant Permanent auprès de l’OMM (WMO PR).","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5848,"name":"Carla Barroso ","email":"carla.barroso@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":24,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":259,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Niger","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":4,"value":"French","iso639":"fr"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"name":"EUMETCast Africa"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":138,"name":"FCI","uniqueName":"FCI (MTG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":140,"name":"LI","uniqueName":"LI (MTG)"}]},{"id":2640,"created":1763619160000,"updated":1767858876000,"title":"Satellite Application Course - Middle East","titleNative":"Satellite Application Course - Middle East","startDate":1770508800000,"endDate":1770854400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Muscat","host":"VLab Center of Excellence-Oman ","contactPerson":"Ibrahim Al Abdulsalam ","contactEmail":"","contactUrl":"https://coemct.met.gov.om/ ","description":"This course will address the benefits of Meteosat satellites in convective weather forecasting in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region. With new MTG Meteosat-12 satellite becoming operational, emphasis will be given on utilisation of novel FCI and LI instrument data. The training will be done mostly through hands-on experiences with the real MTG data. Different use cases will be demonstrated through case scenarios (also from the MENA region).\n\nThe course will be preceded by a preparatory online course covering the fundamentals and principles of weather satellites, to be held from 19 to 22 January  2026 .","country":null,"learningOutcome":"The students should be able to:\n\n- Understand benefits of the novel MTG-I data in different application areas (focus on weather forecasting)\n- Acknowledge the added value of MTG data with respect to MSG and other available meteorological data\n- know how to visualise MTG-I data using SIFT and Jupiter Notebook visualisation tools\n- Produce their own case studies based on the available MTG-I (and auxiliary) data for their region of interest, interpret the data and validate its value\n- Learn about best practices of operational use of satellite data in other regions of MENA","summary":"This course will address the benefits of Meteosat satellites in convective weather forecasting in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region. With new MTG Meteosat-12 satellite","content":null,"targetAudience":"Operational users (eg forecasters) from the Middle East and North Africa region. Working language both English and Arabic.","registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":1764460800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5879,"name":"Ibrahim Al Abdulsalam","email":"Ibrahim.AbdulSalam@caa.gov.om","agency":"","country":"OM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":7,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"HYBRID","attendeeCount":23,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":262,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":1,"value":"Arabic","iso639":"ar"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"title":"Aerosols","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":26,"title":"Cloud types","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":36,"title":"Fires","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"name":"Metop"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":138,"name":"FCI","uniqueName":"FCI (MTG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":137,"name":"SEVIRI","uniqueName":"SEVIRI (MSG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":140,"name":"LI","uniqueName":"LI (MTG)"}]},{"id":2671,"created":1768205360000,"updated":1768205388000,"title":"Introduction to AI and ML (for weather)","titleNative":"Introduction to AI and ML (for weather)","startDate":1770120000000,"endDate":1770123600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":"EUMETCAL","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/ui#/page/Newsitem76","description":"The field of weather forecasting is moving quickly, with AI-driven methods increasingly coming in from outside meteorology. This has created a mismatch between the background of the forecaster and the methods behind the forecast. In this talk, we will give a basic introduction to Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML), with a focus on their applications in weather. The main goal is to provide insight into how these methods work and how one should reason about them, helping to bridge this gap.\n\nThis webinar will be presented by Martin Andrae and Erik Larsson (Linköping University, Sweden) on 3 February 2026 at 12:00 UTC / 13:00 CET.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary