ntactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"EUMETSAT, in partnership with the South African Weather Service’s (SAWS) Regional Training Centre (RTC), invites applications for the upcoming Satellite Applications Face-to-Face Course, dedicated to enhancing the capabilities of operational meteorologists across Africa.\n\nThis English-language training course is designed to optimize the use of data from meteorological satellites and improve the accuracy of weather forecasts. It offers a unique opportunity for African forecasters to build their expertise in satellite meteorology, with a focus on real-world applications.\n\nEligible participants who have applied will be nominated by their Permanent Representatives (PRs) with the World Meteorological Organization (WMO).\n\nWe look forward to welcoming a new group of skilled meteorologists dedicated to improving forecasting capabilities across Africa\n","country":{"id":"ZA","name":"SOUTH AFRICA","region":"RA-I","type":"RA-I"},"learningOutcome":"Participants will:\n1. Analyze and interpret satellite data including nowcasting tools to monitor and detect severe weather events.\n2. Gain proficiency in interpreting MSG and MTG RGB satellite imagery.\n3. Acquire hands-on experience with display software (s) for weather applications.\n4. Learn to integrate satellite data into operational forecasting process.\n","summary":"EUMETSAT, in partnership with the South African Weather Service’s (SAWS) Regional Training Centre (RTC), invites applications for the upcoming Satellite Applications Face-to-Face C","content":null,"targetAudience":"While preference is given to operational meteorologists within the Southern African Development Community (SADC), the course is open to all meteorologists from African countries. Individuals who have successfully completed and been certified in a recent online satellite applications course hosted by EUMETSAT and African training partners will be considered as well as candidates with strong English language skills.\n","registrationHowto":"Each WMO PR may nominate one or two candidates from the list of eligible applicants in their country, by emailing the names, emails and addresses of the proposed candidates, together with a short CV of each candidate, to training@eumetsat.int by 30 September 2025. The final selection will be conducted by EUMETSAT and SAWS and thereafter PRs will be informed of the outcomes and are encouraged to ensure nominated candidates are placed in positions where their new competencies can be applied.\n","registrationEndDate":1759190400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":454,"name":"Jannie Stander","email":"jannie.stander@weathersa.co.za","agency":" South African Weather Service","country":"ZA","programme":" ","trainingPartner":{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":21,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":258,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":27,"title":"Atmospheric phenomena","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":7,"title":"Large-scale and synoptic systems","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"title":"Mesoscale systems and convective storms","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"name":"EUMETCast Africa"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2665,"created":1766520395000,"updated":1766559707000,"title":"Advanced NASA Earth Observations and Tools for Active Fire, Smoke, and Post-Fire Monitoring","titleNative":"Observaciones Satelitales y Herramientas de la NASA para el Monitoreo de Incendios Activos, Humo y Evaluación Post-Incendio","startDate":1768420800000,"endDate":1769117400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/es/learn/trainings/observaciones-satelitales-y-herramientas-de-la-nasa-para-el-monitoreo-de-incendios","description":"Las observaciones satelitales pueden utilizarse para detectar incendios activos y también para monitorear los impactos de estos en la calidad del aire y los ecosistemas. El monitoreo de los impactos en la vegetación después de un incendio es esencial para evaluar la severidad del mismo, lo cual informa el riesgo de flujo de escombros, el entendimiento de patrones de recuperación de un ecosistema y la identificación de áreas que necesitan de intervenciones restaurativas. \n\nEl monitoreo del humo de los incendios forestales también es fundamental para las aplicaciones de calidad del aire y salud pública, ya que la exposición al material particulado fino presente en el humo puede causar problemas respiratorios y otras complicaciones de salud. Además de los incendios activos, los sensores satelitales pueden detectar anomalías térmicas estáticas (STA por sus siglas en inglés) provenientes de fuentes naturales de calor, como volcanes, o de fuentes industriales, como plantas de procesamiento de minerales, quemadores de gas y plantas de incineración de desechos.\n\nMúltiples fuentes de imágenes satelitales y datos derivados relevantes al humo, las condiciones posteriores al incendio y la identificación de STAs, están disponibles dentro del Sistema de Información de Incendios para la Gestión de Recursos (Fire Information for Resource Management System o FIRMS) de la NASA.\n\nEsta capacitación de dos sesiones presentará a los participantes varias capacidades de FIRMS que no se incluyeron en la capacitación previa de ARSET, Introducción al Monitoreo y Gestión de Incendios Forestales Mediante Observaciones Satelitales y Herramientas de la NASA. Los participantes aprenderán a utilizar los índices de aerosoles de FIRMS, los productos de área quemada, los índices e imágenes posteriores al incendio, la información sobre anomalías térmicas estáticas y a usar cuadernos de Jupyter para ingerir y visualizar datos de detección de incendios activos.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Al final de esta capacitación, los participantes podrán:\n\nAcceder a imágenes posteriores al incendio en el Modo “Burned Area” (área quemada) de FIRMS para evaluar el área quemada, la severidad de la quema y otros factores relacionados con un evento de interés.\n\nIdentificar y monitorear el humo de incendios forestales a lo largo del tiempo utilizando Reflectancia Corregida, el Índice de Aerosoles de OMPS y las capas de PyroCb (pirocumulonimbo) disponibles en el Modo de Humo/Aerosoles de FIRMS. Entender cuándo se debe usar la capa de PyroCb.\n\nUtilizar datos relevantes en FIRMS para identificar anomalías térmicas estáticas (STA) asociadas con fuentes industriales o naturales que no son incendios de vegetación.\n\nUsar cuadernos de Jupyter para acceder y visualizar datos de FIRMS en diferentes estudios de caso.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Representantes de agencias federales y estatales, gobiernos locales, organizaciones no gubernamentales (ONG) y representantes del ámbito académico que estén interesados en explorar una variedad de capacidades avanzadas disponibles en FIRMS para apoyar la identificación, el monitoreo y la gestión de incendios forestales y sus impactos.\nInstituciones académicas y organizaciones de investigación interesadas en entender los datos y herramientas de la NASA disponibles en FIRMS, así como las consideraciones para su uso en aplicaciones científicas.","registrationHowto":"español: https://go.nasa.gov/4qlec6j\n\ninglés: https://go.nasa.gov/47u7Gn2 \n\n","registrationEndDate":1769025600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],