:1761782400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Darmstadt","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Christine Traeger-Chatterjee","contactEmail":"events@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://www.eventsforce.net/eumetsat/frontend/reg/thome.csp?pageID=27549&eventID=70&CSPCHD=001001000000jzbP1pGdYrbKRn68Daryv18i8nazkQgnq4qEeZ","description":"Join us in celebrating 25 years of Climate@EUMETSAT! \n\nThe EUMETSAT Climate Symposium “From Space to Climate Service: Co-creation in Action” will be designed to strengthen the interactions between policy and decision makers, operational national and European climate service providers, the research community, and EUMETSAT to unlock the power of satellite climate data for the benefit of society employing the latest technology.\n\nThis symposium targets policy and decision makers at all administrative levels, developers of workflows using satellite climate data, providers of operational climate data and services and their users as well as experts for suitable technology for co-creation in cloud computing. Through keynote talks and discussions, the event aims to facilitate connections, networking, and collaboration between the participating communities, promoting and fostering partnerships and joint action.   \n\nThe sessions will focus on:\n- Climate Policy and Socio-Economic Benefits of Climate Data;\n- Usage of Satellite Climate Products in Applications and their Future Design;\n- Interaction between Research and Operations in the joint Development of Applications;\n- Technical Co-evolution of Data Access and Utilisation.\n- Engage in constructive discussions, share your insights and visions, and help shape the future of climate data services.\n","country":{"id":"DE","name":"GERMANY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"Join us in celebrating 25 years of Climate@EUMETSAT! \n\nThe EUMETSAT Climate Symposium “From Space to Climate Service: Co-creation in Action” will be designed to strengthen the inte","content":null,"targetAudience":"- policy and decision makers at all administrative levels\n- developers of workflows using satellite climate data\n- providers of operational climate data and services and their users\n- experts for suitable technology for co-creation in cloud computin","registrationHowto":"Find out more <a href=\"https://www.eventsforce.net/eumetsat/frontend/reg/thome.csp?pageID=27549&eventID=70&CSPCHD=001001000000jzbP1pGdYrbKRn68Daryv18i8nazkQgnq4qEeZ\"><strong>here</strong></a>.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"Yes","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5847,"name":"Christine Traeger Chatterjee","email":"christine.traeger@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":126,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2519,"created":1736852530000,"updated":1757494378000,"title":"FMI-EUMETSAT Aviation Forecaster Testbed 2","titleNative":"FMI-EUMETSAT Aviation Forecaster Testbed 2","startDate":1761555600000,"endDate":1761915600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Darmstadt","host":"FMI, EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"In partnership with the Finnish Meteorological Institute (FMI), EUMETSAT offers testbeds to prepare Aviation Forecasters for using data from EUMETSAT satellite programmes in their operations. The FMI aviation testbeds simulate an on-site operational meteorological forecasting environment, enhancing the skills of aviation forecasters in using EUMETSAT satellite data and products in aviation services. FMI will use the GEOWEB environment to run the training testbed, providing the full suite of typical products from models to observations, including in particular satellite-based products on icing potential, fog and low clouds, visibility, turbulence, volcanic ash, lightning, etc. \n\nDuring the testbeds, forecasters first receive theoretical background on the products, then produce on-the-spot forecasts and nowcasts based on selected cases, using a mix of new nowcasting and forecasting tools, followed by a verification and discussion.\n\nParticular focus will be given to the use of MTG-I1 FCI and LI data and products.\n\nThe testbeds are part of the EUMETSAT-led User Preparation projects for MTG and EPS-SG.","country":{"id":"DE","name":"GERMANY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"Enhanced skills in using EUMETSAT data and products, in particular those from MTG FCI and LI, to improve aviation weather forecasts.","summary":"In partnership with the Finnish Meteorological Institute (FMI), EUMETSAT offers testbeds to prepare Aviation Forecasters for using data from EUMETSAT satellite programmes in their ","content":null,"targetAudience":"Aviation forecasters from the EUMETSAT Member States.","registrationHowto":"Candidates need to come from EUMETSAT Member States and have a nomination by their home institution. Nominated candidates can apply at:\nhttps://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","registrationEndDate":1741391940000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5844,"name":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","email":"natasa.strelecmahovic@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":23,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"name":"Metop"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2629,"created":1761064950000,"updated":1761065017000,"title":"Spaceborne Lidar for Monitoring Vegetation Structure and Biomass using GEDI","titleNative":"Spaceborne Lidar for Monitoring Vegetation Structure and Biomass using GEDI","startDate":1761231600000,"endDate":1762462800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/spaceborne-lidar-monitoring-vegetation-structure-biomass-using-gedi","description":"This three-part training builds on a fundamental understanding of light detection and ranging (lidar), including its strengths, limitations, and distinguishing characteristics compared to other active and passive remote sensing observations. A central focus of the training is NASA’s Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI) mission, a novel spaceborne lidar sensor that began collecting data in April 2019. GEDI is the first mission specifically designed for observing the vertical structure of forests and vegetation. Through case studies and an overview of available GEDI data products, participants will explore how GEDI informs analyses of canopy structure and supports biomass estimation for forests. The training introduces the sensitivity of waveform lidar by guiding participants through plotting waveforms and deriving key vegetation structure metrics, such as canopy cover, elevation, relative height, and plant area indices. In addition, participants will learn how to work with GEDI-derived biomass estimates  in combination with data fusion products. The course covers the theoretical background of GEDI's capabilities and demonstrations on accessing, plotting, and interpreting GEDI data. The training concludes with an introduction to interpreting biomass datasets for decision support, which can be accessed through Google Earth Engine using the OBIWAN (Online Biomass Inference using Waveforms And iNventory) Application Programming Interface (API).","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\n\nIdentify the fundamental physical principles of lidar remote sensing, including the uses and limitations for terrestrial applications.\nAccess and retrieve full waveform lidar data from public NASA repositories to visualize vegetation structure.\nIdentify key GEDI products, including elevation, canopy height, vegetation structure metrics, and biomass, across different processing levels and spatial resolutions.\nAccess and visualize GEDI data related to elevation, height, vegetation structure, and biomass for specific areas of interest using openly available tools.\nUtilize GEDI standard biomass products at both footprint and 1 km scales to map forest resources.\nAccess the open-source OBIWAN API to generate estimates of biomass change in areas of interest.\nIdentify how OBIWAN can be used to create baseline scenarios for forest carbon accounting projects.\n ","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Audience\nThis training is designed for end users interested in improving their ability to monitor, manage, and understand vegetation dynamics, including impacts, and disturbances. It will be especially valuable for technical teams seeking to explore novel lidar technologies to integrate into existing workflows. It is geared toward technical audiences, including scientists, researchers, analysts, and land managers who are already using geospatial tools to support vegetation-related management and decision-support. These participants may be involved in areas such as ecosystem extent and health assessments, carbon quantification, and conservation or restoration planning.\nLand managers who use technical tools or work on fire-related issues, water quality, or water conservation management may also benefit from understanding how these technologies and tools help monitor and interpret changing vegetation dynamics relevant to their work.","registrationHowto":"Course Format\nOnline, instructor-led\nThree 2-hour sessions, including a 30-minute Q&A\nBilingual (English and Spanish)\nEnglish Session: 11:00 a.m.-1:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nhttps://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/spaceborne-lidar-monitoring-vegetation-structure-biomass-using-gedi\n\nSpanish Session: 2:00 -4:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nhttps://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/es/learn/trainings/lidar-espacial-para-el-monitoreo-de-la-estructura-y-biomasa-de-la-vegetacion-usando","registrationEndDate":1762387200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"title":"Climate","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":39,"title":"Climate system monitoring - Terrestrial","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":39,"name":"ISS"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2602,"created":1756481917000,"updated":1756481991000,"title":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - October 2025 Weather and Climate Discussion","titleNative":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - October 2025 Weather and Climate Discussion","startDate":1761145200000,"endDate":1761150600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Erin Sanders","contactEmail":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"The monthly weather and climate discussions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and focus on WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). The sessions highlight the use and interpretation of satellite imagery and products for meteorological applications, using SLIDER (http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu) for imagery. Anyone with an interest is welcome to attend.\n\nGoToWebinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 15:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/5450102567172643423","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":257,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":true},{"id":267,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":true},{"id":265,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":true},{"id":266,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2615,"created":1758789142000,"updated":null,"title":"AI Use Case: Developing Asynchronous Mentoring Training","titleNative":"AI Use Case: Developing Asynchronous Mentoring Training","startDate":1761138000000,"endDate":1761141600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":"EUMETCAL","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/ui#/page/Newsitem69","description":"We are delighted to invite you to the next webinar of the Community of Practice (CoP) for professionals using Generative AI (GenAI) in education and training.\n\nPlease forward this to anyone who you think might be interested.\n \nWhen: 22 October 13:00 UTC\nPresenter: Christina Crowe, Master Instructor, US National Weather Service Leadership Academy\nTopic: AI Use Case: Developing Asynchronous Mentoring Training\n \nWhile the National Weather Service (NWS) Leadership Academy has provided mentoring training in live sessions for years, a gap remained in terms of an asynchronous training option for all employees. When access to AI tools was granted to NWS employees in July 2025, we saw an opportunity to test its capabilities to accelerate the curriculum development process.\n\nThis presentation will explore our approach to leveraging the power of two different AI tools for content development and avatar generation. We will discuss our methodology and lessons learned from incorporating these advanced AI capabilities into the curriculum development process, including initial reactions from reviewers.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/88ba1480-f43e-4ec3-a205-5e815f91cc81@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2601,"created":1756365081000,"updated":1757494338000,"title":"Fish-PACE Virtual Hackweek for using hyperspectral color in fisheries and ocean ecosystem studies","titleNative":"Fish-PACE Virtual Hackweek for using hyperspectral color in fisheries and ocean ecosystem studies","startDate":1760918400000,"endDate":1761264000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA","contactPerson":"Ryan Vandermeulen","contactEmail":"ryan.vandermeulen@noaa.gov","contactUrl":"https://ocean-satellite-tools.github.io/hackweek-2025/","description":"We are excited to announce the \"Fish-PACE Hackweek,\" a 5-day collaborative virtual hack event scheduled for October 20-24, 2025. https://ocean-satellite-tools.github.io/hackweek-2025/\n\nThe goal of this hackweek is to a) increase familiarity and proficiency with the new hyperspectral data from NASA's PACE satellite within the fisheries and ocean ecosystem communities and b) to jointly work on pilot projects and ideas. During the hackweek, we will provide hands-on training in cloud-native tools and workflows (in Python) during the middle of the day (to accommodate multiple time zones) and hack together before (eastern time zones) and after (western time zones). We’ll conclude the hack week with presentations from each project team. Participants do not need to have expertise in programming or hyperspectral data; this is a “learn by doing” experience using projects to motivate the learning process – with the side-benefit of getting some pilot ideas off the ground.\n\nTo register for the hackweek, please fill out the following application. The event is no-cost and open to all. Participants should plan to attend all days, join a project, and participate in one of the project work times each day. Confirmations and logistics information sent to applicants on October 6th. Schedule here: https://ocean-satellite-tools.github.io/hackweek-2025/index.html?jump_to=schedule \n\nApplication Deadline: September 29, 2025","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"Find out more and apply at https://ocean-satellite-tools.github.io/hackweek-2025/. ","registrationEndDate":1759104000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5850,"name":"Hayley Evers-King","email":"Hayley.EversKing@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":106,"value":"Seminar"},"attendance":null,"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2605,"created":1756970450000,"updated":1757494248000,"title":"Ocean Hackathon 2025","titleNative":"Ocean Hackathon 2025","startDate":1760659200000,"endDate":1761346800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Various cities","host":"Campus mondial de la Mer","contactPerson":"Laura Perrin","contactEmail":"contact@campusmer.fr","contactUrl":"https://www.campusmer.fr/Hackathon-2021-4222-0-0-0.html","description":"Ocean Hackathon® is a non-stop, 48-hour event where teams develop prototypes and explore their practical applications using marine data. Several months before the event, a call for challenges is issued to gather project proposals from participating towns and cities. The event culminates in a weekend where teams compete, and the top team from each location advances to the International Grand Finale in Brest, France. Winners have the chance to receive prizes sponsored by our Ocean Hackathon® Ambassadors.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"See more information at https://www.campusmer.fr/Hackathon-2021-4222-0-0-0.html.","registrationEndDate":1761001200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5850,"name":"Hayley Evers-King","email":"Hayley.EversKing@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":null,"attendance":null,"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[{"id":3,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"language":[],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":6,"title":"Marine","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2620,"created":1759868321000,"updated":1759902386000,"title":"Introduction to GR02T: A Forecasting Tool to Detect Environments Favorable for Hail and Severe Convection","titleNative":"Introducción al GR02T: Herramienta de pronóstico para detectar ambientes favorable para granizo y convección severa","startDate":1760544000000,"endDate":1760547600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Erin Sanders","contactEmail":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"El granizo representa una amenaza significativa en latitudes medias de Sudamérica, especialmente en las cuencas del Paraná y del Río de la Plata. Las dificultades asociadas con el pronóstico de granizo en estas regiones motivaron el desarrollo de una herramienta de pronóstico especializada, diseñada para identificar ambientes favorables para la ocurrencia de granizo y convección severa. La versión inicial, creada en 2015 por José Manuel Gálvez (WPC) y Néstor Santayana (INUMET), evolucionó posteriormente hasta convertirse en \"Granizo Version 2 - Trial\" (GR02T), lanzada en 2019. El GR02T es ampliamente utilizado en Sudamérica y ha demostrado también un desempeño destacado en Estados Unidos, México, el norte de Centroamérica y el Caribe norte. Esta sesión presentará una descripción general del algoritmo GR02T, junto con orientación práctica sobre su interpretación y aplicación en eventos de tiempo severo recientes y en tiempo real, resaltando el uso de productos satelitales para la verificación.\n\nLa sesión comenzará a las 16:00 UTC y el seminario web comenzará 10 minutos antes (15:50 UTC) para aquellos que deseen revisar su micrófono.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://register.gotowebinar.com/register/927396727111968602","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":257,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":true},{"id":267,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":true},{"id":265,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":true},{"id":266,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2617,"created":1759414575000,"updated":1759414602000,"title":"Solar Induced Fluorescence (SIF) Observations for Assessing Vegetation Changes Related to Floods, Drought, and Fire Impacts","titleNative":"Solar Induced Fluorescence (SIF) Observations for Assessing Vegetation Changes Related to Floods, Drought, and Fire Impacts","startDate":1760536800000,"endDate":1761764400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/41PfPzl","description":"Solar-Induced Chlorophyll Fluorescence (SIF) provides a unique remote sensing approach for measuring photosynthetic activity, offering real-time insights into vegetation stress and productivity that surpass traditional indices (e.g. Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI)) in sensitivity and accuracy. This intermediate training builds on a previous Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET) training that introduces the SIF measurement and covers several case studies of the impact of floods and droughts on agricultural systems and the impacts of fire on forested ecosystems.\n\nParticipants will learn fundamental principles of SIF remote sensing and its practical applications for monitoring vegetation dynamics across cropland and natural systems. The course demonstrates how SIF data can track crop phenological cycles, assess agricultural drought impacts, evaluate wildfire damage and recovery patterns, and quantify relationships between SIF observations and Gross Primary Production (GPP). Participants will gain hands-on experience analyzing SIF datasets from NASA missions including the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) and Orbiting Carbon Observatory-3 (OCO-3), as well as using gap-filled data products derived using machine learning techniques.\n\nARSET también ofrece esta capacitación en español. Conozca más sobre la agenda y cómo inscribirse: https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/es/learn/trainings/observaciones-de-fluorescencia-inducida-por-el-sol-sif-para-evaluar-cambios-en-la\n\nCourse Format\nThree, 2-hour parts. Each part includes a 30-min Q&A session.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nRecognize how the Solar Induced Fluorescence (SIF) is measured and used as a complementary measurement to commonly used indices (NDVI) for land and vegetation applications.\n\nIdentify advantages and limitations of using space-based SIF measurements to monitor and evaluate vegetation health and condition.\n\nRun a given Jupyter Notebook to generate a Snapshot Area Map (SAM) plot from OCO-3 data for selected regions of interest to analyze and evaluate vegetation and land change due to fire impacts.\n\nRun a given Jupyter Notebook using GoSIF data for selected regions of interest to produce visualizations to analyze and interpret episodic land change due to droughts and floods.\n\nCompare how SIF products aggregated in space and time using open source tools are used to study vegetation change across different regions in a variety of science and applied use cases.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Researchers, analysts, and end users who are interested in learning how to use SIF to study vegetative change for agriculture, land management, pre-fire conditions, and droughts.\nStudents and academics who are interested in assessing vegetative change with satellite data and understanding SIF.","registrationHowto":"Click this link to register for the training: \n\nEnglish: https://go.nasa.gov/41PfPzl\n\nSpanish: https://go.nasa.gov/3VyuDin","registrationEndDate":1761757200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2619,"created":1759867745000,"updated":1759902345000,"title":"Introduction to GR02T: A Forecasting Tool to Detect Environments Favorable for Hail and Severe Convection","titleNative":"Introduction to GR02T: A Forecasting Tool to Detect Environments Favorable for Hail and Severe Convection","startDate":1760371200000,"endDate":1760374800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Erin Sanders","contactEmail":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"Hail poses a significant threat across the mid-latitudes of South America, particularly within the Paraná and Río de la Plata Basins. The challenges of forecasting hail in these regions led to the development of a specialized forecasting tool designed to identify environments favorable for hail and severe convection. The initial version, created in 2015 by José Manuel Gálvez (WPC) and Néstor Santayana (INUMET), has since evolved into the Granizo Version 2 – Trial Algorithm (GR02T), released in 2019. GR02T is widely used across South America and has also demonstrated strong performance in the United States, Mexico, northern Central America, and the northern Caribbean. This session will provide an overview of the GR02T algorithm and practical guidance on interpreting and applying it to recent and real-time severe weather events, placing emphasis on satellite product applications for verification.\n\nGoTo Webinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 16:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (15:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://register.gotowebinar.com/register/7412429557431743584","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":257,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":true},{"id":267,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":true},{"id":265,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":true},{"id":266,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2492,"created":1731073252000,"updated":1757494181000,"title":"ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbed on Severe Convective Storms","titleNative":"ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbed on Severe Convective Storms","startDate":1760349600000,"endDate":1760706000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Wiener Neustadt","host":"ESSL","contactPerson":"Stephan Bojinski","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"EUMETSAT, in partnership with the European Severe Storms Laboratory (ESSL), offers a series of testbeds to prepare operational meteorologists / forecasters to use data from the Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) and the EUMETSAT Polar System Second Generation (EPS-SG) Programmes.\n\nParticular focus is given to the use of novel MTG satellite data from the FCI spectral imager and from the LI lightning imager instrument. In addition, a range of state-of-the-art nowcasting tools and numerical weather prediction-based guidance will be evaluated.","country":{"id":"AT","name":"AUSTRIA","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"The testbeds teach (i) forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or experimental forecasting tools, and (ii) the evaluation of such tools. \n\nAt these events, forecasters learn by simulating an operational forecasting situation, making severe weather forecasts and nowcasts using a mix of new nowcasting and forecasting tools, and then evaluating the quality of the forecast and the value of the data and tools.","summary":"EUMETSAT, in partnership with the European Severe Storms Laboratory (ESSL), offers a series of testbeds to prepare operational meteorologists / forecasters to use data from the Met","content":null,"targetAudience":"The target group of the are operational forecasters with a strong background in severe storms forecasting.","registrationHowto":"Applications to this course are based on the nominations by the heads of delegations of EUMETSAT members. \n","registrationEndDate":1742601540000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5844,"name":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","email":"natasa.strelecmahovic@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":15,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":235,"value":"ESSL","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":29,"title":"Severe storms","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"title":"Mesoscale systems and convective storms","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":138,"name":"FCI","uniqueName":"FCI (MTG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":137,"name":"SEVIRI","uniqueName":"SEVIRI (MSG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":140,"name":"LI","uniqueName":"LI (MTG)"}]},{"id":2557,"created":1747295424000,"updated":1757494100000,"title":"Seventh Joint ECMWF/ESA/EUMETSAT Training on Atmospheric Composition","titleNative":"Seventh Joint ECMWF/ESA/EUMETSAT Training on Atmospheric Composition","startDate":1760338800000,"endDate":1760706000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Brussels","host":"BIRA-IASB","contactPerson":"Dominika Leskow-Czyzewska","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://atmostraining.info/","description":"The European Organisation for the Exploitation of Meteorological Satellites (EUMETSAT), the European Space Agency (ESA), and the European Center for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF) with the Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS) are organising the seventh joint training course on Atmospheric Composition.\n\nThe course will take place in Brussels from 13 to 17 October 2025 hosted by the Royal Belgian Institute for Space Aeronomy BIRA-IASB. Online lectures will precede the onsite training. While the course week at BIRA-IASB will be reserved to selected candidates, the online phase will be open to all interested participants.\n\nWhilst the topics of the training cover the main processes involved in atmospheric composition monitoring and modelling, the focus this year will be more on Advanced Atmospheric Composition Products & Applications with an emphasis on aerosols.\n\nThe main topics of the training include the following:\n\n- Atmospheric processes: aerosols, secondary organic aerosol production, associated chemical species, air quality.\n- Modelling: Global/regional; analysis / re-analysis / forecast; bias correction & downscaling; Data Assimilation.\n- Emissions: Inventories, inverse modelling (including aerosol precursors).\n- Observations: Spaceborne, in-situ, remote sensing retrievals, methods and analysis, exploitation.\n- Operational applications and services: Policies; applications & services.\n","country":{"id":"BE","name":"BELGIUM","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"The school will present the state-of-the-art in atmospheric composition monitoring and modelling with a focus on chemistry and climate.\n\nIt aims to provide an end-to-end overview of observations, remote sensing and modelling enhancing the capacity to access and analyse state-of-the-art datasets.\n\nThe course also aims to foster collaboration amongst participants from multiple countries and backgrounds and encourage the development of individual and team projects using the data, resources and methodologies presented.","summary":"The European Organisation for the Exploitation of Meteorological Satellites (EUMETSAT), the European Space Agency (ESA), and the European Center for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts ","content":null,"targetAudience":"The course is targeted to post-graduate level students, researchers, professionals or anyone interested in furthering their knowledge of atmospheric composition monitoring and modelling and developing their practical skills in data handling.\nSome basic background in physics, chemistry, mathematics and computing is assumed, and elementary familiarity with Python programming would be beneficial to make the most of the training.\nWhilst the onsite training is limited to around 30 participants, the online lectures preceding the onsite phase will be streamed to a wider audience.\n\nWhen selecting candidates for the on-site training, participants from Copernicus contributing countries and member states of ESA and EUMETSAT will be given preference. Students from all other countries are also welcome to apply, subject to availability of space.\nFinancial support (for accommodation in Brussels) will be available upon request for a limited number of participants. Priority will be given to candidates from Copernicus contributing countries or working on an EU project.\n\n","registrationHowto":"For more information about the event, see https://atmostraining.info/. ","registrationEndDate":1751666400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":214,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":223,"value":"ESA","visible":true},{"id":224,"value":"ECMWF","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[{"id":3,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"title":"Aerosols","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id"