t, will gather together operational meteorologists from Central Asia and beyond. The event will focus on introducing the participants with services and operations of EUMETSAT, CMA and CIMSS. \n\nThe programme will include lectures and hands-on sessions. The detailed programme is available at WMO Community platform web address.   ","country":{"id":"UZ","name":"UZBEKISTAN","region":"RA-II","type":"Middle East"},"learningOutcome":"- Skills in retrieving and working with satellite data and imagery for Central Asia. \n- Skills in working with NWC SAF nowcasting products.\n- Skills in using real-time satellite viewers. \n- Skills in satellite-based cloud, flood and fire analysis tools. ","summary":"This regional training event, hosted by Uzhydromet, will gather together operational meteorologists from Central Asia and beyond. The event will focus on introducing the participan","content":null,"targetAudience":"Operational staff of regional NMHSs.  ","registrationHowto":"The participants will be nominated by the PR with WMO of their respective countries.   ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":1,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":100,"value":"WMO","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":10,"title":"Floods","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":26,"title":"Cloud types","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":36,"title":"Fires","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2613,"created":1758637844000,"updated":1758637891000,"title":"Remote Sensing for Climate-Sensitive Infectious Diseases","titleNative":"Remote Sensing for Climate-Sensitive Infectious Diseases","startDate":1759849200000,"endDate":1760029200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/remote-sensing-climate-sensitive-infectious-diseases?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=InfectiousDisease2025","description":"Climate-sensitive vector-borne diseases such as malaria impact millions of people each year, causing more than 700,000 deaths annually, according to the World Health Organization (WHO). Satellite remote sensing data can provide valuable insights for monitoring conditions which support disease vectors. In this training, participants will learn the basic principles of how satellite remote sensing data can be applied to track climate-sensitive vector-borne disease outbreaks and provide early warnings for potential outbreaks. Participants will learn about general approaches to apply satellite remote sensing data when studying or forecasting climate-sensitive infectious diseases. These will be illustrated with a case study example in the forecasting of malaria. Participants will also become familiar with some of the common, freely available NASA remote sensing datasets used in these applications, as well as where and how to access them and how to decide which datasets are fit for their purpose.\n\nTwo 2-hour sessions\n11:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)\nEach session will include a 30-minute live Q&A session.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nIdentify environmental variables and conditions that can be observed from space which are relevant to climate-sensitive infectious disease outbreaks.\n\nIdentify the steps and tools needed to study climate-sensitive infectious disease outbreaks using remote sensing data.\n\nRecognize several remote sensing datasets commonly used to study and forecast climate sensitive infectious diseases, along with their key attributes such as resolution, coverage, latency, and uncertainty.\n\nSelect appropriate remote sensing datasets for studying climate-sensitive infectious diseases based on the disease characteristics, region of interest, and relevant environmental parameters.\n\nExamine the benefits and challenges of using remote sensing data for studying climate-sensitive infectious diseases through the case study of the EPIDEMIA system tracking and forecasting malaria in Ethiopia.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Biostatisticians, medical students, vector ecologists, and biologists studying disease vector organisms.\nPublic health officials and non-governmental organization (NGOs) tasked with monitoring and preparing for infectious disease outbreaks","registrationHowto":"Register here: \nhttps://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/learn/trainings/remote-sensing-climate-sensitive-infectious-diseases?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=InfectiousDisease2025","registrationEndDate":1760022000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":107,"value":"Webinar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"title":"Water quality","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":17,"name":"Aqua/Terra"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":46,"name":"MODIS","uniqueName":"MODIS (Aqua/Terra)"}]},{"id":2612,"created":1758578665000,"updated":1758595777000,"title":"Forecasting Tools Developed at the WPC International Desks","titleNative":"Herramientas de Pronóstico Desarrolladas en los Escritorios Internacionales del WPC","startDate":1759420800000,"endDate":1759424400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Erin Sanders","contactEmail":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"Desde 2015, cinco herramientas de pronóstico han sido desarrolladas por José Manuel Gálvez y colaboradores durante su labor en los Escritorios Internacionales del WPC de la NOAA. Estas herramientas incluyen el Índice Gálvez-Davison y Dinámica (GDID), la Herramienta de Caracterización de la Inversión Termal en ambientes de Vientos Alisios (TWIN), la Herramienta para Ambientes de Granizo y Tiempo Severo (GR02T), la Herramienta de Caracterización de Frentes y Masas de Aire (FRAM), y la Herramienta de Detección de Ondas Tropicales (TROPW). Su desarrollo fue motivado por necesidades operativas de pronóstico identificadas en las Américas y el Caribe. Cada herramienta fue diseñada específicamente para apoyar las prácticas de pronóstico regionales y, desde entonces, han sido ampliamente adoptadas en la región. En agosto de 2025, las cinco herramientas fueron actualizadas con mejoras orientadas a optimizar la visualización, simplificar y optimizar el código subyacente, y fortalecer el rigor matemático de sus algoritmos.\n\nEsta sesión especial de capacitación presentará la versión actualizada de las cinco herramientas. Discutiremos su propósito, aplicación, cómo acceder a sus productos, cómo acceder al código fuente, y las aplicaremos junto con imágenes satelitales para analizar las condiciones meteorológicas presentes durante la sesión. Cabe destacar que, debido a la gran cantidad de información para una sesión de 45 minutos, no podremos abordar cada herramienta en profundidad en esta ocasión. Sin embargo, tenemos previsto realizar sesiones especiales dedicadas a cada herramienta en los próximos meses.\n\nLa sesión comenzará a las 16:00 UTC y el seminario web comenzará 10 minutos antes (15:50 UTC) para aquellos que deseen revisar su micrófono.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2967117240129233751","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":257,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":true},{"id":267,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":true},{"id":265,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":true},{"id":266,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2609,"created":1757600146000,"updated":1758636847000,"title":"Airborne Data Applications for Invasive Species Mapping","titleNative":"Airborne Data Applications for Invasive Species Mapping","startDate":1759251600000,"endDate":1759266000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":null,"contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/4240mLR ","description":"Invasive species can cause catastrophic impacts on ecosystem health and function. Under a changing climate, urban expansion, and extreme weather events, the occurrence and impacts of invasive species is expected to increase. Remote sensing has been used as a tool to monitor the current extent and predict future spread of invasive species in aquatic and terrestrial environments. \n\nThis Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET) training highlights the different types of remote sensing data and products that can be used for terrestrial invasive species mapping and monitoring. Participants will be introduced to airborne and in situ field data applications for invasive species mapping utilizing datasets acquired during the Biodiversity Survey of the Cape (BioSCape) campaign in South Africa. A demonstration of invasive species mapping using a managed Jupyterhub Python environment will provide the opportunity to apply a cloud based workflow to train airborne data from the Airborne Visible-Infrared Imaging Spectrometer Next-Generation (AVIRIS-NG) and utilize in situ field data. Participants will develop a site specific scene demonstrative of using in situ field data and imaging spectroscopy to map and visualize data, providing insights into the analysis of estimating invasive species using supervised image classification. \n\nUpon the training's conclusion, participants will leave with a better understanding of the applications and limitations of imaging spectroscopy and airborne data products. Participants will also develop skills to apply their knowledge of the data to areas of interest to inform land management and planning decisions.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\nRecognize how image classification of airborne imaging spectroscopy and labeled field data can be used to map invasive species.\n\nUse a Jupyter Notebook to access NASA JPL AVIRIS-NG data located on the NASA Earthdata cloud for a spatial and temporal region of interest.\n\nApply a cloud based workflow in a provided Jupyter Notebook to classify species using a trained machine learning model for the Greater Cape Floristic Region, South Africa.\n\nIdentify key considerations for interpreting machine learning end products for invasive species mapping for land management decisions.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Resource managers (local, state, regional, international), agriculture sector, remote sensing technicians, ecologists, and academics.","registrationHowto":"Times: 11:00-13:00 or 15:00-17:00 EDT [UTC-4]\nRegister: https://go.nasa.gov/4240mLR \n\nCourse Format\nOne 2-hour part","registrationEndDate":1759251600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2611,"created":1758578290000,"updated":1758595763000,"title":"Forecasting Tools Developed at the WPC International Desks","titleNative":"Forecasting Tools Developed at the WPC International Desks","startDate":1759251600000,"endDate":1759255200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Erin Sanders","contactEmail":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"Since 2015, five forecasting tools have been developed by José Manuel Gálvez and collaborators during his tenure at NOAA's Weather Prediction Center (WPC) International Desks.  These tools include the Gálvez-Davison Index and Dynamics Tool (GDID), the Trade Wind Inversion Characterization Tool (TWIN), the Hail and Severe Weather Environments Tool (GR02T), the Front and Airmass Characterization Tool (FRAM), and the Tropical Wave Detection Tool (TROPW).  Their development was driven by operational forecasting needs identified throughout the Americas and the Caribbean.  Each tool was specifically designed to support regional forecasting practices and has since seen widespread adoption.  In August 2025, all five tools underwent enhancements aimed at improving visualization, optimizing and simplifying the underlying code, and strengthening the mathematical rigor of their algorithms.\n \nThis special training session will present the updated version of the five tools.  We will discuss their purpose, application, how to access their output, how to access the code, and we will apply them together with satellite imagery to understand the weather present during the session.  Note that given the large amount of information for a 45-minute session, we will not be able to dive with too much detail into each tool at this time.  Yet, we plan to do so in special sessions tailored for each tool in the coming months.\n\nGoTo Webinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 17:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (16:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/1865054745376551773","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"