- Middle East","startDate":1757808000000,"endDate":1758196800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Salalah","host":"CAA","contactPerson":"Ivan Smiljanic","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"This course will strongly address the benefits of Meteosat satellites in convective weather forecasting in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region. With new MTG Meteosat-12 satellite becoming operational, emphasis will be given on utilisation of novel FCI and LI instrument data. The training will be done mostly through hands-on experiences with the real MTG data. Different use cases will be demonstrated through case scenarios (also from the MENA region).","country":{"id":"OM","name":"OMAN","region":"RA-II","type":"Middle East"},"learningOutcome":"The students should be able to:\n- understand benefits of the novel MTG-I data in different application areas (focus on weather forecasting)\n - acknowledge the added value of MTG data with respect to MSG and other available meteorological data\n- know how to visualise MTG-I data using SIFT and Jupiter Notebook visualisation tools\n - produce their own case studies based on the available MTG-I (and auxiliary) data for their region of interest, interpret the data and validate its value\n- learn about best practices of operational use of satellite data in other regions of MENA\n\n","summary":"This course will strongly address the benefits of Meteosat satellites in convective weather forecasting in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region. With new MTG Meteosat-12 ","content":null,"targetAudience":"Operational users (eg forecasters) from the Middle East region. Working language both English and Arabic. ","registrationHowto":"Applications are now closed. ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5845,"name":"Ivan Smiljanic","email":"ivan.smiljanic@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":null,"attendeeCount":17,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":262,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":1,"value":"Arabic","iso639":"ar"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"title":"Mesoscale systems and convective storms","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":138,"name":"FCI","uniqueName":"FCI (MTG)"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":140,"name":"LI","uniqueName":"LI (MTG)"}]},{"id":2600,"created":1756153912000,"updated":1756192281000,"title":"Assessing Extreme Weather Statistics using NASA Earth eXchange Global Daily Downscaled Projections (NEX-GDDP-CMIP6)","titleNative":"Assessing Extreme Weather Statistics using NASA Earth eXchange Global Daily Downscaled Projections (NEX-GDDP-CMIP6)","startDate":1757509200000,"endDate":1760734800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3GViIaK","description":"According to the World Meteorological Organization, the frequency and intensity of extreme weather events, including heat and cold waves, heavy precipitation and droughts, and wildfires, have increased fivefold in the last 50 years. While current Earth observations from NASA allow monitoring of these extreme events for developing regional adaptation and mitigation strategies, it is crucial to assess how these events will unfold over the coming decades.\n\nThe typical spatial resolution of current Global Climate Models (GCMs) from various international institutions is larger than 1x1 degree latitude-longitude. For regional applications, higher spatial resolution GCM data is required. NASA Earth eXchange Global Daily Downscaled Projections (NEX-GDDP-CMIP6) provide global, high resolution, bias-corrected projections of daily minimum and maximum temperatures, precipitation, humidity, windspeed, and surface radiation from the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 (CMIP6) GCMs at 0.25x0.25 degree resolution. \n\nThis two-part training, offered by NASA's Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET), focuses on evaluating projected statistics of extreme heat and cold wave events, and extreme precipitation excess and deficit events, from NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 at regional scale during the 21st century to assess regional risk.\n\nCourse Format\nTwo 120-minute parts, each including a 30-minute Q&A session.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to: \n\nIdentify available daily, high-resolution geophysical parameters from NASA Earth eXchange – Global Daily Downscaled Projections – Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 (NEX-GDDP-CMIP6) to assess extreme weather statistics at a regional scale.\n\nAccess daily temperature and precipitation data for a region of interest to calculate and analyze weather statistics using NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 data in Google Earth Engine.\n\nVisualize maps of extreme weather statistics for a region of interest using NEX-GDDP Dashboard.\n\nCustomize provided Python/Jupyter notebook scripts to calculate extreme weather statistics for a region of interest.\n\nAssess projected statistics of extreme heat and cold wave events, and excess and deficit precipitation events from NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 at regional scale during the 21st century to evaluate risk for a region of interest.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Primary Target Audience: Personnel from local, state, regional, and federal government and non-governmental organizations involved in managing disasters and planning for adaptation and mitigation.\nSecondary target audience: Academic faculty and students","registrationHowto":"Time: 11:00-13:00 or 15:00-17:00 EDT [UTC-4] (two session times are available for each part)\n\nRegister Today: https://go.nasa.gov/3GViIaK\n\n","registrationEndDate":1758114000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":nul