":1752054028000,"updated":1761835520000,"title":"African Weather Briefing ","titleNative":"African Weather Briefing ","startDate":1753948800000,"endDate":1753952400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":null,"contactPerson":"South African Weather Service Regional Training Centre","contactEmail":"met.training@weathersa.co.za","contactUrl":"http://training.tools.eumetsat.int/videos/AfricaWeatherBriefing/Weather_Briefing_31Jul2025_SAWS.mp4","description":"The July online weather briefing was hosted by the South African Weather Service and looked at identification of potential turbulence areas using various satellite products.\n\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"https://training.tools.eumetsat.int/videos/AfricaWeatherBriefing/Tornardgenesis(PS-update).pptx","summary":"The July online weather briefing was hosted by the South African Weather Service and looked at identification of potential turbulence areas using various satellite products.\n\n","content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":454,"name":"Jannie Stander","email":"jannie.stander@weathersa.co.za","agency":" South African Weather Service","country":"ZA","programme":" ","trainingPartner":{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":12,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":258,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2579,"created":1751553685000,"updated":1751554125000,"title":"Introduction to NASA Snow and Ice Data Products and Applications for Water Resources Management","titleNative":"Introduction to NASA Snow and Ice Data Products and Applications for Water Resources Management","startDate":1753369200000,"endDate":1754598600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA ARSET","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3Sf2cEz","description":"Frozen water on or below the earth’s surface, known as the cryosphere, is a vital component of the Earth system and includes snow, glaciers, ice sheets, sea ice, and permafrost. Approximately 75% of the Earth's total freshwater is stored in glaciers, ice sheets, and permafrost (USGS). In many regions, snowmelt also serves as a major source of freshwater. Snow and ice play a crucial role by influencing albedo (the fraction of sunlight reflected back into space) which affects the Earth’s energy balance and temperature. Snow cover also significantly influences weather patterns, ecosystems, transportation, recreational activities, and infrastructure (NSIDC). Mountain snowpack can lead to avalanches, and the timing and volume of melt can trigger flooding in snow-fed rivers. Conversely, reduced snowpack can result in snow droughts, impacting water availability.\n\nNASA provides regional and global snow and ice data products based on remote sensing observations. Additionally, NASA Earth system models can diagnose snowfall during winter and estimate snow water equivalent—a key parameter for assessing freshwater resources. This collaborative training between ARSET, NASA’s National Snow and Ice Data Center, and NASA's Western Water Actions Office (WWAO), introduces NASA’s snow and ice data products and snow water equivalence data, with a particular focus on snow data application to monitoring and forecasting snow-fed river flow and water availability.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: \n\n1.\tRecognize the importance and applications of snow data and Snow Water Equivalent (SWE) in monitoring water availability in snow-fed rivers.\n2.\tIdentify NASA snow and ice data products and their applications for water resource management and disaster monitoring.\n3.\tAccess and visualize snow and ice data products using NASA webtools for water supply prediction.\n4.\tCompare how snow data across platforms are utilized in water resources management and monitoring snow-fed river flow.\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"•\tPrimary Target Audience: Regional, state, and federal water resources managers, river network and disasters managers, fish and wildlife managers\n•\tSecondary Target Audience: Academic students and faculty\n","registrationHowto":"Course Dates: July 24, 31 & August 7, 2025\nCourse Format: Three 1.5-hour parts including Q&A.\n \nTo Register: https://go.nasa.gov/3Sf2cEz\n","registrationEndDate":1754578800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":n