732024800000,"endDate":1732224600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training (ARSET) Program","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/get-involved/training/english/arset-methane-observations-large-emission-event-detection-and?utm_source=social&utm_medium=ext&utm_campaign=Methane_2024","description":"While carbon dioxide (CO2) is the primary greenhouse gas emitted through human activities, methane is estimated around 80 times more effective at trapping heat in the atmosphere on a 20 year timescale.\n\nMethane is an attractive target for mitigation activities because it has a  relatively short decadal-scale lifetime in the  atmosphere (as compared with the century-scale carbon dioxide lifetime), it can be utilized as an energy source or combusted (converted to CO2) in order to reduce its global warming potential, and the safety issues associated with high concentrations of this flammable gas. Many activities can lead to the release of large concentrations of methane during the course of normal industrial operations or as accidental releases, these are often referred to as super emitter events, which can be identified from modern satellites.  \n\nThis introductory two-part training will begin with an introduction to the U.S. Greenhouse Gas Center and provide participants with an overview of how methane observations from the Earth surface Mineral dust Source Investigation (EMIT) mission can be used to identify and monitor areas of high methane enhancement. This course will also demonstrate how to navigate the U.S. Greenhouse Gas center portal to access data products as well as highlight tools for visualizing methane observations.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: \n1.\tIdentify the goals and objectives of  the U.S. Greenhouse Gas Center\n2.\tDefine the roles of methane and large emission events in climate change\n3.\tIdentify the sensors used to measure methane\n4.\tRecognize the strengths and limitations of satellite observations used to measure methane for large emission event tracking\n5.\tNavigate the U.S. Greenhouse Gas Center Portal and the EMIT Open Data Portal to access and visualize data for large emission event tracking\n\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for Federal, state and local government agency representatives, United Nations agencies with policy mandates related to GHG emission measurement, monitoring and mitigation (e.g., UNEP), entities whose operations produce methane (i.e. oil and gas sector, landfills, livestock, etc.), as well as non-public sector entities with roles/responsibilities in providing GHG data, solutions and services.","registrationHowto":"To Register: \nEnglish: https://go.nasa.gov/4daJYfe \nSpanish: https://go.nasa.gov/4escv11 \n\nCourse Format: Two 1.5-hour parts including Q&A.","registrationEndDate":1732215600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"title":"Climate","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":24,"title":"Greenhouse gases and ozone","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":37,"title":"Climate system monitoring - Atmosphere","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2468,"created":1726060373000,"updated":1727702869000,"title":"NWP SAF Workshop on Satellite Observations of the Earth System Interfaces","titleNative":"NWP SAF Workshop on Satellite Observations of the Earth System Interfaces","startDate":1731974400000,"endDate":1732233600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Reading","host":null,"contactPerson":null,"contactEmail":"events@ecmwf.int","contactUrl":" https://events.ecmwf.int/event/420","description":"Interface observations refer to observations that are sensitive to multiple Earth system components, including atmosphere, land, sea ice, snow, and the ocean. They are key for Numerical Weather Prediction (NWP) and reanalysis. In a coupled data assimilation framework, used in Earth system approaches, satellite-sensed interface observations have the unique potential to simultaneously constrain the various Earth system components, providing consistent initial and boundary conditions to Earth system prediction models.\n\nThe goals of this workshop are to: \n\n- Provide an overview of the various interface observations which are available to the NWP community, as well as of the forward models which are being developed or are already in use to exploit this type of observations;\n- Assess the state-of-the-art on the use of interface observations both in retrievals and in coupled data assimilation systems;\n- Understand the direction in which the scientific community is taking this important and complex problem.\n","country":{"id":"GB","name":"UNITED KINGDOM","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"Interface observations refer to observations that are sensitive to multiple Earth system components, including atmosphere, land, sea ice, snow, and the ocean. They are key for Nume","content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"Please note that this is an in-person event and is by invitation only. A poster session will be organised to allow participants to share their results. Oral presentations are by invitation only.\n\nIf you have been invited and would like to attend, please submit the registration form by 1 October 2024.\n\nIf you have not been invited, but would like to attend, please contact events@ecmwf.int.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":null,"awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9959,"name":"Madalina System Admin Ungur ","email":"madalina.ungur@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":228,"value":"NWP SAF","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":221,"value":"ECMWF","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2493,"created":1731404022000,"updated":null,"title":"ECMWF Online computing training week 2024","titleNative":"ECMWF Online computing training week 2024","startDate":1731888000000,"endDate":1732233600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"ECMWF","contactPerson":"Becky Hemingway","contactEmail":"becky.hemingway@ecmwf.int","contactUrl":"https://events.ecmwf.int/event/444/","description":"ECMWF are happy to annouce that we will be running Online computing training week 2024 between 18 and 22 November 2024. The week features six, hour-long webinars covering a variety of topics related to ECMWF products and services:\n- Transition to the new Copernicus Data Store (CDS) and Atmospheric Data Store (ADS)\n- EarthKit\n- JupyterHub on Atos\n- Working with BUFR data: ecCodes Python API\n- Migration of ECMWF Forecast Data to GRIB2 format\n- European Weather Cloud\n\nRegistration is available for each webinar on this page: https://events.ecmwf.int/event/444/registrations/","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"https://events.ecmwf.int/event/444/registrations/","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":106,"value":"Seminar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2486,"created":1730284305000,"updated":1752481244000,"title":"EWC - 7th European Weather Cloud User Workshop","titleNative":"EWC - 7th European Weather Cloud User Workshop","startDate":1731488400000,"endDate":1731506400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"We are excited to invite our users to the 7th European Weather Cloud (EWC) User Workshop, a virtual event that brings together the EWC user community to discuss the latest developments, use cases, and future possibilities of the European Weather Cloud. This workshop, jointly organised by ECMWF and EUMETSAT, provides a great opportunity for users to engage directly with the teams behind the EWC and with peers who are leveraging this platform for various applications in meteorology, climate, and environmental science.\n\nAdditionally, this workshop will be mainly dedicated to showcasing what EWC users are accomplishing on the platform. Users from diverse backgrounds—ranging from fisheries and environmental monitoring to weather prediction and AI-driven modeling—will share their experiences, challenges, and innovative solutions developed using EWC. This segment highlights the real-world applications of the cloud.\n\nDownload the full agenda here: https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=547.\n\nThe workshop is open to participants from ECMWF and EUMETSAT Member and Co-operating States. \n\nIf you’re interested in presenting your own use of EWC during the next workshops, please get in touch with us through our support platform: http://support.europeanweather.cloud.\n\nFind the recordings here: https://classroom.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=547","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Stay Informed: Users will get the latest updates on EWC, including new features, capabilities, and plans for the future.\n\nLearn from Real-World Use Cases: users will discover how other users are making the most of the EWC for their unique applications and gain inspiration for your own projects.\n\nEngage with the Community: Users will have the chance to connect with other EWC users, share insights, and find potential collaborators.\n\nProvide Feedback: Users will be able to share their thoughts with the EWC team to help shape future improvements.","summary":"We are excited to invite our users to the 7th European Weather Cloud (EWC) User Workshop, a virtual event that brings together the EWC user community to discuss the latest developm","content":null,"targetAudience":"Participants from ECMWF and EUMETSAT Member and Co-operating States. ","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/meeting/register/tZIud-CsqDkiGNK9zHFl_KD3NFjR46eiPRC9.","registrationEndDate":1731430800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email":"deleted.trainer@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"--","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":224,"value":"ECMWF","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"name":"European Weather Cloud"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2472,"created":1726227723000,"updated":1726227768000,"title":"Meteorological Simulator for Training","titleNative":"Meteorological Simulator for Training","startDate":1731412800000,"endDate":1731416400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":"EUMETCAL","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/972f0d94-817c-4b0f-a3cd-e1edd1a800e8@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","description":"Meteorological simulators have been used for quite many years in training events. The simulators present a saved weather situation, that the meteorologist investigates by using the given meteorological data, preparing forecasts while the time goes on and more data comes in.\n\nThe objective of these simulator exercises is to give an opportunity for meteorologists to work with a real data for a real case, and to prepare forecasts and verify them. By doing this, they will have a more engaging learning experience than just by viewing a case study presentation.\n\nFor the meteorological trainers and assessors, the simulators offer a way to assess the participants' competencies, and to help the participants practice in a safe environment the correct procedures during high impact weather events. An important part of simulation is to offer an authentic working environment, where the data flows in as it does in an operational environment. To build such an authentic working environment may be tedious. It takes time to retrieve the meteorological data for the case, to build the user interface and to organize the data in the interface.\n\nEUMETSAT has recently developed a simple meteorological simulator that runs on European Weather Cloud (EWC), allowing the simulator to be available for anyone willing to build such exercises. During the webinar, we will demonstrate our simulator at the workshop and exchange experiences with webinar attendees.\n\nIt will be presented by Natasa Strelec Mahovic and Vesa Nietosvaara (EUMETSAT).","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"https://events.teams.microsoft.com/event/972f0d94-817c-4b0f-a3cd-e1edd1a800e8@396b9e20-59c1-4cf1-bc03-d43fd5759427","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2481,"created":1728551597000,"updated":1752481244000,"title":"From Satellite Data to Images in Minutes Using Our Web Mapping Application, EUMETView","titleNative":"From Satellite Data to Images in Minutes Using Our Web Mapping Application, EUMETView","startDate":1731409200000,"endDate":1733184000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"DAS Training Team","contactEmail":"das-training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"The EUMETSAT Data Access Team invites you to participate in our 4-week course series designed to take users from the basics to advanced features of our Pull Data Access Service, EUMETView. These short, focused sessions will guide you step-by-step, ensuring you gain practical experience with the tools and services available.\n\nAll course participants will be provided access to the course resources, including presentations, documentation, and additional materials, via a dedicated section on the EUMETSAT Classroom platform. You will also be able to interact with the instructors and fellow participants through this portal.\n\n\n\nSESSION 1: Basics of EUMETView and GUI Interaction\n\nIn this session, participants will learn the fundamentals of EUMETView, including how to locate relevant resources. We will cover the graphical user interface (GUI) and guide you through customizing the basemap, adding layers, creating images and animations, and saving or sharing your outputs with colleagues.\n\nRegister for Session 1 here → https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_V4wo7QzbQlacimQgsb85Uw\n\n\n\nSESSION 2: Integrating EUMETView with QGIS\n\nIdeal for users with GIS skills, this session will demonstrate how to integrate EUMETView data into QGIS. We’ll show you how to upload EUMETSAT data layers into your QGIS maps, simplifying your workflows and enhancing your analysis.\n\nRegister for Session 2 → https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_XFQHpgLeRBOkwFzA0T_sLg\n\n\n\nSESSION 3: Advanced EUMETView - Using APIs\n\nThis session will focus on interacting with EUMETView’s APIs via Jupyter Notebooks. You will learn how to leverage various services provided by EUMETView, allowing you to access and download specific data collections and products based on your needs. \n\nRegister for Session 3 here → https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_yEmKqySYTTOaDy1g8nwdTQ\n\n\n\nSESSION 4: Showcasing EUMETView Use Cases\n\nIn the final session, we will invite participants to showcase their work using EUMETView. We will begin with a brief introduction, followed by user presentations that highlight successful applications of the service in real-world projects.\n\nRegister for Session 4 here → https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_hAMhtQ62RuiKolXh3slL9g","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Users should gain a comprehensive understanding of EUMETSAT's key data access services.\nUsers should be able to find the correct documentation and resources for ongoing learning and reference beyond the course sessions.\nUsers should learn how to use the GUI of EUMETView and related services: WCS, WFS, WMS.\nUsers should understand the conceptual correlation between the EUMETView services and their API.\nUsers should be equipped with the ability to independently download EUMETSAT data through EV GUI and Jupyter Notebooks.","summary":"The EUMETSAT Data Access Team invites you to participate in our 4-week course series designed to take users from the basics to advanced features of our Pull Data Access Service, EU","content":null,"targetAudience":"Everyone, from basic to advanced","registrationHowto":"Session 1: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_V4wo7QzbQlacimQgsb85Uw\n\nSession 2: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_XFQHpgLeRBOkwFzA0T_sLg\n\nSession 3: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_yEmKqySYTTOaDy1g8nwdTQ\n\nSession 4: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_hAMhtQ62RuiKolXh3slL9g","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email":"deleted.trainer@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"--","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5841,"name":"Regina Hoefenmayer","email":"regina.hoefenmayer@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":208,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":21,"value":"Not Applicable"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2478,"created":1727704248000,"updated":1727704283000,"title":"An Introduction to Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) and Its Applications","titleNative":"An Introduction to Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) and Its Applications","startDate":1730903400000,"endDate":1732138200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training (ARSET) ","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/get-involved/training/english/arset-introduction-synthetic-aperture-radar-sar-and-its-applications?utm_source=social&utm_medium=ext&utm_campaign=SAR2024","description":"Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) has the capability to observe the Earth's surface both day and night and through most weather conditions, making it an ideal sensor to support a wide range of science and applications. Additionally, unique capabilities of SAR include its ability to penetrate through various mediums (such as vegetation, snow, and soil), measure surface deformation and small movements on the order of centimeters, and its sensitivity to structure and to moisture content. These capabilities are valuable for monitoring vegetation structure and land use, detecting surface deformation and movements in land (e.g., earthquakes, landslides) and ice (e.g., glacier movement), flooding, and soil moisture, to name a few.\n\nHistorical and operational SAR data have been used to support numerous science and applications questions and needs. However, upcoming SAR satellite sensors, such as NISAR and BIOMASS, will enhance these capabilities with their unique observing frequencies and spatial and temporal resolutions.\n\nThis online webinar will provide an introduction to SAR, including interferometric SAR (InSAR), as well as a review of the characteristics of historical, current and upcoming openly available SAR satellite data. It will also explore the type of applications that each sensor can best address. Additionally, this webinar will discuss online sources of openly available SAR data, along with tools, software, and other resources to understand, explore, and facilitate the analysis of SAR data.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: \n1.\tRecognize basic features and functionality of Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR)\n2.\tEvaluate SAR sensor characteristics for addressing different science questions and application areas\n3.\tInterpret the information content in SAR images to distinguish different features (e.g., vegetation, water, inundation) detected by the sensor\n4.\tEvaluate creation of an interferogram through interferometric SAR (InSAR)\n5.\tInterpret an interferogram to measure surface deformation and small movements \n6.\tCompare and contrast the capabilities of historic, current, and upcoming SAR data \n7.\tAccess and visualize SAR data for a given location and time\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for Remote sensing end users, geospatial analysts, academics and students, managers from local, state, federal government, and NGO’s.","registrationHowto":"To Register: \nEnglish: https://go.nasa.gov/47BRLS1 \nSpanish: https://go.nasa.gov/4ddMlxS\n\nCourse Format: Three 2-hour parts including Q&A.","registrationEndDate":1732131000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":10,"title":"Floods","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":56,"name":"Sentinel-1"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2414,"created":1715929295000,"updated":1721817265000,"title":"Marine User Days","titleNative":"Marine User Days","startDate":1730790000000,"endDate":1730851200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Lisbon","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":null,"contactEmail":"UserPreparation@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://www.eumetsat.int/marine-user-days","description":"The Marine User Days will take place on 5-6 November 2024 in Lisbon, Portugal. \n\nThis Marine User Days event is hosted by EUMETSAT. Partners include the European Commission, European Space Agency, the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts, Mercator Ocean International and the Copernicus Marine Service.\n\nThe Marine User Days will bring together existing and prospective users from across the diverse marine user community. Focusing on the data provided by the European organisations involved in the Copernicus programme and their role in oceanography and marine applications, the event will act as a showcase for use cases. It is also a networking opportunity for users across the value chain, and will feature a workshop to engage with user needs and feedback.\n\nEmphasis will be placed on data from across the value chain including satellite data, derived products, climate data records and their applications. Copernicus contributing mission data from EUMETSAT’s meteorological missions and data from the satellite application facility specialising in ocean and sea ice will broaden the scope.\n\nThe Marine User Days will be open to participation from across the community including those involved at the policy and governance level, academia and research and commercial entities.  Attendees will benefit from the opportunity to present their use cases to the wider community of users and potential customers, but also to the European Commission and Copernicus entrusted entity representatives. They will have to opportunity to discuss their use cases and needs with the latter.\n\nThe workshop is open for any existing or prospective users who wish to attend.\n\nRegistration is now open, with a deadline on 10 October.","country":{"id":"PT","name":"PORTUGAL","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"The Marine User Days will take place on 5-6 November 2024 in Lisbon, Portugal. \n\nThis Marine User Days event is hosted by EUMETSAT. Partners include the European Commission, Europe","content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"Register at https://www.eventsforce.net/eumetsat/frontend/reg/thome.csp?pageID=23972&eventID=60.","registrationEndDate":1729116000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5850,"name":"Hayley Evers-King","email":"Hayley.EversKing@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":1130,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":6,"title":"Marine","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":9,"name":"Sentinel-6"},{"eventMetada