000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"Bernie.Connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"These monthly weather and climate discussions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and focus on WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). The sessions highlight the use and interpretation of satellite imagery and products for meteorological applications, using SLIDER (http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu) for imagery. Anyone with an interest is welcome to attend.\n\nGoToWebinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 15:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/755855310379698006","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2461,"created":1722363404000,"updated":1722366222000,"title":"Invasive Species Monitoring with Remote Sensing","titleNative":"Invasive Species Monitoring with Remote Sensing","startDate":1723644000000,"endDate":1724859000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/get-involved/training/english/arset-invasive-species-monitoring-remote-sensing","description":"Invasive species costs the U.S. economy billions of dollars a year and can cause declines in ecosystem health. NASA data can be used to identify the impacts of invasive species including the extent, potential distribution, and impacts to affected ecosystems. With improvements to the temporal, spatial, and spectral resolution of data alongside cloud-based computing there are new opportunities to apply NASA data, products, and tools to landscape management.\n\nThis course will provide participants with an overview of typical NASA satellites and sensors used to map invasive plants such as Landsat, MODIS, and VIIRS, as well as innovative or upcoming data and missions such as the Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI), HyMap, the Surface Biology and Geology (SBG) mission, and the Geosynchronous Littoral Imaging and Monitoring Radiometer (GLIMR). This training will highlight project-based applications of remote sensing for plant species of interest especially those affecting grasslands and aquatic inland lakes and waterbodies, and more with a lens towards innovative uses of hyperspectral data for additional invasive species detection.\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \n•\tGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts\n•\tGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\n•\tGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n•\tRecognize the extent and impacts of invasive species on biodiversity and a changing climate.\n•\tIdentify the types of remote sensing data and products that can be used for invasive species mapping and monitoring.\n•\tExplore key considerations, benefits and limitations of remote sensing data sets for  invasive species.\n•\tIdentify where to access remote sensing data for monitoring invasive species and mapping relevant habitat and climate variables.\n•\tEvaluate remote sensing methods used to monitor aquatic and grassland invasive plant species.\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Primary target audience: Natural resource managers (local, state, regional, international)\nSecondary target audience: technicians \nOther potential participants: academics\n","registrationHowto":"Register Here: https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/get-involved/training/english/arset-invasive-species-monitoring-remote-sensing\n\nCourse Format: Three-part webinar series, 90 minutes each (60-minute presentation, 30-minute Q&A)","registrationEndDate":1724853600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":38,"title":"Climate system monitoring - Ocean","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":39,"title":"Climate system monitoring - Terrestrial","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2436,"created":1719484571000,"updated":1752481244000,"title":"Training course on EUMETSAT Data Access Services for Users in Africa","titleNative":"Training course on EUMETSAT Data Access Services for Users in Africa","startDate":1723539600000,"endDate":1725361200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"online","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"DAS Training Team","contactEmail":"das-training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"This is a course designed specifically for African users affiliated with the WMO. The training will consist of four sessions, each focusing on various aspects of the EUMETSAT Data Access Services. The goal of each session is to provide participants with in-depth knowledge of the specific services utilized and their potential interactions. Participants will receive comprehensive support materials, including guides, exercises, and recordings, ensuring ongoing access to resources even after the course concludes.\n\nSession 1: Data Store and Data Tailor through Graphical user interface\n\nSession 2: Data Store and Data Tailor through EUMDAC Command Line Interface\n\nSession 3: EUMETView\n\nSession 4: Questions and answers\n\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"- Users should gain a comprehensive understanding of EUMETSAT's key data access services.\n- Users should be able to find the correct documentation and resources for ongoing learning and reference beyond the course sessions.\n- Users should learn how to use the GUI of the main service Data Store and the customization tool, Data Tailor.\n- Users should understand the conceptual correlation between the DA services and their API\n- Users should acquire proficiency in executing basic Command Line Interface (CLI) commands in EUMDAC\n- Users should understand where and how to find the Jupyter Notebooks if needed.\n- Users should be equipped with the ability to independently download EUMETSAT data through either GUIs or APIs","summary":"This is a course designed specifically for African users affiliated with the WMO. The training will consist of four sessions, each focusing on various aspects of the EUMETSAT Data ","content":null,"targetAudience":"African users","registrationHowto":"Register to session 1: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_4j6XOriDTze8BgbA1ic1nw\n(If your registration is approved you will get access to a EUMETSAT Classroom page where you will be able to access the registration links to the next sessions and the EUMETSAT Data Access Services general resources)\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email":"deleted.trainer@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"--","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":100,"value":"WMO","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":8,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Kenya","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"name":"EUMETView"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2459,"created":1721667138000,"updated":1721817700000,"title":"NASA Atmospheric Composition Ground Networks Supporting Air Quality and Climate Applications","titleNative":"NASA Atmospheric Composition Ground Networks Supporting Air Quality and Climate Applications","startDate":1723129200000,"endDate":1724344200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA ","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3XoE4mv","description":"In this five-part, online, intermediate-level training, multiple NASA networks will participate and provide training on their atmospheric composition ground networks. These networks provide data relevant to aerosol and trace gas column concentrations and vertical structure, supporting applications in air quality and climate. Surface networks from NASA and other international contributions form the backbone of long-term atmospheric composition observations globally and provide continuity and validation across typically shorter satellite mission lifetimes. This online training will provide a basic understanding of aerosol and trace gas measurements using both active and passive techniques for ground based remote sensing. It will also teach users how to select, access, and incorporate relevant data from these ground networks into their work. Finally, it will demonstrate an application comparing ground-based and satellite remote sensing products. The training will include lectures, hands-on exercises, and a homework assignment.\n\nCourse Format: Five 1.5-hour parts including Q&A.\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\n\nGoal 3: Ensure healthy lives and promote well-being for all at all ages\nTarget 3.9: By 2030, substantially reduce the number of deaths and illnesses from hazardous chemicals and air, water and soil pollution and contamination\n\nGoal 11: Make cities and human settlements inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable\nTarget 11.6: By 2030, reduce the adverse per capita environmental impact of cities, including by paying special attention to air quality and municipal and other waste management","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n1.\tIdentify the basic characteristics, capabilities, and limitations of the NASA instruments used for ground-based active and passive remote sensing of aerosols, ozone, and NO2.\n2.\tRecognize how the ground networks presented in the training sustain global long-term observations, support air quality and climate applications, and complement satellite observations.\n3.\tAccess relevant atmospheric composition data from appropriate NASA ground networks for given locations and application purposes.\n4.\tCompare and jointly analyze specific ground-based atmospheric composition data products with relevant satellite remote sensing data (e.g., satellite aerosol data products) for a given location and time.\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for Air Quality or Climate Application & Research Scientists and Researchers at Environmental Agencies.","registrationHowto":"To Register: https://go.nasa.gov/3XoE4mv\n\n\n","registrationEndDate":1724338800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"title":"Aerosols","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":24,"title":"Greenhouse gases and ozone","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":22,"title":"Air quality","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":37,"title":"Climate system monitoring - Atmosphere","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2443,"created":1720598548000,"updated":1721817663000,"title":"Monthly Regional Weather and Climate Forums - July 2024 Weather Briefings for Middle East Region","titleNative":"Monthly Regional Weather and Climate Forums - July 2024 Weather Briefings for Middle East Region","startDate":1722409200000,"endDate":1722412800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":null,"contactPerson":"Ibrahim AbdulSalam","contactEmail":"IbrahimAbdulSalam@caa.gov.om","contactUrl":"https://coemct.met.gov.om/course/view.php?id=83","description":"Monthly Regional Weather and Climate Forums- Weather Briefings generally focus on the latest hot events related to weather and climate in the Middle East Region and the latest updates in satellite earth observations.\n\nThe next Forum will be held on 31 July 2024, from 07:00 to 08:00 UTC, and it will focus on the following topics:\n1. Weather Briefing of July over Jordon.\n2. Project management essentials for Meteorology Professional\n\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"•\tMeteorologists & Weather Forecasters\n•\tEnvironment and Atmospheric Scientists\n•\tProject Managers of related fields\n•\tGeneral users of weather and Satellite Applications","registrationHowto":"To join the Forum, go to https://coemct.met.gov.om/course/view.php?id=83","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":8109,"name":"Zamzam AL-Rawahi ","email":"Zamzam.Rawahi@caa.gov.om","agency":"","country":"OM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":7,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false},{"id":7,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2447,"created":1721155759000,"updated":1721156569000,"title":"Drought Monitoring, Prediction, and Projection using NASA Earth System Data","titleNative":"Drought Monitoring, Prediction, and Projection using NASA Earth System Data","startDate":1721743200000,"endDate":1722542400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/4aNtEjv","description":"The United Nations has documented that drought frequency and duration have increased globally since 2000. According to this report 2.3 billion people around the world are currently facing water stress. Although droughts only represent 15% of natural disasters, they killed 650,000 people between 1970 and 2019. Also, it is predicted that by 2050, drought could affect more than 75% of the world’s population. Droughts affect drinking water availability, ecosystems, and crop production – critical for quality and sustainability of plant, animal, and human lives. For better water and agricultural management, it is crucial to monitor and forecast drought conditions. \n This four-part advanced training will build upon previous ARSET trainings and provide hands-on data analysis exercises for monitoring different types of drought (meteorological, hydrological, and agricultural). Moreover, the training will include drought prediction analysis on sub-seasonal to seasonal (S2S) time scales and climate change projection analysis of drought conditions.\n\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\n•\tGoal 2: End hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition and promote sustainable agriculture\n\n•\tGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts\n\nX (formerly Twitter) announcement:  https://x.com/nasaarset/status/1798750446351512022?s=46\n\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: \nBy the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n•\tIdentify important Earth Observations and tools for assessing short term (week to month) to long-term (seasonal to multi-decadal) drought conditions.\n•\tIdentify drought portals and relevant geophysical parameters for monitoring droughts globally and regionally. \n•\tAccess and analyze sub-seasonal to seasonal forecasts of temperature and precipitation for evolving drought conditions for a region of interest.\n•\tAccess and analyze climate change projection data to assess impacts on long term drought conditions for a region of interest.\n•\tExplore selected regional drought monitoring tools that can be customized for a user’s region of interest.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for water resources and disastermanagers and planners from local, state, federal, and international organizations interested in using remote sensing to monitor drought.","registrationHowto":"To Register: https://go.nasa.gov/4aNtEjv\n\nCourse Dates: July 23, 25, 30, and August 1, 2024\n\nTime: 10:00-12:00 or 14:00-16:00 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\n\n Format: Four 2-hour parts including Q&A. ","registrationEndDate":1722520800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","emai