ribbean - June 2024 Weather and Climate Discussion","titleNative":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - June 2024 Weather and Climate Discussion","startDate":1718722800000,"endDate":1718728200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"Bernie.Connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"These monthly weather and climate discussions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and focus on WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). The sessions highlight the use and interpretation of satellite imagery and products for meteorological applications, using SLIDER (http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu) for imagery. Anyone with an interest is welcome to attend.\n\nGoToWebinar is the software platform used. The session will begin at 15:00 UTC, with the webinar opening 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who wish to check their microphone.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2160225227190349141","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2416,"created":1716367273000,"updated":1752481244000,"title":"European Weather Cloud - Using object storage from customised instances in the EWC","titleNative":"European Weather Cloud - Using object storage from customised instances in the EWC","startDate":1718697600000,"endDate":1718703000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"online","host":"EUMETSAT & ECMWF","contactPerson":"DAS Training Team","contactEmail":"das-training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=436","description":"The European Weather Cloud (EWC) teams at ECMWF and EUMETSAT are pleased to invite you to the third thematic EWC webinar of 2024.\n\nThese recurring thematic webinars are aimed at the users of the EWC service, provided by EUMETSAT and ECMWF, which is available to all ECMWF and EUMETSAT Member and Co-operating States. Our primary objective is to provide guidance to users on how to effectively use the different capabilities of the EWC.\n\nWhile the first two webinars in 2023 focused on the basics to get you started, in this series we explore various aspects of cloud utilisation through examples, with a specific emphasis on key functionalities and cloud skills. \n\nIn this upcoming webinar, we will explore how to manage custom instance types and object storage within the EWC. Participants will be guided through the process of creating a customised instance type to set up a JupyterHub, building on the automation shown in our previous webinar in this series. From that Jupyter session, we will introduce you to the S3 object storage offering on the EWC and demonstrate how to manage it, covering various tools and practical examples.\n\nBy the end of this 1.5-hour session, participants will have gained an understanding of how to create instance types and effectively use object storage in the EWC environment. This webinar aims to equip you with the knowledge and skills needed to leverage the power of the European Weather Cloud for your data storage and management needs.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"The European Weather Cloud (EWC) teams at ECMWF and EUMETSAT are pleased to invite you to the third thematic EWC webinar of 2024.\n\nThese recurring thematic webinars are aimed at th","content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"Register at https://events.ecmwf.int/event/417/registrations/271/.","registrationEndDate":1718582400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email":"deleted.trainer@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"--","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"name":"European Weather Cloud"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2388,"created":1707401057000,"updated":1707719992000,"title":"International Summer School on Applications with the Newest Multi-spectral Environmental Satellites","titleNative":"International Summer School on Applications with the Newest Multi-spectral Environmental Satellites","startDate":1718607600000,"endDate":1719399600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Bracciano","host":"Italian Air Force - Met Service","contactPerson":"Ivan Smiljanic","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"The objective of the Summer School is an in depth explanation of methods and techniques used to extract information from environmental satellite data (both GEO and LEO), with emphasis on the latest measuring technologies, namely MTG mission. Students are guided through a practical work with the selected satellite data, exploring its utility using different visualisation tools widely available (e.g. SIFT, McIDAS-V, Jupyter Notebooks). This is accompanied with the theoretical background in remote-sensing principles.\n\nThis year the School will be named after Paolo Rosci, officer of the Italian Air Force who dedicated his entire professional life to the satellite meteorology. Bright remote sensing scientist, he combined within himself the ability to conceive new methods and develop operational applications in this field, as well as the empathetic character to foster the sharing of ideas, tutoring with enthusiasm the new generations of satellite meteorologists.\nPaolo passed away last April, after years of courageous fight against a disease.\n\n","country":{"id":"IT","name":"ITALY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"Enhanced skills in satellite data analysis.","summary":"The objective of the Summer School is an in depth explanation of methods and techniques used to extract information from environmental satellite data (both GEO and LEO), with empha","content":null,"targetAudience":"Experienced professionals working with remote sensing data (forecasters, researchers, post-graduate students etc.) interested in updating their knowledge and skills on the newest developments in remote sensing data. The applicants must have an academic degree either in Meteorology, Physics, Environmental Sciences or Environmental Engineering. ","registrationHowto":"Applications to this course are based on the nominations by the heads of delegations of EUMETSAT members. \nNominated candidates may apply at:\nhttps://training.eumetsat.int/mod/questionnaire/view.php?id=16791","registrationEndDate":1714348800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5844,"name":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","email":"natasa.strelecmahovic@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":23,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":70,"value":"NHMS","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":27,"title":"Atmospheric phenomena","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":21,"name":"European Weather Cloud"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":60,"name":"Various","uniqueName":"Various (ERS)"}]},{"id":2418,"created":1716446250000,"updated":null,"title":"EUMeTrain Monthly Weather Briefing ","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Monthly Weather Briefing ","startDate":1718107200000,"endDate":1718109600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMetrain","contactPerson":"Dominik Rukavina","contactEmail":"drukavina@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"https://resources.eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Once a month EUMeTrain organizes a Weather Briefing open to everyone to discuss the present weather situation. The basis of the discussions are todays satellite images and the corresponding numerical weather prediction data. A basic knowledge on conceptual models used in satellite meteorology is of advantage to follow the presentations.\n\nThe session is online and is done through the Webex software.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"Once a month EUMeTrain organizes a Weather Briefing open to everyone to discuss the present weather situation. The basis of the discussions are todays satellite images and the corr","content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters\n\nStudents","registrationHowto":"To join, go to: https://resources.eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html and follow the instructions.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9945,"name":"Dominik Rukavina","email":"drukavina@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"nan","country":"HR","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":17,"value":"EuMeTrain"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":23,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2412,"created":1715005592000,"updated":1715005749000,"title":"Earth Observations for Humanitarian Applications","titleNative":"Earth Observations for Humanitarian Applications","startDate":1717682400000,"endDate":1718908200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/get-involved/training/english/arset-earth-observations-humanitarian-applications?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=Humanitarian2024","description":"Refugees, internally displaced people (IDPs), and other displaced populations are made more vulnerable to climate change impacts due to their socio-political marginalization. This three-part training presents concrete strategies for mapping localized climate conditions with risks faced by refugee and IDP communities around the world.\nThe training will focus on flood risk assessments and specific challenges for assessing flood risk in refugee and IDP camps; gauging long-term heat stress in refugee camps and the challenges with decision making surrounding heat risk; and monitoring drought effects on agricultural landscapes in refugee settings using Earth observations (EO) to explore the correlations between anomalies in crop productivity and weather-based factors.\nBy the end of the training, participants will be able to integrate EO, building footprint and infrastructure data, and population data to quantify climate risk and development trends in specific humanitarian settings, and recognize the value and limitations of specific EO and geospatial datasets.\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\n•\tGoal 13.1: Strengthen resilience and adaptive capacity to climate-related hazards and natural disasters in all countries\n\nCourse Format: Three 1.5-hour parts including Q&A.\n\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n•\tRecognize the importance of measuring flood risk, long-term heat stress, and drought effects in refugee and IDP communities around the world\n•\tApply workflows incorporating Earth observations, geospatial, and demographic data to identify localized climate risk in refugee and IDP settings\n•\tDiscuss decision making strategies for mapping and managing climate conditions with risks faced by refugee and IDP communities\n•\tSummarize opportunities and shortcomings of specific Earth observations and geospatial datasets for climate risk and development indicators in humanitarian settings\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for Government ministries, international agencies, and non-governmental organizations (NGOs) involved in humanitarian response and decision support activities.","registrationHowto":"\nTimes: 10:00-11:30 or 13:00-14:30 EDT (UTC-4)\n\nThere will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\n","registrationEndDate":1718902800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"title":"Climate","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":16,"title":"Emergency","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":10,"title":"Floods","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2409,"created":1713802204000,"updated":1715192397000,"title":"NOAA/WMO RA IV An Interactive Analysis of Tropical Storm Philippe Workshop","titleNative":"NOAA/WMO RA IV An Interactive Analysis of Tropical Storm Philippe Workshop","startDate":1717506000000,"endDate":1717776000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Bridgetown","host":"Caribbean Institute for Meteorology and Hydrology, Barbados","contactPerson":"Kathy-Ann Caesar","contactEmail":"kacaesar@cimh.edu.bb","contactUrl":"https://rammb2.cira.colostate.edu/training/analysis-tropical-storm-philippe-workshop/","description":"The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA), in collaboration with the Caribbean Institute for Meteorology & Hydrology (CIMH) and the Caribbean Meteorological Organization (CMO) Headquarters Unit have developed a virtual training case study workshop focused on analyzing Tropical Storm Philippe as the cyclone evolved and crossed the Caribbean Islands late September/early October 2023. \n\n\nThis workshop will provide an interactive analysis of TS Philippe through the use of satellite data and products, models, forecasts and reports which were used to identify, track, predict, and warn about the multi-hazard impacts of the storm, successes, challenges, and future improvements. Hands-on exercises and prediction practices will supplement the training, allowing the participants to address forecasting challenges and improve decision making. This is an English language only event with no simultaneous interpretation available.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"At the end of the workshop, participants will have developed competency in using the data and products for weather forecasting, prediction and monitoring aligned with WMO Satellite Skills and Knowledge for Operational Meteorologists and the AmeriGEO Societal Benefit Areas (Water, Disasters, Health, etc.).\n\nReference \nWMO Satellite Skills and Knowledge for Operational Meteorologists - https://library.wmo.int/viewer/55487?medianame=sp-12-2018_en_#page=1&viewer=picture&o=bookmark&n=0&q=\n\n AmeriGEO Societal Benefit Areas (Water, Disasters, Health, etc.) - https://www.amerigeo.org","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters","registrationHowto":"The link to the workshop information page is  here(https://rammb2.cira.colostate.edu/training/analysis-tropical-storm-philippe-workshop/).\nThe link to register for the workshop is here (https://forms.gle/uaQg1cMKGPrjAKVa9)","registrationEndDate":1716681540000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5869,"name":"Kathy-Ann Caesar ","email":"kacaesar@cimh.edu.bb","agency":"","country":"BB","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":106,"value":"Seminar"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":110,"value":"NASA","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true},{"id":190,"value":"RTC","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":8,"value":"PWS: Weather Broadcasters and Communicators"},{"id":1,"value":"Aviation: Aeronautical Meteorological Forecaster"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2413,"created":1715333654000,"updated":1715333983000,"title":"EUMeTrain Forecaster's Event Week","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Forecaster's Event Week","startDate":1717416900000,"endDate":1717678800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":null,"contactPerson":"Dominik Rukavina","contactEmail":"drukavina@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"https://resources.eumetrain.org/events/ForecastersEW_Agenda.pdf","description":"Between 3 - 6 June, EUMeTrain is organising a practice-oriented event week - the Forecaster's Week. For one week, there will be lectures and workshops on topics clearly related to weather forecasting. The spectrum of lectures ranges from aviation meteorology, Sahara sand to convection and RGBs. The working methods of various weather services, their country-specific challenges and products will also be highlighted this week. As always with our Event Weeks, the presentations will also be recorded. Further information will be made available on the EUMeTrain website.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"Between 3 - 6 June, EUMeTrain is organising a practice-oriented event week - the Forecaster's Week. For one week, there will be lectures and workshops on topics clearly related to ","content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters\n\nStudents","registrationHowto":"To register, follow the link: [https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdlXtvCLMChjvECeI-CLHtzSt6HbmH9_jbZtBpejp-FlQTepw/viewform?usp=sf_link].","registrationEndDate":1717286400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9945,"name":"Dominik Rukavina","email":"drukavina@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"nan","country":"HR","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":17,"value":"EuMeTrain"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":106,"value":"Seminar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":161,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2363,"created":1703069173000,"updated":1703070191000,"title":" ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbeds on Severe Convective Storms in Preparation for MTG","titleNative":" ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbeds on Severe Convective Storms in Preparation for MTG","startDate":1717405200000,"endDate":1717768800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Darmstadt","host":"ESSL","contactPerson":"Stephan Bojinski","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"EUMETSAT, in partnership with the European Severe Storms Laboratory (ESSL), offers a series of testbeds to prepare operational meteorologists / forecasters to use data from the Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) and the EUMETSAT Polar System Second Generation (EPS-SG) Programmes. \nThis second instance of the testbeds will be exceptionally organized at EUMETSAT premises in Darmstadt.\n\nThe testbeds teach (i) forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or experimental forecasting tools, and (ii) the evaluation of such tools. At these events, forecasters learn by simulating an operational forecasting situation, making severe weather forecasts and nowcasts using a mix of new nowcasting and forecasting tools, and then evaluating the quality of the forecast and the value of the data and tools.\n\nParticular focus is given to the use of novel MTG satellite data from the FCI spectral imager and from the LI lightning imager instrument. In addition, a range of state-of-the-art nowcasting tools and numerical weather prediction-based guidance will be evaluated.","country":{"id":"DE","name":"GERMANY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"EUMETSAT, in partnership with the European Severe Storms Laboratory (ESSL), offers a series of testbeds to prepare operational meteorologists / forecasters to use data from the Met","content":null,"targetAudience":"The target group of the 2024 testbeds is operational forecasters who train other forecasters and forecasters with a strong background in severe storms meteorology.","registrationHowto":"Applications to this course are based on the nominations by the heads of delegations of EUMETSAT members. Nominated candidates may apply at: \nhttps://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","registrationEndDate":1707955200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5844,"name":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","email":"natasa.strelecmahovic@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":15,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":29,"title":"Severe storms","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"title":"Mesoscale systems and convective storms","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2406,"created":1712755343000,"updated":1712757033000,"title":"55th International Liege Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics short course: Detection and analysis of ocean extremes","titleNative":"55th International Liege Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics short course: Detection and analysis of ocean extremes","startDate":1716800400000,"endDate":1717174800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Liege","host":"University of Liège","contactPerson":"Ben Loveday","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"Marine Earth observation is an essential tool for monitoring physical and biogeochemical ocean systems. By adopting multi-sensor approaches, and continually engaging in reprocessing campaigns, we are able to ever-expand our satellite records in time, moving beyond monitoring the event-scale to assess interannual, and in some cases decadal, variability. As a result, we are now able to establish climatological baselines from which we can start to categorise extremes in many of these systems.\n\nIn this 3-hour practical tutorial, held at the <b><a href=\"https://www.ocean-colloquium.uliege.be/cms/c_14229949/en/international-liege-colloquium-on-ocean-dynamics\">55th International Liege Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics</a></b>, we will introduce the EUMETSAT marine satellite products made available through our Copernicus and Mandatory missions, as well downstream products offered by the EUMETSAT Ocean and Sea Ice Satellite Applications Facility (OSI SAF) and Copernicus Marine Service, commenting on key differences between working with data at various satellite levels. Following this, we will introduce a series of workflows that participants can select and explore to learn more about selecting appropriate satellite data to monitor ocean extremes, including:; harmful algal blooms, extreme waves, sea ice and marine heatwaves. These workflows will be supported by dedicated, Python-based, Jupyter Notebooks, which will outline data access, manipulation and visualisation and will be made available for free use by all attendees after the training event concludes. Experts in the various products will be available to support your questions throughout the training session. \n\nWe highly encourage you to pre-register for this session, as this will allow us to provide you with preparatory information on accessing the various data sets we will need for our analyses.","country":{"id":"BE","name":"BELGIUM","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of the course, participants will be able to;\n\n* recall what Copernicus marine data is available from EUMETSAT and downstream services\n* access this data using EUMETSAT data access services and the Copernicus WEkEO harmonised data access API\n* interact with ocean remote sensing products via our Jupyter Notebook training catalogue\n* understand how to identify and monitor ocean extremes in physical and biogeochemical ocean systems using Copernicus satellite data","summary":"Marine Earth observation is an essential tool for monitoring physical and biogeochemical ocean systems. By adopting multi-sensor approaches, and continually engaging in reprocessin","content":null,"targetAudience":"This training session is appropriate for;\n\n* Established and early career marine remote sensing scientists\n* Oceanographers of all disciplines who are looking to incorporate remote sensing into their research\n* Staff from agencies monitoring the coastal and open ocean environment for extreme phenomena","registrationHowto":"Information for registering for the 55th International Liège Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics can be found at https://www.ocean-colloquium.uliege.be/. All colloquium attendees are welcome at the training.","registrationEndDate":1716422400000,"fees":"Yes","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5877,"name":"Ben Loveday","email":"Ben.Loveday@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":