rl":"","description":"Testbeds are organized both to teach forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or even experimental forecasting tools, and to evaluate such tools. At these events forecasters will learn by doing, by making severe weather forecasts and nowcasts using a mix of new nowcasting and forecasting tools, followed by a verification. Particular focus will be given to the use of proxy-MTG satellite data (e.g., from the NASA MODIS, NOAA VIIRS and Sentinel-3/OLCI instruments) and, once operationally available, real data from MTG in order to prepare forecasters for the use of next-generation satellite data. In addition, a range of state-of-the-art nowcasting tools and numerical weather prediction-based guidance (i.e. from DWD, ZAMG, ESSL, and ECMWF) will be evaluated.","country":{"id":"AT","name":"AUSTRIA","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"Enhancing skills in forecasting severe convective storms.\nEvaluating different products for convection nowcasting, including satellite products. ","summary":"Testbeds are organized both to teach forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or even experimental forecasting tools, and to evaluate such tools. At these events","content":null,"targetAudience":"Weather forecasters from EUMETSAT Member States who train other forecasters and forecasters with a strong background in severe storms meteorology.","registrationHowto":"Invitation has been sent to EUMETSAT Heads of Delegations. If you wish to apply for the testbed please contact the Head of Delegation of your country or your Head of forecast office.","registrationEndDate":1675382400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5844,"name":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","email":"natasa.strelecmahovic@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":29,"title":"Severe storms","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"title":"Mesoscale systems and convective storms","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"name":"Metop"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":3,"name":"MTG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2334,"created":1695392245000,"updated":1695395086000,"title":"Transforming Earth Observation (EO) Data into Building Infrastructure Data Sets for Disaster Risk Modeling","titleNative":"Transforming Earth Observation (EO) Data into Building Infrastructure Data Sets for Disaster Risk Modeling","startDate":1696341600000,"endDate":1696968000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA’s Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET) ","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/47w2xsM","description":"Climate change is not only changing the location, frequency, and severity of environmental extremes and hazards, but also the baseline spatial and temporal patterns that have served as the basis for land use planning, the design of infrastructure, and the development of construction standards and practices. To anticipate the emerging impacts on communities, we must improve understanding of exposed assets, including the built infrastructure and its characteristics such as structure type, use, occupance, compliance with engineering standards, and replacement cost. GIS databases track exposure on a wide variety of scales, formats, and levels of detail, yet aggregating these data for risk assessment typically results in skewed datasets that present a view of risk that is limited to known assets. In this short course, we will cover the basics of natural hazard risk modeling and exposure development with a focus on fusing data from multiple datasets expressly for the purposes of risk assessment.  We will also present examples applying the techniques to applications related to flood risk assessment, climate adaptation, and earthquake modeling. A portion of this training was funded by the NASA Applied Sciences Disasters Program.\n  \nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \n•\tSDG 11: Make cities and human settlements inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable\n•\tGoal 9: Build resilient infrastructure, promote inclusive and sustainable industrialization and foster innovation\n\n\n\nTransformar Datos de Observación de la Tierra en Conjuntos de Datos de Infraestructura Construida para la Modelación del Riesgo de Desastres\n                                                                                \nEl cambio climático está cambiando no solo la ubicación, frecuencia y severidad de los extremos y peligros ambientales, sino también los patrones espaciales y temporales fundamentales que sirven de base para la planificación del uso del suelo, el diseño de la infraestructura y el desarrollo de estándares y prácticas de construcción. Para anticipar los impactos emergentes en las comunidades, debemos mejorar nuestro entendimiento sobre bienes expuestos, incluso la infraestructura construida y sus características, como tipo de infraestructura, uso, capacidad, cumplimiento con estándares de ingeniería y costo de reemplazamiento. Las bases de datos de GIS (sistemas de información geográfica por sus siglas en inglés) vigilan la exposición en una gran variedad de escalas, formatos y niveles de detalle. Sin embargo, la agregación de estos datos para la evaluación del riesgo normalmente produce conjuntos de datos sesgados que presentan una visión del riesgo que se limita a bienes conocidos. En este breve curso, cubriremos los fundamentos de la modelación de peligros naturales y el desarrollo de la exposición con un enfoque a la fusión de datos de múltiples conjuntos de datos explícitamente para propósitos de evaluación del riesgo.  También presentaremos ejemplos aplicando las técnicas a aplicaciones relacionadas con la evaluación del riesgo de inundaciones, adaptación climática y modelación de terremotos. Una parte de esta capacitación fue financiada por el Programa de Desastres de Ciencias Aplicadas de la NASA.\n\nObjetivos de Desarrollo Sostenible de la ONU Relevantes:\n•\tObjetivo 11: Objetivo 11: Lograr que las ciudades y los asentamientos humanos sean inclusivos, seguros, resilientes y sostenibles\n•\tObjectivo 9: Construir infraestructuras resilientes, promover la industrialización inclusiva y sostenible y fomentar la innovación\n\n \n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n•\tRecognize what building vulnerability is and why it is important for risk modeling\n•\tIdentify the core elements of natural hazard risk modeling and asset loss estimation\n•\tIdentify fundamental approaches for developing building-exposure models using Earth Observation data and tools\n•\tApply a basic procedure to model built infrastructure exposure and vulnerability characteristics from Earth Observation data\n•\tEvaluate building-specific exposure data sets to identify key components for fit, validity, consistency and rectify bias \n•\tEvaluate the appropriate use of modeled building exposure data to a given community\n•\tApply strategies to identify and address equity and bias considerations\n•\tApply approaches to validate building data with imagery for regional datasets\n•\tDocument your exposure development process through metadata so that others can understand the process used, the limitations, and how to update if necessary.","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Audience: Advanced-level students interested in risk assessment and climate change adaptation, emergency managers, risk modelers, GIS analysts, EO researchers and analysts, Structural Engineers, climate adaptation researchers and planners","registrationHowto":"Times and Registration Information:  \n  \nEnglish Session: 10:00-12:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/47w2xsM\nSpanish Session: 14:00-16:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3YEL6SX","registrationEndDate":1696960800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":25,"value":"NASA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2327,"created":1694434195000,"updated":1697795397000,"title":"Micro-Learning in the U.S. National Weather Service","titleNative":"Micro-Learning in the U.S. National Weather Service","startDate":1696248000000,"endDate":null,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETCAL","contactPerson":null,"contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/ui#/catalog/course/41c80ad3-585e-45aa-926f-b5cbed6b34ee","description":"Are you ready to transform your training methods? Join our upcoming webinar as we discuss 'micro-learning,' the global technique for bite-sized learning. We'll explore how it applies to atmospheric science, overview its strengths and weaknesses, and showcase practical examples from the U.S. National Weather Service.\n\nThe webinar will be presented by Megan Taylor. She is a Master Instructor at the U.S. National Weather Service Training Center. She specializes in training for media interviews and communicating weather, water, and climate phenomena.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"https://knmi.webex.com/weblink/register/r914c7f89e75ac5b7653aa419a61aeb82","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":null,"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"