Connell","contactEmail":"Bernie.Connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb2.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"The sessions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). Session time is 15:00 UTC; it will start 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection. GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the internet. \n\nSatellite imagery shown during the session is from SLIDER: http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/931114157786562907 ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2321,"created":1691760095000,"updated":1691760179000,"title":"Satellite Data for Air Quality Environmental Justice and Equity Applications","titleNative":"Satellite Data for Air Quality Environmental Justice and Equity Applications","startDate":1692811800000,"endDate":1694032200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3rjqUJo","description":"In this training, the NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training (ARSET) program in collaboration with the Health and Air Quality Science Team (HAQAST) Tiger Team on Satellite Data for Environmental Justice, will demonstrate how remotely-sensed environmental indicators, specifically for air pollution, can be paired with demographic data to understand disproportionate exposures among minoritized and marginalized population subgroups. \nThis training will consist of three two-hour sessions and be held online. The first session will provide an introduction to how satellite data have been used in environmental justice (EJ) applications. The second session will focus on satellite remote sensing of air quality, emphasizing aspects of environmental justice related to air pollution exposure. Finally, the third session will have interactive exercises combining satellite and demographic data with EJSCREEN and Python. The primary focus of this training is on environmental justice issues in the United States, with some additional examples from around the world.\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\n•\tGoal 3: Ensure healthy lives and promote well-being for all at all ages\n•\tTarget 3.9: By 2030, substantially reduce the number of deaths and illnesses from hazardous chemicals and air, water and soil pollution and contamination\n•\tGoal 11: Make cities and human settlements inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable\n•\tTarget 11.6: By 2030, reduce the adverse per capita environmental impact of cities, including by paying special attention to air quality and municipal and other waste management\n\nCourse Dates: August 23, 30, & September 6, 2023\n \nTime: 13:30-15:30 EDT (UTC-4)\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\n\tDescribe, at a high level, how satellite data have been combined with socioeconomic information to investigate EJ issues such as heat exposure or access to green space\n\tIdentify remote sensing data products which are most relevant to assessing EJ related to air quality and pollutant exposure  \n\tArticulate the benefits and limitations of using remote sensing data to assess EJ concerns related to air quality  \n\tImport relevant air quality datasets into EJSCREEN, and use EJSCREEN to investigate and compare air quality with other environmental and demographic datasets\n\tPair appropriate satellite datasets for environmental indicators (air quality) with demographic information using Python\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"The primary audience for this training consists of:\n\tCommunity groups and other organizations involved in developing EJ mapping tools\n\tResearchers analyzing EJ issues, especially related to air quality, and interested in incorporating remote sensing data into their research\n\tGovernment, non-profit, and private agencies involved in EJ activities\n\tCommunity groups and individuals (especially in the USA) concerned with EJ and interested to learn about the potential of satellite data for informing EJ action\n","registrationHowto":"Please register here: https://go.nasa.gov/3rjqUJo","registrationEndDate":1694021400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2350,"created":1699890080000,"updated":1700689054000,"title":"Introduction to Meteosat Third Generation and EUMETSAT Satellite Data Processing  - Space Week Northeast 2023","titleNative":"Introduction to Meteosat Third Generation and EUMETSAT Satellite Data Processing  - Space Week Northeast 2023","startDate":1692057600000,"endDate":1692230400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":null,"contactPerson":null,"contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"Learn about the new generation of EUMETSAT geostationary satellites (MTG - Meteosat Third Generation) and the processing of data and products from EUMETSAT's GEO and LEO satellites. Through the use of Python Jupyter notebooks participants learn to retrieve and explore data from EUMETSAT data store and from EUMETSAT SAFs (LSA and Hydrology). Special focus is given to  land monitoring products such as LST, Vegetation and Soil Moisture.","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Through the use of python language in JNs, participants will learn: EUMESAT data access; How to visualize EUMETSAT satellite products; How to compute products anomalies.","summary":"Learn about the new generation of EUMETSAT geostationary satellites (MTG - Meteosat Third Generation) and the processing of data and products from EUMETSAT's GEO and LEO satellites","content":null,"targetAudience":"University students","registrationHowto":null,"registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5848,"name":"Carla Barroso ","email":"carla.barroso@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"BLENDED","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":80,"value":"Other","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":5,"value":"Portuguese","iso639":"pt"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":19,"title":"Data Applications","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2272,"created":1678361509000,"updated":1681198512000,"title":"Trevor Platt Science Foundation Symposium 2023 webinar series and training course","titleNative":"Trevor Platt Science Foundation Symposium 2023 webinar series and training course","startDate":1691398800000,"endDate":1691514000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Plymouth","host":"Plymouth Marine Laboratory","contactPerson":"Ben Loveday","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://www.trevorfoundation.org/symposium/training-course-tpsf-symposium-2023","description":"The [**Trevor Platt Science Foundation (TPSF)**](https://www.trevorfoundation.org/home) was launched in Aug 2021 to promote capacity building, research, and education in ocean sciences, with a strong emphasis on international networking, to nurture the next generation of young scientists and give them the tools they need to succeed in their chosen careers. As part of its 2023 activities, TPSF and partners are organising a scientific symposium on 'Satellite-based tools for investigating aquatic ecosystems'. This symposium will be preceded by a [**training course**](https://www.trevorfoundation.org/symposium/training-course-tpsf-symposium-2023) with both online and in-person components, both of which will feature contributions and support from EUMETSAT. Please see below for more details on each component;\n\n#### Webinar series:\n\nThe online training component will feature a [**webinar series**](https://esait.webex.com/webappng/sites/esait/webinar/webinarSeries/register/c9ad9cca96744300bd91c26fea8e4120) dedicated to a subset of the main symposium themes, including: remote sensing of ocean colour, water quality and human health, primary production, climate change, physiology and ecology of marine phytoplankton and practical aspects of working with satellite data. The series is open to all. Please see below for details on how to register. EUMETSAT will be contributing to the sessions on data repositories an access (webinar #3).\n\n#### In person training:\n\n***Applications for the in person training session are closed.***\n\nIn person training will focus on giving participants the the opportunity to learn state-of-the-art techniques in data processing, optical modelling and machine learning. EUMETSAT trainers will provide tools and tutorials for working with products from their Copernicus marine products portfolio, with a particular focus on Sentinel-3 OLCI. These tools will be exploited to help participants access and visualise marine remote sensing products in ways that are directly relevant to their own applications of interest.","country":{"id":"GB","name":"UNITED KINGDOM","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"Participants will be able to;\n\n* Recall the main marine Earth observation product repositories (webinar #3).\n* Access this data using web and API based approaches (webinar #3).\n* Interact with ocean remote sensing products using software tools such as Python based Jupyter Notebooks and SNAP (in person training).\n* Implement these tools to access and visualise remote sensing products aligned with their own applications (in person training).","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"The training is open to early career scientists, technicians, postgraduate students (PhD or MSc) and post-doctoral scholars involved in aquatic ecosystems research.","registrationHowto":"* Applications for the in person training event are closed.\n* Registration for the online webinar series is still possible via this [**link**](https://esait.webex.com/webappng/sites/esait/webinar/webinarSeries/register/c9ad9cca96744300bd91c26fea8e4120).","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5877,"name":"Ben Loveday","email":"Ben.Loveday@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2316,"created":1689024628000,"updated":1689057394000,"title":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - July 2023","titleNative":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean - July 2023","startDate":1689778800000,"endDate":1689782400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"Bernie.Connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb2.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"The sessions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). Session time is 15:00 UTC; it will start 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection. GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the internet. \n\nSatellite imagery shown during the session is from SLIDER: http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2049733098629847644 ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2313,"created":1687440882000,"updated":1700686953000,"title":"EUMETSAT series of short courses: Warming oceans: using satellites to monitor sea surface temperature, ocean heat content and marine heatwaves","titleNative":"EUMETSAT series of short courses: Warming oceans: using satellites to monitor sea surface temperature, ocean heat content and marine heatwaves","startDate":1689764400000,"endDate":1689775200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Ben Loveday","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=489","description":"This advanced event will focus on the application of satellite sea surface temperature (SST) measurements to several current applications including ocean heat content, cyclogenesis, and marine heatwaves. It will present some technical background on how products are derived, and how to select the most suitable products for different applications. \n\nA practical demonstration will show the calculation of SST anomalies and marine heatwave indicators. Leading experts will then present the use of SST and derived products for research applications, and what this data is telling us about the current status of heat in different ocean regions. \n\nThe short course will consist of a 3-hour webinar on July 19th (11:00 - 14:00 UTC), which will include a Q&A and feedback session. To support participants in using the data sets and tools we highlight, we will keep the online Q&A platform open for one week following the webinar, responding quickly to any queries posted.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"\nParticipants will:\n- Understand the links between sea surface temperature, ocean heat content, and marine heat waves.\n- Know how to access SST data. \n- Understand how and when to use different types of SST products and data records.\n- Be able to calculate SST anomalies.\n- Be aware of the current status of ocean temperatures, heat content, marine heatwaves, and their impacts.","summary":"This advanced event will focus on the application of satellite sea surface temperature (SST) measurements to several current applications including ocean heat content, cyclogenesis","content":null,"targetAudience":"\n- Established and early career sea surface temperature researchers.\n- Operational oceanographers.\n- Private and public sector specialists engaged in marine monitoring activities.\n- Previous attendees of EUMETSAT Supporting Marine Applications courses that focussed on sea surface temperature applications.","registrationHowto":"Please register at this link: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_Q6zwuWRTS1SjT-uRQrs-7Q ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":38,"title":"Climate system monitoring - Ocean","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":41,"title":"Ocean dynamics","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":6,"title":"Marine","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":20,"name":"WEkEO"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2314,"created":1688049604000,"updated":1688049678000,"title":"Monitoring Water Quality of Inland Lakes using Remote Sensing","titleNative":"Monitoring Water Quality of Inland Lakes using Remote Sensing","startDate":1689688800000,"endDate":1690318800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3q09EZ4","description":"This advanced-level training will focus on using remote sensing observations from Landsat 8 and 9, Sentinel-2, and Sentinel-3 for assessing water quality parameters, including chlorophyll-a concentration, turbidity, and Total Suspended Solids (TSS) in inland lakes. This training will also highlight the importance of in situ measurements of these parameters, coincident with satellite observations, in developing methodologies for operational water quality monitoring. Participants will perform hands-on exercises in Google Earth Engine (GEE) to access satellite data and develop methodologies to assess water quality parameters. In addition, an overview of Cyanobacteria Assessment Network (CyAN), an early warning system to assess algal blooms in freshwater lakes will be provided.\n \nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\n•\tGoal 6: Ensure availability and sustainable management of water and sanitation for all\n•\tTarget 6.6: By 2030, protect and restore water-related ecosystems, including mountains, forests, wetlands, rivers, aquifers and lakes\n \nCourse Dates: July 18, 20, & 25, 2023\n \nTimes: 10:00-12:00 or 15:00-17:00 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\n \nCourse Format: Three, 2-hour parts including Q&A\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives:\nBy the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\n·       Identify satellites, sensors, and data relevant for inland water quality monitoring\n·       Identify best practices for using remote sensing data for water quality monitoring based on the strengths and limitations of data from the available remote sensors\n·       Recognize how remote sensing data is used in conjunction with in situ data to provide reliable and actionable results\n·       Follow the Google Earth Engine procedure and settings to be able to analyze water quality for different types of inland water bodies\n·       Follow methodology on Google Earth Engine (GEE) to derive water quality parameters from remote sensing and in situ measurements\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Audience: The primary audience for this training consists of local, state, federal government and non-governmental entities working with water resources and lake ecosystem management, drinking water utilities, public health advisories, and freshwater fisheries.\n ","registrationHowto":"\nTo Register: https://go.nasa.gov/3q09EZ4\n \n\n","registrationEndDate":1690311600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2319,"created":1689164807000,"updated":null,"title":"Destination Earth Data Lake Workshop for Use cases","titleNative":"Destination Earth Data Lake Workshop for Use cases","startDate":1689080400000,"endDate":1689181200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Darmstadt","host":null,"contactPerson":"Danaele Puechmaille","contactEmail":"Danaele.Puechmaille@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":null,"country":null,"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":"By invitation only.","registrationEndDate":1689066000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5841,"name":"Regina Hoefenmayer","email":"regina.hoefenmayer@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"BLENDED","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2283,"created":1678962380000,"updated":1700686896000,"title":"EUMETSAT short course: Wildfire Monitoring with Next-Generation Satellites","titleNative":"EUMETSAT short course: Wildfire Monitoring with Next-Generation Satellites","startDate":1689062400000,"endDate":1689066000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":null,"contactPerson":"Federico Fierli","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=480","description":"Recently launched and upcoming satellites, such as the Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) satellite (launched in December 2022) and the Metop - Second Generation (Metop-SG) (to be launched in 2025) will provide advanced capabilities and valuable data for monitoring fires and their impacts. This short course showcases a series of case studies and associated workflows in Jupyter notebooks for fire applications using proxy data from MTG and Metop-SG. Join us to explore the case studies focusing on fires affecting the area around Gironde, France during summer 2022! \n\nEach case study focuses on a specific past fire event to demonstrate how data from MTG and Metop-SG can be used for operational and monitoring purposes. Different stages of the fire life cycle are highlighted: from pre-fire risk, active burning and smoke transport to post-fire burnt area assessment. Each figure shown in the case study is linked to a Jupyter notebook that demonstrates in an instructional, step-by-step workflow how the figure was created and which proxy data was used.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":"Recently launched and upcoming satellites, such as the Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) satellite (launched in December 2022) and the Metop - Second Generation (Metop-SG) (to be lau","content":null,"targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Please register at this link: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_MpZlqzhnRCibhjte5bZ8ag ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2306,"created":1684870187000,"updated":1684870598000,"title":"Assessing the Impacts of Fires on Watershed Health","titleNative":"Assessing the Impacts of Fires on Watershed Health","startDate":1688655600000,"endDate":1689280200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training (ARSET) Program","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3B8RjLF","description":"This advanced-level training will focus on using remote sensing observations for monitoring post-fire impacts on watershed health, building off the ARSET training offered in 2021: Satellite Observations and Tools for Fire Risk, Detection, and Analysis. Specifically, this training will highlight uses of NASA Earth observations (EO) for pre-fire land cover mapping, watershed delineation and stream mapping, post-fire burn severity mapping, and pre- and post-fire riverine and freshwater water quality. This three-part training will highlight case studies that use remote sensing data for assessing the impacts of fires on watersheds. This training will also provide participants with hands-on exercises for using NASA EO for these assessments within the Soil Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) and Google Earth Engine.\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\n•\tGoal 6: Ensure availability and sustainable management of water and sanitation for all\n•\tTarget 6.6: By 2030, protect and restore water-related ecosystems, including mountains, forests, wetlands, rivers, aquifers and lakes\n•\tGoal 11: Make cities and human settlements inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable\n•\tTarget 11.5: By 2030, significantly reduce the number of deaths and the number of people affected and substantially decrease the direct economic losses relative to global gross domestic product caused by disasters, including water-related disasters, with a focus on protecting the poor and people in vulnerable situations\n•\tGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss\n•\tTarget 15.3: By 2030, combat desertification, restore degraded land and soil, including land affected by desertification, drought and floods, and strive to achieve a land degradation-neutral world\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\n•\tDistinguish, compare, and contrast the biophysical conditions pre-and post-fire\n•\tAnalyze the key fire science criteria to select the appropriate data from satellites/instruments for a given watershed\n•\tAcquire land use & land cover maps for the region of interest\n•\tSelect river basin and sub-basin boundaries for their region of interest\n•\tRecognize how to develop a river basin-scale model using SWAT to simulate the quality and quantity of surface and groundwater\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"The primary audience for this training consists of local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations involved in resource and ecosystem management and disaster response. The secondary audience for this training are those with an interest in applying remote sensing data toward fire science.","registrationHowto":"Course Dates: July 6, 11, & 13, 2023\n\nTimes: 11:00-12:30 or 15:00-16:30 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\n\nTo register: https://go.nasa.gov/3B8RjLF\n","registrationEndDate":1689274800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2301,"created":1684140411000,"updated":1752481244000,"title":"Data Access Services - How to use the EUMETSAT Data Access Client (EUMDAC) Command Line Interface (CLI)","titleNative":"Data Access Services - How to use the EUMETSAT Data Access Client (EUMDAC) Command Line Interface (CLI)","startDate":1688472000000,"endDate":null,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Noemi Marsico","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=436#section-1","description":"This series of 30-minute sessions is designed to support the transition from the EUMETSAT Data Centre in favour of the Data Store. This particular session focuses on using the EUMETSAT Data Access Client (EUMDAC) through Command Line Interface (CLI). Due to the reduction of products in the Data Centre, this session will be a great opportunity to remove any technical challenges in using the Data Access Client Services (EUMDAC) to interact with APIs, and engage users in discussion with EUMETSAT experts and colleagues. Please, read more information about the transition here: https://eumetsatspace.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/EUM/blog/2023/03/22/1965359114/Switching+the+data+access+from+the+Data+Centre+to+the+Data+Store+for+faster+delivery+times.\n\nThe session will cover 30 minutes approximately.\n\nWe encourage you to post your questions on sli.do ( https://app.sli.do/event/flsdivtc ) so that we can adapt the content of the next session to answer them!\n\nThe sessions are open to all and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to scientists and forecasters. Registration via Zoom is required.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Users should be able to use the Data Store through EUMDAC service to browse, search for, download and eventually subscribe to, collections and products of interest;\nUsers should be able to use the Data Tailor Web Service through EUMDAC service to customise products to create bespoke data streams that fit their applications;\nUsers should know where to find resources to support the use of the new data access services;\nUsers should know where to go for further help in using the new data access services.","summary":"This series of 30-minute sessions is designed to support the transition from the EUMETSAT Data Centre in favour of the Data Store. This particular session focuses on using the EUME","content":null,"targetAudience":"Anyone interested in using the new EUMETSAT Data Access Services, especially users of the Data Centre.","registrationHowto":"Register for this webinar: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_PJDxu5mYRUSTJGPk6tIrRw","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email"