[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":4,"title":"Atmospheric composition","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2242,"created":1673281477000,"updated":1696575433000,"title":"ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbed on Severe Convective Storms","titleNative":"ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbed on Severe Convective Storms","startDate":1685923200000,"endDate":1686268800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Wiener Neustadt","host":"ESSL","contactPerson":"Stephan Bojinski","contactEmail":"Stephan.Bojinski@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"Testbeds are organized both to teach forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or even experimental forecasting tools, and to evaluate such tools. At these events forecasters will learn by doing, by making severe weather forecasts and nowcasts using a mix of new nowcasting and forecasting tools, followed by a verification. Particular focus will be given to the use of proxy-MTG satellite data (e.g., from the NASA MODIS, NOAA VIIRS and Sentinel-3/OLCI instruments) and, once operationally available, real data from MTG in order to prepare forecasters for the use of next-generation satellite data. In addition, a range of state-of-the-art nowcasting tools and numerical weather prediction-based guidance (i.e. from DWD, ZAMG, ESSL, and ECMWF) will be evaluated.","country":{"id":"AT","name":"AUSTRIA","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"Enhancing skills in forecasting severe convective storms.\nEvaluating different products for convection nowcasting, including satellite products. ","summary":"Testbeds are organized both to teach forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or even experimental forecasting tools, and to evaluate such tools. At these events","content":null,"targetAudience":"Weather forecasters from EUMETSAT Member States who train other forecasters and forecasters with a strong background in severe s