436 ","description":"This session will focus on how to use the latest new features of EUMETView and is scheduled on:\n\nThursday 26 January 2023, 13:00-14:00 UTC\n\nThis is part of a monthly series of open, online short sessions on the Data Access Services and their usage via GUIs and APIs, answering top questions posted by participants in an open sli.do (event code #data-services, link: https://app.sli.do/event/flsdivtc).\n\nWe encourage you to post your questions in sli.do now so that we can adapt the content of the next session to answer them.\n\nThe sessions are open to all and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to scientists and forecasters. Each session requires registration via Zoom. A great opportunity to remove any technical challenges in using the Data Access Services GUIs and APIs, and engage users in discussion with EUMETSAT experts and colleagues.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"- Users should be able to use the Data Store service to browse, search for, download and eventually subscribe to, collections and products of interest using both GUI and API, as required.\n- Users should be able to use the Data Tailor Web Service to customise products to create bespoke data streams that fit their applications, via both GUI and API, as required.\n- Users should be able to use the Data Tailor Web Service to customise products to mirror existing processing chains that rely on EUMETCast and/or the Data Centre.\n- Users should be able to use the EUMETView OMS GUI to: i) discover layers, ii) craft their own views, iii) manipulate and share views, iv) export data from layers as time series, images and animations, and v) link layers to Data Store collections where appropriate.\n- Users should be able to use the EUMETView OMS API to i) discover capabilities, ii) exploit the WMS/WCS/WFS endpoints to construct customised data requests.\n- Users should know where to find resources to support the use of the new data access services.\n- Users should know where to go for further help in using the new data access services.","summary":"This session will focus on how to use the latest new features of EUMETView and is scheduled on:\n\nThursday 26 January 2023, 13:00-14:00 UTC\n\nThis is part of a monthly series of open","content":null,"targetAudience":"Anyone interested in using the new EUMETSAT Data Access Services, especially users of the Data Centre, CODA/CODAREP (and possibly also EUMETCast).","registrationHowto":"You can register at this link: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/1116699895119/WN_4gbEjAIBS92yC2RD1UtoCA ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":28,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2251,"created":1674172669000,"updated":1698908058000,"title":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean ","titleNative":"Regional Focus Group of the Americas and the Caribbean ","startDate":1674662400000,"endDate":1674666000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"Bernie.Connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://rammb2.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup/","description":"The sessions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). Session time is 16:00 UTC; it will start 10 minutes earlier (15:50 UTC) for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection. GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet. \n\nSatellite imagery shown during the session is from SLIDER: http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/1124704066734948446 ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2239,"created":1671733404000,"updated":1698908034000,"title":"Connecting Citizen Science with Remote Sensing","titleNative":"Connecting Citizen Science with Remote Sensing","startDate":1674572400000,"endDate":1675197000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NASA’s Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET) ","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3TPfrtT","description":"Citizen science projects provide the opportunity for community member volunteers to monitor the Earth. These projects have dramatically increased in the last decade and can provide great benefits to projects that combine Earth Observations with ground-based information in the fields of climate change, sustainable development, ecosystem monitoring and characterization, drought, and land cover or land-use change.\n\nThis training will provide attendees an overview of citizen science efforts that use Earth Observations, and how to engage with community members in a supportive and meaningful manner to achieve project goals. Attendees will also be provided with case-study examples of successful citizen science projects, with some examples from NASA supported projects and activities. We will highlight projects like NeMO-Net, a global coral reef classification with 2D and 3D images application combined with machine learning; Floating Forests, a Giant Kelp monitoring platform where participants can classify kelp in Landsat images; Snapshot Wisconsin, a project that uses images of wildlife from trail cameras to assist with habitat mapping; and Soundscapes to Landscapes, where bird diversity in California is monitored by identifying specific species through sound recordings. We will also highlight the Global Learning and Observations to Benefit the Environment (GLOBE) Program, an international science and education program, and GLOBE Observer, a citizen science app that allows volunteers in GLOBE countries to take observations and contribute to the community.\n  \nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \n•\tGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts \n•\tGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\n•\tGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss \n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\n•\tSummarize applications of Earth Observations for citizen science \n•\tOutline key aspects of citizen science projects including community engagement, quality assurance of data collection, cataloging and summarizing data, and effective communication of project results\n•\tDiscover case study examples of the use of Earth Observations for NASA Applied Sciences projects\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Local, regional, state, federal, and non-governmental organizations involved in land management, conservation, biodiversity, and forest monitoring. Educators and scientists interested in involvement with citizen science would also be interested in this training topic.","registrationHowto":"Times and Registration Information:  \n  \nEnglish Session: 11:00-12:30 EST (UTC-5): https://go.nasa.gov/3TPfrtT\n\nSpanish Session: 14:00-15:30 EST (UTC-5): https://go.nasa.gov/3GTuPm4\n","registrationEndDate":1675195200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":110,"value":"NASA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2212,"created":1668690720000,"updated":1698908014000,"title":"NUMERICAL WEATHER & CLIMATE PREDICTIONS WITH MACHINE LEARNING","titleNative":"NUMERICAL WEATHER & CLIMATE PREDICTIONS WITH MACHINE LEARNING","startDate":1673222400000,"endDate":null,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"ECMWF","contactPerson":"Chris Stewart (ECMWF)","contactEmail":"Chris.Stewart@ecmwf.int","contactUrl":"https://lms.ecmwf.int/pages/index.html","description":"In this Massive Open Online Course (MOOC) you will explore the application of Machine Learning – a branch of Artificial Intelligence (AI) – across the main stages of numerical weather and climate predictions. These range from the acquisition and handling of input observations to their assimilation into models, and finally to forecasting and post-processing. In each element you will uncover both the added value and limitations of Machine Learning.\n\nThroughout your journey you will have the chance meet and interact with leading experts across the communities of Earth system sciences, high-performance computing and Machine Learning from around the world. By the end, you will have a sound understanding of the capabilities of Machine Learning in weather and climate and gain actual experience in applying advanced techniques.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"It is a fully online and FREE training open to anyone interested in the subject. The first part of the course is a high level introduction to the key concepts. Later sections become more technical and provide hands-on experience in the application of Machine Learning algorithms.","registrationHowto":"https://lms.ecmwf.int/pages/index.html","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":18,"name":"Winifred Jordaan","email":"winifred.jordaan@weathersa.co.za","agency":"SAWS","country":"ZA","programme":"VLab","trainingPartner":{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2216,"created":1669383174000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1671541200000,"endDate":1671544800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"Marko Blaskovic","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeT