llite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2183,"created":1660628000000,"updated":1660630622000,"title":"Australian VLab CoE Regional Focus Group meeting, August 2022","titleNative":"Australian VLab CoE Regional Focus Group meeting, August 2022","startDate":1660788000000,"endDate":1660791600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Melbourne","host":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence","contactPerson":"Bodo Zeschke","contactEmail":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","contactUrl":"http://www.virtuallab.bom.gov.au/","description":"The next Australian VLab CoE Regional Focus Group meeting is scheduled for 02UTC 18th August 2022\nThe topics of discussion are as follows:\nThe significant Swell Event in the Pacific Ocean during 11-17th July, as monitored by satellite and surface observations and NWP data (facilitator Mr Bodo Zeschke, Australian Bureau of Meteorology Training Centre)\nAltimetric wave heights and how to view them efficiently (facilitator Mr Scott Lindstrom, SSEC University of Wisconsin-Madison)","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"weather forecasters from RA V","registrationHowto":"We will be using Microsoft Teams Web Conferencing Software and the link to register is at https://teams.microsoft.com/registration/tX2t0d2XK0-BblDWY7e7lA,5WkBI5KjK0C45Ws29gOHyw,EEJWHDkhGUGkD5jhFcu4MQ,R_53aVNcQECGGYs_828g_g,ikHx3icCfk6O832awvhGzA,8WqQhe5z5EapTGEE-Tblcw?mode=read&tenantId=d1ad7db5-97dd-4f2b-816e-50d663b7bb94  ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5860,"name":"Bodo Zeschke","email":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","agency":"","country":"AU","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":5,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Australia"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":5,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Australia","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2165,"created":1654883202000,"updated":1661968740000,"title":"RFG of the Americas and the Caribbean - 15:00 UTC","titleNative":"RFG of the Americas and the Caribbean - 15:00 UTC","startDate":1660748400000,"endDate":1660752000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"Bernie.Connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"http://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup.asp","description":"The sessions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean). Session time is 15:00 UTC, but as usual, it will start 10 minutes earlier (14:50 UTC) for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection. GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet. \n\nSatellite imagery shown during the session is from SLIDER: http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu \nMore information can be found on our webpage: http://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup.asp","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/4796734249030479887","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2174,"created":1658243299000,"updated":1658244395000,"title":"Satellite Remote Sensing for Measuring Urban Heat Islands and Constructing Heat Vulnerability Indices","titleNative":"Satellite Remote Sensing for Measuring Urban Heat Islands and Constructing Heat Vulnerability Indices","startDate":1659438000000,"endDate":1660244400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3mGJLsq","description":"Structures such as buildings, roads, and other infrastructure absorb and re-emit the sun’s heat more than natural landscapes such as forests and water bodies. Urban areas, where these structures are highly concentrated and greenery is limited, become “islands” of higher temperatures relative to outlying areas. These pockets of heat are referred to as “heat islands.” The rapid growth of urban populations, the urban heat island effect, and increase in the frequency and duration of heat waves due to climate change, raise a series of issues about the increased health risks of sensitive urban populations to extreme heat and the effective means of mitigating the impacts of heat waves. \n\nRemote sensing provides global, timely, objective observations to monitor the effects of urban heat islands (UHI) over time. Thermal mapping from satellites can be used to monitor land surface temperature (LST), while optical data collected from satellites can inform where and when land use and land cover have changed over time and can be used to approximate air temperatures. Once UHIs have been mapped, incorporating socioeconomic data pertaining to population, demographics, and health information into heat vulnerability indices (HVI) can help guide interventions to manage heat related risks to public health. This four-part, advanced webinar will build on ARSET’s urban heat island training held in November 2020 with hands-on exercises for participants to measure UHI and construct HVIs for their areas of interest.\n\nThis webinar series is a collaboration between NASA’s Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET) and the National Integrated Heat Health Information System (NIHHIS).\n\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \n•\tGoal 11: Make cities and human settlements inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable\n\nCourse Dates: August 2, 4, 9, & 11, 2022\n\nTimes: 11:00-12:30 or 15:00-16:30 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, participants will be able to:\n•\tDefine what an urban heat island is and why it matters to urban planners and public health experts\n•\tMap land surface temperature from Landsat 4, 5, 7, 8, & 9 using Google Earth Engine and source complementary urban heat measurements\n•\tEngage with local decision-makers to identify the socioeconomic inputs appropriate for constructing heat vulnerability indices\n•\tApply common techniques used to create heat vulnerability maps for an urban area of interest\n","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Community, municipal, city, state, federal, and private institutions involved in urban planning, health care, energy supply and demand management, and climate mitigation.","registrationHowto":"Register Here: https://go.nasa.gov/3mGJLsq\n\nTimes: 11:00-12:30 or 15:00-16:30 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.","registrationEndDate":1660089600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"