egion":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/8936745172304696080","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5874,"name":"Erin Sanders","email":"Erin.Sanders@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":30,"value":"CIRA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":3,"name":"VLAB"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2200,"created":1665054206000,"updated":1700666029000,"title":"IOCCG Summer Lecture Series","titleNative":"IOCCG Summer Lecture Series","startDate":1658102400000,"endDate":1659052800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Villefrance-Sur-mer","host":null,"contactPerson":null,"contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":null,"country":{"id":"FR","name":"FRANCE","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":null,"summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":null,"registrationHowto":null,"registrationEndDate":null,"fees":null,"awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":24,"name":"Partners"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":null,"attendeeCount":6,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":6,"title":"Marine","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":12,"title":"Ocean biogeochemistry","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":14,"title":"Water quality","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":20,"name":"WEkEO"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":11,"name":"Data Store"}],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":8,"name":"Sentinel-3"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":1,"name":"Metop"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2159,"created":1653085020000,"updated":1653159860000,"title":"Monitoring Aquatic Vegetation with Remote Sensing","titleNative":"Monitoring Aquatic Vegetation with Remote Sensing","startDate":1657645200000,"endDate":1658266200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3LtoVaT","description":"Aquatic vegetation (AV) provides a habitat for numerous small invertebrate and fish species. Kelp forests, for example, are the dominant coastal ecosystem in temperate waters. The presence of AV in coastal waters is a normal occurrence, as it is an important component of the natural marine environment. Nevertheless, in the last decades, the appearance of increasingly abundant mats of certain floating species in other coastlines (i.e., Caribbean/Atlantic) has become a nuisance to local economies.\r\n\r\nSargassum mats, for example, are reaching coastal areas by tons every year and there is still not a clear hypothesis as to why this is happening. While these mats provide an important habitat for small invertebrate and fish species, they also affect the amount of light reaching shallow-water ecosystems like coral reefs and seagrass beds. Several NASA-funded efforts are currently using remote sensing techniques to monitor the presence of Sargassum in Caribbean and Atlantic waters, while others have a citizen science-oriented approach to follow giant kelp populations in the Pacific.\r\n\r\nThis training will combine basic information on the remote sensing of AVs, spectrometry of aquatic/coastal vegetation, and a demonstration of the NASA-funded Floating Forests citizen science tool.\r\n  \r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\tGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts \r\n\tGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\r\n\tGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss \r\n\r\nCourse Dates: July 12, 14, & 19, 2022\r\n \r\nTimes and Registration Information:  \r\n  \r\nEnglish Session: 11:00-12:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3LtoVaT \r\nSpanish Session: 14:00-15:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/38JIcWX\r\n \r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\r\n\tDistinguish some types of submerged and floating aquatic flora\r\n\tSummarize the major features of AV spectral signatures\r\n\tRecognize the primary satellite sensors used for studying AV\r\n\tBecome familiarized with a citizen science (Floating Forests) tool for classifying kelp forests in the west coast of the United States\r\n\tDiscover remotely-sensed and in situ tools to study the presence of seaweed in the Atlantic/Caribbean including the Sargassum Watch System (SaWS) \r\n","summary":null,"content":"Three, 1.5 hour parts","targetAudience":"This webinar series is intended for local, regional, state, federal, and non-governmental organizations involved in land and coastal ecosystems management. Applied scientists and GIS professionals interested in learning how to assess the presence of aquatic vegetation independently. ","registrationHowto":"Please register here: \r\n\r\nEnglish Session: 11:00-12:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3LtoVaT \r\nSpanish Session: 14:00-15:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/38JIcWX","registrationEndDate":1658196000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2168,"created":1655712444000,"updated":1657547353000,"title":"International training on remote sensing and geospatial analyses for human health","titleNative":"International training on remote sensing and geospatial analyses for human health","startDate":1657548000000,"endDate":1659106800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":null,"host":"ONWARD project","contactPerson":"Shubha Sathyendranath ","contactEmail":"onward.gcrf@gmail.com","contactUrl":"https://onwardnetwork.net/training_new","description":"Climate variability and change, occurrences of extreme weather events and anthropogenic pressures on the environment, all contribute to growth and dispersal of pathogens, leading to outbreaks of water associated diseases such as cholera, malaria, dengue, among others. The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) dealing with health, climate, and life below water recommends an integrated approach to maintain high water quality. From monitoring the water bodies via satellite remote sensing to new techniques for identifying and isolating pathogens, there has been much advancement in our capability to monitor water quality.\n\nWith this course, we aim to create a link among the international researchers and experts on the advanced satellite remote sensing and geospatial techniques for water quality monitoring and generate highly skilled manpower in the area. The lectures will be offered by experts who are highly experienced in the subject. You will leave this course with a clear understanding of why and how to use EO data to assess the water quality.\n\nThe training is being organised in three modules:\n\n1. Module 1 (11-15 July2022):\nIntroduction to remote sensing, in collaboration with EUMETSAT - led by Dr. Hayley Evers King\n2. Module 2 (18-22 July 2022):\nGeospatial data and analyses - INPE, Brazil - led by Dr. Milton Kampel & Dr. Philipe Riskalla Leal\n3. Module 3 (25-29 July2022):\nCase studies on Earth Observation for Human Health -INPE, Brazil - led by Dr. Milton Kampel & Dr.Shubha Sathyendranath\n\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"- Awareness of the variety EO data available for water quality applications.\n- Understanding of the different ways to access and work with relevant EO data for water quality. ","summary":null,"content":null,"targetAudience":"Post graduate students and early career researchers (including master’s and doctoral students) and post-doctoral scientists, and public health experts who have a basic knowledge of remote sensing/geospatial data analyses..\n\n200 participants will be allowed to attend the online tutorial lecture series, on a first come first serve basis.","registrationHowto":"\nInterested candidates may apply for the training programme by filling the form given here https://forms.gle/aka2dSRgk4UmJDvF8.","registrationEndDate":1656892800000,"fees":null,"awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5850,"name":"Hayley Evers-King","email":"Hayley.EversKing@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"ONLINE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2097,"created":1637086078000,"updated":null,"title":"2nd ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbed on Severe Convective Storms 2022","titleNative":"2nd ESSL-EUMETSAT Testbed on Severe Convective Storms 2022","startDate":1657497600000,"endDate":1657843200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Wiener Neustadt","host":"ESSL","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"ESSL and EUMETSAT organise a series of testbeds to prepare forecasters for data from next-generation Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) and Metop Second Generation (Metop-SG) satellites. These testbeds are organized both to teach forecasting severe convective storms using state-of-the-art or even experimental forecasting tools, and to evaluate such tools. At these events we will learn by doing, by making experimental severe weather forecasts and nowcasts using a mix of new nowcasting and forecasting tools, followed by a verification. Particular focus will be given to the capabilities of the VIIRS instrument on polar-orbiting satellites, which serves as a proxy for MTG's FCI instrument, and other proxy data for MTG instruments. In addition, a range of state-of-the-art nowcasting tools and numerical weather prediction-based guidance from DWD, ZAMG, ESSL, and ECMWF will be evaluated.","country":{"id":"AT","name":"AUSTRIA","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Forecasters who train other forecasters and forecasters with a strong background in severe storms meteorology. Have a look at this self-evaluation before applying: https://www.surveymonkey.com/r/D3B3PBM. \r\n\r\nCandidates will also need to have a nomination by their home institution. \r\n\r\nFor accepted candidates, EUMETSAT plans to sponsor the cost of testbed attendance and, upon request, the cost for travel, accommodation and subsistence.","registrationHowto":"In order to apply, go to https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158. Make sure you read all instructions and fill in the application form. Please note that you will need to register before applying, if you do not already have an account. ","registrationEndDate":1645747200000,"fees":"","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2167,"created":1655284715000,"updated":1700665291000,"title":"EUMETSAT series of short courses: Wildfire Monitoring with Next-Generation Satellites","titleNative":"EUMETSAT series of short courses: Wildfire Monitoring with Next-Generation Satellites","startDate":1657112400000,"endDate":1657117800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"null","contactPerson":"Federico Fierli ","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=461","description":"This webinar is part of the EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.\r\n\r\nIn the near future, new satellites such as Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) and Metop - Second Generation (Metop-SG) will provide advanced capabilities and valuable data for monitoring fires and their impacts. This short course showcases a series of case studies and associated workflows in Jupyter notebooks for fire applications using proxy data from MTG and Metop-SG. Join us to explore the case studies focusing on the August Complex fires in 2020, California USA and the Mediterranean fires in summer 2021! \r\n\r\nEach case study focuses on a specific past fire event to demonstrate how data from MTG and Metop-SG can be used for operational and monitoring purposes. Different stages of the fire life cycle are highlighted: from pre-fire risk, active burning and smoke transport to post-fire burnt area assessment. Each figure shown in the case study is linked to a Jupyter notebook that demonstrates in an instructional, step-by-step workflow how the figure was created and which proxy data was used. ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Participants will have the opportunity to learn: \r\n- which are the EUMETSAT and Copernicus free and open data.\r\n- where to access the data and where to find information about the data.\r\n- how to handle the data using the tools proposed.","summary":"This webinar is part of the EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.\r\n\r\nIn the near future, new satellites such","content":"null","targetAudience":"The courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to  scientists and forecasters. ","registrationHowto":null,"registrationEndDate":null,"fees":null,"awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5847,"name":"Christine Traeger Chatterjee","email":"christi