ll,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2106,"created":1637274156000,"updated":1654002546000,"title":"Marine Weather Forecasting - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"Marine Weather Forecasting - Series of online short courses","startDate":1654696800000,"endDate":1654704000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=442","description":"Satellite data products are relevant tools for monitoring marine weather and sea state conditions, as situ observations over ocean are scarce. Without satellite data, in essence, marine forecasters are left to rely solely on model data. In this course, you will learn through practical examples how to better assess current sea and marine weather conditions, and the accuracy of NWP forecasts, with the aid of scatterometer and altimeter data.\r\n\r\nThis course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"Satellite data products are relevant tools for monitoring marine weather and sea state conditions, as situ observations over ocean are scarce. Without satellite data, in essence, m","content":"","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar at the course page: https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=442.\r\n\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2220,"created":1669737171000,"updated":null,"title":"Shaping the future - EUMETSAT support to Climate Services","titleNative":"Shaping the future - EUMETSAT support to Climate Services","startDate":1654678800000,"endDate":1654819200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Darmstadt","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Christine Träger","contactEmail":"Christine.Traeger@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"The aim of this workshop is to provide you, as a Climate Information/Service provider, a forum to talk to EUMETSAT about your work, share challenges and discuss potential needs for observations from space and of associated services. Further, EUMETSAT will offer an overview on its support to Climate Services today and its possible evolution. Your participation and feedback is an opportunity to influence the medium-term evolution of this support.\n\nThe three-day workshop will feature invited talks by climate services and breakout group discussions. The focus themes of the breakout groups will be defined together with the participants at the second day of the workshop","country":null,"learningOutcome":"After the workshop participants should\n\nRecall that EUMETSAT and others provide many CDRs of ECVs and the will know where to access the catalogue.\nBe able to discuss the potential of satellite based data for climate applications in the context of other data ( surface, model, societal)\nKnow colleagues (other users as well as data providers) to contact to discuss issues around the applications of satellite data in climate applications and have seen perspectives from different people in the system / chain. \nIdeally: actively participate in a pathfinder project identified during the workshop.","summary":"The aim of this workshop is to provide you, as a Climate Information/Service provider, a forum to talk to EUMETSAT about your work, share challenges and discuss potential needs for","content":null,"targetAudience":"Representatives of EUMETSAT Member State Climate Units. Participants should have a good overview on the tasks and challenges of their climate services. They do NOT need have satellite expertise. ","registrationHowto":null,"registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5841,"name":"Regina Hoefenmayer","email":"regina.hoefenmayer@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"BLENDED","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2156,"created":1652122224000,"updated":1652159141000,"title":"Applications of Remote Sensing-Based Evapotranspiration Data Products for Agricultural and Water Resource Management","titleNative":"Applications of Remote Sensing-Based Evapotranspiration Data Products for Agricultural and Water Resource Management","startDate":1654095600000,"endDate":1655328600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3rvZyx2","description":"Evapotranspiration (ET) is the process by which the land surface returns water to the atmosphere in the form of moisture. ET is a very important part of the water cycle in the Earth system. It is the sum of evaporation from bare soil and transpiration from vegetation. For a given watershed, the supply of water from precipitation, surface and groundwater can be depleted via ET. Therefore, estimating the amount of ET is crucial for calculating the overall water budget and for effective water management. Since ET indicates loss of moisture from the soil and vegetation, monitoring ET on agricultural fields helps with crop irrigation activities and water conservation.\r\n\r\nBecause ET depends on land surface characteristics such as type of vegetation and soil moisture, available heat energy from sunlight, and atmospheric weather conditions; it is not possible to measure ET directly. Remote sensing observations provide several of the land and atmospheric parameters useful for estimating ET. Several methodologies have been developed using various satellite observations to estimate ET over the past two decades. These ET products are used for a variety of applications from the farm scale to the watershed scale.\r\n\r\nThis 3-part webinar series focuses on introducing newly available ET products derived from remote sensing observations. It will specifically cover a web portal called OpenET (https://openetdata.org/), which includes ET products estimated by using six models as well as Landsat satellite observations. These ET products cover the western United States. In addition, information about global ET products derived from ECOsystem Spaceborne Thermal Radiometer Experiment on the Space Station (ECOSTRESS) will also be covered. The webinar series will provide details about OpenET and ECOSTRESS ET products, demonstrations and hands-on exercises for data access and analysis, and examples of applications of the data.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\tGoal 2: End hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition and promote sustainable agriculture\r\n\tTarget 2.4: By 2030, ensure sustainable food production systems and implement resilient agricultural practices that increase productivity and production, that help maintain ecosystems, that strengthen capacity for adaptation to climate change, extreme weather, drought, flooding and other disasters and that progressively improve land and soil quality\r\n\tTarget 6.4: By 2030, substantially increase water-use efficiency across all sectors and ensure sustainable withdrawals and supply of freshwater to address water scarcity and substantially reduce the number of people suffering from water scarcity \r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\r\n\tIdentify state-of-the-art techniques to derive evapotranspiration (ET) using remote sensing\r\n\tRecognize how ET data can be used in water resources and agricultural management\r\n\tAccess OpenET and ECOSTRESS ET data products for their own applications\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Three, 90-minute parts","targetAudience":"This webinar series is intended for local, regional, federal, and non-governmental organizations from agriculture and water resources related agencies involved in water resources and agricultural management.","registrationHowto":"Times: 11:00-12:30 or 16:00-17:30 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\r\n\r\nRegister Here: https://go.nasa.gov/3rvZyx2\r\n","registrationEndDate":1655251200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2219,"created":1669726119000,"updated":null,"title":"New Programme - User days","titleNative":"New Programme - User days","startDate":1653901200000,"endDate":1654102800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Darmstadt","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Stephan Bojinski","contactEmail":"Stephan.Bojinski@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":null,"description":"EUMETSAT is planning the User Days on its next-generation satellite programmes Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) and EUMETSAT Polar System Second Generation (EPS-SG) as an in-person event. With the first MTG imaging satellite due for launch in late 2022, operational services need to ascertain their readiness to access and process data products and learn how to most effectively use them.\n\nFurther, over the coming five years, MTG and EPS-SG will deliver novel data with massive potential for advancing weather, climate and Earth system research, as well as to enhance operational forecasting. Several of the missions are new for the European user community, such as the geostationary lightning and infrared sounding missions, or the ice-cloud imaging mission in polar orbit. \n\nThe three-day event is planned to be dominated by panel discussions, technical exhibitions, exchange of knowledge and experience, hands-on and brainstorming sessions among participants. This will be facilitated by roundtable-type exchange among participants on the themes identified in the agenda and in the pre-event survey questionnaire.","country":{"id":"DE","name":"GERMANY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"EUMETSAT is planning the User Days on its next-generation satellite programmes Meteosat Third Generation (MTG) and EUMETSAT Polar System Second Generation (EPS-SG) as an in-person ","content":null,"targetAudience":"We have invited 220 representatives from: \nOperational user community \nR&D/academic community experienced in using satellite data, focus on Europe \nResearch funding bodies \nPartner space agencies \nCopernicus services \nProviders/manufacturers of processing and display SW/HW\nWMO RA VI\nNorth Africa NMHS\nEUMETSAT Secretariat and SAFs","registrationHowto":"Invitation letter.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5841,"name":"Regina Hoefenmayer","email":"regina.hoefenmayer@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":null,"format":"RESIDENCE","attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2131,"created":1645023567000,"updated":1647867176000,"title":"Supporting Marine Earth Observation Educators Workshop","titleNative":"Supporting Marine Earth Observation Educators Workshop","startDate":1653894000000,"endDate":1655485200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"IOC/IODE/OTGA & EUMETSAT & Mercator International","contactPerson":"IOC/IODE/OTGA","contactEmail":"ioc.training@unesco.org","contactUrl":"https://oceanexpert.org/event/3405","description":"The purpose of this training workshop is to share knowledge, skills, resources and training techniques so that attendees are able to independently train others on available Earth Observation (EO) data and tools. During the course, participants will work with others to create their own (learning) artefacts. This is an interactive knowledge exchange workshop, not a technical training event.\r\n\r\nAims and objectives:\r\n* To support marine and earth observation educators in their training activities, by sharing Copernicus Marine data resources and exchanging experience of different learning approaches.\r\n* To foster a community of marine EO educators who can collaborate in future.\r\n* To co-create learning artefacts that can used in participants own training activities and more broadly by the Earth observation community.\r\n\r\nTrainers:\r\n* Ben Loveday (EUMETSAT/Innoflair UG)\r\n* Aida Alvera-Azcárate (EUMETSAT/GHER University of Liege)\r\n* Ana Ruescas (EUMETSAT/Brockmann Consult GmbH/University of Valencia)\r\n* Hayley Evers-King (EUMETSAT)\r\n* Fabrice Messal (CMEMS/Mercator Ocean)\r\n* Greg Reed (IOC/IODE)","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"At the end of the workshop, participants should be able to/will have the skills to:\r\n* Train on the use of Copernicus Sentinel-3 and CMEMS data for marine applications, covering the use of sea surface temperature, altimetry and ocean colour data, as well as downstream products and model outputs.\r\n* Show others how to access the Copernicus Marine Data through online portals, APIs and other methods.\r\n* Explain the concepts of data quality (including basic principles of validation) and selection of appropriate products when helping trainees design workflows.\r\n* Understand the use of the following tools in training  Web portals, SNAP, Git, Python & Jupyter notebooks, cloud computing (WEkEO).\r\n* Select and adapt existing resources and develop new resources for their own training activities.\r\n* Select the appropriate training methods for introducing these resources in both face-to-face and online learning interventions.","summary":null,"content":"Workshop duration and format:\r\n\r\nThis is an online event with asynchronous, self-paced activities and synchronous sessions. The estimated workload should be 5-10 hours/week, up to 25 hours in total across the 3 weeks, to be completed by 17 June 2022.\r\n\r\nOnline, synchronous discussions sessions are planned twice a week. These will take place at 08:00 and 17:00 CET/UTC+2 (with a maximum duration of 2 hours each) to accommodate the different timezones of the participants. Participants need only attend one time zone. In addition, it will be possible to book 1-1 sessions with the trainers, throughout.\r\n\r\nThe workshop is designed to be flexible, to support the needs of a diverse range of participants. Workshop organisers will be happy to discuss any accommodations needed.\r\n\r\nCertification:\r\nParticipants will be expected to develop a mini project as well as contribute to live sessions and asynchronous activities. Two types of Certificates will be issued:\r\n\r\n* Certificate of participation (participants will need to):\r\n  > Have attended minimum 80% of live sessions (or watched recording if couldnt attend)\r\n  > Worked through minimum 90% of course materials\r\n*Certificate of achievement (besides the above, participants will need to):\r\n  > Deliver a presentation/artefact at the end of the course","targetAudience":"This workshop is intended for university teachers, mentors and tutors, as well as staff from National Oceanographic Data Centres, marine research/management institutions and industry professionals who have Earth-observation or marine science related training responsibilities.\r\n\r\nLanguages:\r\nPredominantly English, with some support in Spanish and French.\r\n\r\nPrerequisites:\r\nApplicants must currently (or in very near future) have training or teaching responsibilities in marine science, Earth observation or both; e.g. a university lecturer, a team manager responsible for in-house training, a research supervisor, a freelance consultant who runs customised training courses. A good working knowledge of English is needed for the discussion sessions.","registrationHowto":"To ensure an interactive experience, a limited number of places are available. Please complete the online application form available on https://otga.wufoo.com/forms/zlxwse11skncwe/ or https://bit.ly/34MFv4O\r\n\r\nThe deadline to submit the application is 3 April 2022 (23:59 CET: Central European Time).\r\n\r\nNote: the organisers are committed to promote equal access principles. Applications from minority or under-represented groups are strongly encouraged.\r\n\r\nFor questions please contact the OTGA Secretariat (ioc.training@unesco.org) and/or copernicus.training@eumetsat.int always using the name of the course as email subject.","registrationEndDate":1649026740000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5877,"name":"Ben Loveday","email":"Ben.Loveday@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2160,"created":1653321270000,"updated":null,"title":"100th Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting","titleNative":"100th Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting","startDate":1653624000000,"endDate":1653627600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Melbourne","host":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence","contactPerson":"Bodo Zeschke","contactEmail":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","contactUrl":"https://learn.bom.gov.au/mod/bigbluebuttonbn/view.php?id=13874","description":"The VLab in Asia Oceania on the occasion of the 100th Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting: learning from the past, understanding the present and expectations for the future","country":{"id":"AU","name":"AUSTRALIA","region":"RA-V","type":"Rest of the World"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"1: Initial presentations by the Manager of the Australian CoE, JMA and other stakeholders. 2: A brief summary of the past 100 Australian VLab CoE RFG meetings, with highlights. 3: Looking ahead: what might the 200th RFG meeting look like?\r\nHow to apply: For this session we will be using the Big Blue Button web conferencing system on our training site.","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"For this session we will be using the Big Blue Button web conferencing system on our training site.\r\nTo register please follow the instructions below:\r\n1.\tThe session homepage is at https://learn.bom.gov.au/mod/bigbluebuttonbn/view.php?id=13874 \r\n2.\tYou can join the session either as a guest or as a registered user. We would prefer you to join as a registered user. There is no cost associated with this. \r\nA.\tFor guest users the session password is VLab@2020\r\nB.\tFor users who choose to join the session as a registered user for the first time, please use the link https://learn.bom.gov.au/login/signup.php to create an account. Please include your details including Country of Origin. After signing up you will receive an automated email with a link to confirm your registration within about 15 minutes. If you have not received the confirmation email within this time, check your spam/junk inbox. If there is no confirmation within your spam/junk inbox, contact moodle@bom.gov.au giving their name and email address, stating they had not received their site registration confirmation email for our training site.\r\nC.\tFor users who have already registered, please click \"Continue\" on the Self enrolment (Student) option. Log in using the above Username and Password. If you have forgotten the password then you can use the 'forgot password' function: https://learn.bom.gov.au/login/forgot_password.php\r\nD.\tAustralian Bureau of Meteorology staff please use the 'Bureau staff log in' button to log in to the site.\r\nPlease use either the Google Chrome or Modzilla Firefox web browsers when you join the session.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5857,"name":"Zoya Andreeva","email":"zandreeva@wmo.int","agency":"","country":"CH","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":29,"value":"WMO"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":20,"value":"JMA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2155,"created":1651733691000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1653480000000,"endDate":1653483600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data.\r\nMaintaining a healthy basis of knowledge about satellite meteorology.\r\nUnderstanding the difference between RGBs as well as their benefits and limitations.\r\n","summary":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imag","content":"Weather briefings are recorded. Find a collection of past briefings at http://eumetrain.org/briefings.html and on YouTube at https://www.youtube.com/user/eumetrain/videos.","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are forecasters in shifts, although other meteorologists from different areas are also welcome.","registrationHowto":"In order to join the discussion, please go to http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html. For any questions, please use the Contact email.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2153,"created":1651069837000,"updated":1651070644000,"title":"Measuring Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide from Space in Support of Climate Related Studies","titleNative":"Measuring Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide from Space in Support of Climate Related Studies","startDate":1653408000000,"endDate":1654203600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3KgDnRU","description":"CO2 monitoring from space is becoming an increasingly important and relevant capability in support of climate studies and to inform policy decisions. This introductory four-part webinar series will provide an overview of atmospheric carbon dioxide measurements from space with the OCO-2 and OCO-3 satellite missions. It will include a theoretical portion that will describe the instrument, how the measurement is made, and the characteristics, limitations and validation of the measurement. There will be a discussion of the type of climate studies that such measurements can support. There will also be a practical session where participants will learn how to access, search, filter and display XCO2 data using Jupyter Notebook.\r\n  \r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\tGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts\r\n \r\nCourse Dates: May 24, 26, 31, and June 2\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\r\n\tUnderstand the characteristics and limitations of CO2 measurements from space\r\n\tUnderstand the type of climate studies that these measurements can support\r\n\tBe able to open and visualize CO2 data from OCO-2\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Four, 2 hour parts","targetAudience":"This webinar series is intended for local, regional, federal, and non-governmental organizations from climate agencies to use CO2 remote sensing data in climate studies. ","registrationHowto":"Times and Registration Information:  \r\n  \r\nEnglish Session: 12:00-14:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3LgOnjL\r\nSpanish Session: 15:00-17:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3vnJeRz\r\n \r\n","registrationEndDate":1654196400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":110,"value":"NASA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2154,"created":1651076958000,"updated":1651077300000,"title":"Satellite Oceanography Products: Concepts, Access and Data Processing","titleNative":"Produtos de Oceanografia por Satélite: Conceitos, Acesso e Processamento","startDate":1653393600000,"endDate":1654021800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Cachoeira Paulista","host":"INPE (CGCT / DISSM / DIPTC)","contactPerson":"Diego Souza","contactEmail":"diego.souza@inpe.br","contactUrl":"https://moodle.cptec.inpe.br/course/view.php?id=21","description":"On May 24, 26 and 31, 2022 the VLab CoE Brazil will host the online course Satellite Oceanography Products: Concepts, Access and Data Processing. With a total duration of 15 hours, the course will address some of the satellite oceanography products available, passive and active sensors, among other concepts of remote sensing of the oceans and their applications. In the hands-on sessions, students will learn how to access and manipulate data from various satellites using the Python programming language. The course tutors are specialists from the Division of Satellites and Meteorological Sensors (DISSM), and the Division of Weather and Climate Forecasting (DIPTC) of INPE.","country":{"id":"BR","name":"BRAZIL","region":"RA-III","type":"Rest of the World"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to: \r\n- Understand the characteristics and limitations of ocean measurements from space.\r\n- Know the types of sensors and some of the products available and their applications in marine and meteorological services.\r\n- Be able to access, process and visualize products using the Python programming language. \r\n","summary":null,"content":"The course will include an overview of some of the available ocean products like sea surface temperature, chlorophyll-a concentration, altimetry products, sea surface winds and their applications in weather, climate and oceanographic monitoring. In the hands-on sessions students will learn how to access and process these products via scripts using Python (reading the metadata, adding maps, changing projections, subsect the imagery, add shapefiles, texts, create animations, overlays, perform time series analysis, compare satellite and in situ data among other operations). ","