":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Improved skills in: (a) Identifying key weather systems and features found in Africa and their characteristics in Satellite imagery and products; (b) using the information for nowcasting; (c) Being a competent user of virtual course technology; (d) Developing relationships with course participants via the discussion forums and online briefings.","summary":null,"content":"In terms of content, selected synoptic scale as well as mesoscale weather systems are addressed. The course addresses system/feature identification in satellite imagery and some aspects of nowcasting.","targetAudience":"Instructors in satellite meteorology, operational forecasters or experienced satellite data users with the ability to pass on skills acquired at the course to other operational staff.","registrationHowto":"Candidates are requested to apply to the online course by completing an online questionnaire. Applications open in March 2022 at <a href=\"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158\">http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158</a>.","registrationEndDate":1650589200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":33,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa","visible":false},{"id":8,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Kenya","visible":false},{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":29,"title":"Severe storms","identifier":"dataApplication"},{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":18,"title":"Weather","identifier":"dataApplication"}],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[{"eventMetadataGroup":{"id":1,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"id":2,"name":"MSG"}],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2130,"created":1644578711000,"updated":1646149603000,"title":"Cours enligne: RA-I Applications de la météorologie satellitaire pour de prévision immédiate 2022 ","titleNative":"Cours enligne: RA-I Applications de la météorologie satellitaire pour de prévision immédiate 2022 ","startDate":1651449600000,"endDate":1654214400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Carla Barroso","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"Ce cours sur les applications satellitaires pour les prévisions immédiates vous aidera à améliorer vos compétences en matière d'identification des principaux systèmes et caractéristiques météorologiques en Afrique, et à apprendre à utiliser l'imagerie et les produits satellitaires pour la surveillance et les prévisions immédiates (prévisions dans une plage de temps de 0 à 6 heures). Vous développerez des relations professionnelles avec les participants au cours et explorerez l'utilisation d'une variété d'applications web et mobiles pour communiquer et apprendre ensemble.   ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Amélioration des compétences en : (a) Identifier les principaux systèmes et caractéristiques météorologiques en Afrique et leurs caractéristiques dans l'imagerie et les produits satellitaires ; (b) l'utilisation des informations pour la prévision immédiate;(c) Être un utilisateur compétent de la technologie des cours virtuels ; (d) Développer des relations avec les participants aux cours via les forums de discussion et les briefings en ligne.","summary":null,"content":"Dans ce cours, certains situations météorologiques à échelle synoptique et à méso-échelle sont abordés. Le cours aborde l'identification des systèmes/fonctions sur les caractéristiques, et certains aspects de la prévision immédiate.","targetAudience":"Instructeurs en météorologie satellitaire, des prévisionnistes opérationnels ou des utilisateurs de données satellitaires, avec la possibilité de transmettre les compétences acquises lors du cours à d'autres membres du personnel opérationnel.","registrationHowto":"Les candidats sont invités à s'inscrire au cours en ligne en remplissant un questionnaire en ligne. Les candidatures sont ouvertes à mars 2021 à l'adresse suivante <a href=\"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158\">http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158</a>","registrationEndDate":1650661200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":4,"value":"French","iso639":"fr"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2139,"created":1647419408000,"updated":1647425756000,"title":"Course for the Nordic Operational Meteorologists on Meteorological Applications 2022 (NOMEK)","titleNative":"Course for the Nordic Operational Meteorologists on Meteorological Applications 2022 (NOMEK)","startDate":1650873600000,"endDate":1651154400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Oslo","host":"MET Norway","contactPerson":"Vibeke Kristensen","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"This course is intended for Nordic operational meteorologists and will be organised by MET Norway in collaboration with the Nordic meteorological institutes and with support from EUMETSAT. The course will consist of lessons and practical sessions, including real-time weather room exercises, demonstrations and case studies.","country":{"id":"NO","name":"NORWAY","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"- Skills in identifying high moisture flow leading to high precipitation.\r\n- Improved understanding of the communication between customers and authorities during high impact weather events. \r\n- Updated understanding of the available and future observation networks.\r\n- Improved use of NWP guidance in forecasting high precipitation. ","summary":null,"content":"NOMEK focuses on improving forecasting competencies. The main topics in this NOMEK course will be:\r\n\r\n- Investigating prolonged precipitation events through a recent high impact case over Norway; \r\n- The role of atmospheric rivers; \r\n- Discussion of impact based forecasting in the Nordic countries;\r\n- Communication of weather warnings with various user groups and authorities.","targetAudience":"Operational Meteorologists from Scandinavia, with invitations to forecasters from the Baltic States and Ireland.","registrationHowto":"Please contact your national point of contact for training for more information on the nomination and application process. ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":70,"value":"NHMS","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"},{"id":20,"value":"Other (see description)"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2148,"created":1648738566000,"updated":null,"title":"How to use the EUMETSAT Data Access Client - Data Access Services","titleNative":"How to use the EUMETSAT Data Access Client - Data Access Services","startDate":1650542400000,"endDate":1650542400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT User Helpdesk ","contactEmail":"OPS@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=436","description":"This session will focus on how to use the EUMETSAT Data Access Client (EUMDAC - link:https://eumetsatspace.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/EUMDAC/overview), and is scheduled on Thursday 21 April 2022, 12:00-13:00 UTC.\r\n\r\nThis is part of a monthly series of open, online short sessions on the Data Access Services and their usage via GUIs and APIs, answering top questions posted by participants in an open sli.do EXT (event code #data-services, link https://app.sli.do/event/flsdivtc).\r\n\r\nWe encourage you to post your questions in sli.do now so that we can adapt the content of the next session to answer them.\r\n\r\nThe sessions are open to all and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to scientists and forecasters. Each session requires registration via Zoom. A great opportunity to remove any technical challenges in using the Data Access Services GUIs and APIs, and engage users in discussion with EUMETSAT experts and colleagues.\r\n\r\nFuture sessions are tentatively scheduled for:\r\n\r\nThursday 12 May 2022, 12:00-13:00 UTC\r\n\r\nThursday 9 June 2022, 12:00-13:00 UTC","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"This session will focus on how to use the EUMETSAT Data Access Client (EUMDAC - link:https://eumetsatspace.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/EUMDAC/overview), and is scheduled on Thursday ","content":"","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"Please register at this link: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/9516487378299/WN_2EHI_Hu2ROWKbhCaVOIIMw ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5840,"name":"Rosa Ullucci","email":"rosa.ullucci@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2143,"created":1648008475000,"updated":1648017788000,"title":"RFG of the Americas and the Caribbean - 15:00 UTC","titleNative":"RFG of the Americas and the Caribbean - 15:00 UTC","startDate":1650466800000,"endDate":1650470400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"bernie.connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/4464570685001965072","description":"The sessions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean).  Session time is 15:00 UTC, but as usual, it will start 10 minutes earlier (at 14:50 UTC for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection).  GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet.  Images during the session are from SLIDER: http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu  More Information: http://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup.asp","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/4464570685001965072","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2111,"created":1639141202000,"updated":1649753953000,"title":"Fire Danger Near Real Time Monitoring in the Mediterranean region - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"Fire Danger Near Real Time Monitoring in the Mediterranean region - Series of online short courses","startDate":1650456000000,"endDate":1650463200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=448","description":"This course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.","country":null,"learningOutcome":"","summary":"This course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.","content":"The fire season over the Mediterranean region typically starts in May-June.  Meteorological factors play a crucial role in the setting and spreading of wildfires and they are also an important factor in the resulting fire severity. How to interpret the available satellite products and anticipate severe fire occurrence? In this short course, conducted by Carla Barroso, Federico Fierli (EUMETSAT) and Celia Gouveia (IPMA-LSA SAF), participants will go through:\r\n\r\n- an introduction about how satellite data are important in the fire monitoring process.\r\n\r\n- A demonstration on how the LSA SAF Fire Risk products can be used as a warning tool to extreme fire probability in the Mediterranean region.\r\n\r\n- Accessing and visualizing fire risk products from the LSA SAF using a demo on Jupyter notebooks. This is part of a wider workflow for wildfire monitoring and forecast developed by Sabrina Szeto and Julia Wagemann for EUMETSAT and Copernicus.","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar at the course page: https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=448.\r\n\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2136,"created":1646960654000,"updated":1646985159000,"title":"INTERNATIONAL DISTANCE TRAINING COURSE ON  THE BASIC PRINCIPLES OF SATELLITE REMOTE-SENSING","titleNative":"INTERNATIONAL DISTANCE TRAINING COURSE ON  THE BASIC PRINCIPLES OF SATELLITE REMOTE-SENSING","startDate":1650240000000,"endDate":null,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"China Meteorological Administration Training Centre (RTC-Beijing)","contactPerson":"Ms. DENG Jingmian, Ms. HAN Guolin","contactEmail":"applycmatc@cma.gov.cn","contactUrl":"","description":"The course is aimed to help freshmen in satellite meteorology to understand the concept of satellite remote-sensing, to enhance their basic knowledge in the interpretation of meteorological satellite data, and to improve their skills in  weather analysis and environmental monitoring. Trainees will be expected to improve their capabilities of data acquisition and assimilation, their ability to utilize the satellite monitoring system, as well as their proficiency in satellite remote-sensing application.","country":null,"learningOutcome":"By studying the online materials, participants will be able to: \r\n&#9642;apply the basic theories and principles of radiation to the interpretation of meteorological satellite imageries and products;\r\n&#9642;access, select, display and manipulate satellite data;\r\n&#9642;carry out weather analysis and forecasting by using meteorological satellite imageries and data;\r\n&#9642;use Satellite Weather Application Platform (SWAP) and Satellite Monitoring Analysis Remote-sensing Toolkit (SMART), which cover laboratory practice for torrential rain and strong convective weather monitoring, wild fire monitoring, and drought monitoring;\r\n&#9642;have a better understanding of the Fengyun (FY) satellites, as well as the current and future products of geostationary satellite products such as wind profiling radar (WPR), cloud spectral image, lightning image observation, and space weather monitoring.","summary":null,"content":"The training course includes:\r\n&#9642;Concepts of satellite remote-sensing;\r\n&#9642;Satellite cloud image recognition and interpretation;\r\n&#9642;Satellite Weather Application Platform (SWAP) and Satellite Monitoring Analysis Remote-sensing Toolkit (SMART);\r\n&#9642;Case study on satellite products application in disaster mitigation and prevention;\r\nIntroduction to the latest development of Fengyun satellites and the application of FY products, especially the geostationary satellite products.","targetAudience":"Officials, specialists, forecasters engaged in satellite meteorology and disaster risk management at National Hydrological and Meteorological Services or equivalent institutions.","registrationHowto":"Participants are requested to submit the completed Application Form (https://forms.office.com/r/UUL21TQw2k) no later than 15 April 2022. The accepted applicants will receive the admission notices with Certificate-path Enroll Key endorsed by CMATC.","registrationEndDate":1649980800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5862,"name":"DENG Jingmian","email":"dengjingmian@cma.gov.cn","agency":"","country":"CN","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":9,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence China-Nanjing"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":190,"value":"RTC","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":19,"value":"CMA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2137,"created":1647015628000,"updated":1647015683000,"title":"Using the UN Biodiversity Lab to Monitor the Pulse of the Planet","titleNative":"Using the UN Biodiversity Lab to Monitor the Pulse of the Planet","startDate":1649941200000,"endDate":1651177800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3uGiV8Z","description":"This training, offered in English, Spanish and French, will feature UN Biodiversity Lab (UNBL), a platform with over 400 of the worlds best global data layers on biodiversity, ecosystem services, and sustainable development. It will provide learners with an understanding of the power of spatial data to address key international commitments and instruction on how to use UNBL to implement, monitor, and report on the CBDs Post-2020 Global Biodiversity Framework (GBF).\r\n\r\nPlease see the training details and registration information below (all text is available in English, Spanish, and French, if applicable).\r\n\r\n\r\nUsing the UN Biodiversity Lab to Monitor the Pulse of the Planet\r\n\r\nThis training will focus on using remote sensing and geospatial data within the NASA-supported UN Biodiversity Lab (UNBL) to take action on national conservation and sustainable development priorities. With over 400 of the worlds best global data layers on biodiversity, ecosystem services, and sustainable development, UNBL enables decision-makers and policymakers to access essential global data, upload national datasets, and calculate dynamic indicators for any area of interest - all without any background in remote sensing and GIS. Fully redesigned in 2021 to improve functionality and better meet user needs, UNBL will be a key offering to support parties and other stakeholders to implement, monitor, and report on the CBDs Post-2020 Global Biodiversity Framework (GBF).\r\n\r\nThis training offers three complementary courses -- an intermediate lecture series (Parts 1-3) and two advanced labs --  that participants can select among to choose the learning experience that best meets their needs. Our intermediate course will integrate lectures and Q&A to provide learners with an understanding of the power of spatial data to address key international commitments and a training on how to use all key functionalities of UNBL. Our advanced lab on the UNBL public platform will combine homework and live exercises to build participants confidence in their ability to use all features of UNBL. Our advanced lab on UNBL workspaces will combine homework and live exercises to equip participants with the needed skills to take full advantage of workspace functionalities.  Speakers will include NASA scientists, UN policy specialists and data experts, and national policymakers.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\r\nGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts \r\nGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\r\nGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss\r\n\r\nCourse Dates: \r\n\tIntermediate Series (Parts 1-3): April 14, 21, 28, 2022\r\n\tAdvanced Labs: April 27 & May 4, 2022\r\n\r\nTimes and Registration Information:\r\n\tEnglish Session: 9:00-10:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3uGiV8Z\r\n\tFrench Session: 11:00-12:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3M978q9\r\n\tSpanish Session: 15:00-16:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3BWXyC5\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\nen français\r\n  \r\nUtiliser le UN Biodiversity Lab pour surveiller le pouls de la planète\r\n \r\nCette formation se concentrera sur l'utilisation de la télédétection et des données géospatiales au sein du UN Biodiversity Lab (UNBL), soutenu par la NASA, pour agir sur les priorités nationales en matière de conservation et de développement durable. Avec plus de 400 des meilleures couches de données mondiales sur la biodiversité, les services écosystémiques et le développement durable, le UNBL permet aux décideurs et aux responsables politiques d'accéder à des données mondiales essentielles, de télécharger des ensembles de données nationales et de calculer des indicateurs dynamiques pour tout domaine d'intérêt - le tout sans aucune connaissance en télédétection et en SIG. Entièrement repensé en 2021 pour améliorer sa fonctionnalité et mieux répondre aux besoins des utilisateurs, le UNBL sera un outil essentiel pour aider les Parties et les autres parties prenantes à mettre en uvre, surveiller et rendre compte du Cadre mondial pour la biodiversité (CMB) post-2020 de la CDB. \r\n\r\nCette formation propose trois cours complémentaires  une série de conférences intermédiaires (parties 1-3) et deux sessions avancées  parmi lesquels les participants peuvent choisir l'expérience d'apprentissage qui répond le mieux à leurs besoins. Notre cours intermédiaire intégrera des conférences et des questions-réponses afin de permettre aux apprenants de comprendre le pouvoir des données spatiales pour répondre aux principaux engagements internationaux et de s'entraîner à l'utilisation de toutes les fonctionnalités clés du UNBL. Notre session avancée sur la plateforme publique UNBL combinera des devoirs et des exercices en direct pour renforcer la confiance des participants dans leur capacité à utiliser toutes les fonctionnalités du UNBL. Notre session avancée sur les espaces de travail du UNBL combinera des devoirs et des exercices en direct afin de doter les participants des compétences nécessaires pour tirer pleinement parti des fonctionnalités de l'espace de travail. Parmi les intervenants figureront des scientifiques de la NASA, des spécialistes des politiques et des données de l'ONU, ainsi que des responsables politiques nationaux.\r\n \r\nObjectifs de développement durable pertinents :\r\n \r\nObjectif 13 : Prendre des mesures urgentes pour lutter contre le changement climatique et ses impacts \r\nObjectif 14 : Conserver et utiliser durablement les océans, les mers et les ressources marines pour le développement durable\r\nObjectif 15 : Protéger, restaurer et promouvoir l'utilisation durable des écosystèmes terrestres, gérer durablement les forêts, lutter contre la désertification, et stopper et inverser la dégradation des terres et stopper la perte de biodiversité\r\n \r\nDates de la formation :  \r\n\tSérie de conférences intermédiaires (parties 1-3) : 14, 21, 28 avril et 2022\r\n\tSessions avancées : 27 avril & 4 mai et 2022\r\n \r\nInformations sur les horaires et inscriptions :\r\n\tSession en français : 11h-12h30 EDT (UTC-4) :  https://go.nasa.gov/3M978q9\r\n\tSession en espagnol : 15h-16h30 EDT (UTC-4) :  https://go.nasa.gov/3BWXyC5\r\n\tSession en anglais : 9h-10h30 EDT (UTC-4) :  https://go.nasa.gov/3uGiV8Z\r\n \r\nObjectifs dapprentissage : À la fin de cette formation, les participants seront en mesure de : \r\n\r\n\tComprendre les principaux instruments de la politique mondiale en matière de biodiversité et de développement durable dans la mesure où ils sont liés aux efforts de conservation.\r\n\tUtiliser des exemples de pays inspirants utilisant des données spatiales pour agir en faveur des personnes et de la planète comme base de leur travail.\r\n\tRésumer les types de données disponibles sur le UNBL, et être capable d'identifier des ensembles de données spécifiques liés à ces thèmes.\r\n\tExplorer comment les différentes combinaisons de données disponibles dans les collections du UNBL peuvent générer des idées et informer des actions liées à des questions politiques clés.\r\n\tAppliquer les fonctionnalités de la plateforme publique du UNBL pour rechercher et visualiser des données, calculer des métriques dynamiques, créer des cartes et télécharger des données sur la plateforme publique du UNBL. \r\n\tUtiliser les fonctions de l'espace de travail sécurisé du UNBL pour ajouter des utilisateurs et leur attribuer des privilèges, télécharger des lieux, télécharger et styliser des couches de données, calculer des métriques dynamiques pour votre domaine d'intérêt. \r\n\r\nPublic cible : \r\n\tResponsables politiques nationaux impliqués dans les engagements de conservation et de développement durable de la Convention sur la diversité biologique, de la Convention-cadre des Nations Unies sur les changements climatiques et de l'Agenda 2030 pour le développement durable.\r\n\tPersonnel du système des Nations Unies soutenant des projets et des programmes alignés sur l'approche commune des Nations Unies en matière de biodiversité et l'Agenda 2030. \r\n\tChercheurs et acteurs de la société civile intéressés par l'utilisation de données spatiales pour mettre en uvre, suivre et rendre compte de projets opérant à l'intersection de la biodiversité, du changement climatique et du développement durable.\r\n\r\nFormat de la formation : \r\n\tFormation intermédiaire : Trois sessions dune heure et demie, intégreronta des conférences et des questions-réponses\r\n\tSessions avancées : deux sessions dune heure et demie, intégreronta devoirs et des exercices en direct\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nen Español\r\n\r\nUtilizar el UN Biodiversity Lab para Tomar el Pulso del Planeta\r\n \r\nEsta formación se centrará en el uso de datos de teledetección y geoespaciales dentro del UN Biodiversity Lab (UNBL), apoyado por la NASA, para tomar medidas sobre las prioridades nacionales de conservación y desarrollo sostenible. Con más de 400 de las mejores capas de datos mundiales sobre biodiversidad, servicios de los ecosistemas y desarrollo sostenible, el UNBL permite a los responsables de la toma de decisiones y a los políticos acceder a datos mundiales esenciales, cargar conjuntos de datos nacionales y calcular indicadores dinámicos para cualquier área de interés, todo ello sin necesidad de tener conocimientos de teledetección y SIG. Completamente rediseñada este 2021 para mejorar la funcionalidad y satisfacer mejor las necesidades de los usuarios, la UNBL será una oferta clave para apoyar a las Partes y a otras partes interesadas en la aplicación, el seguimiento y la presentación de informes sobre el Marco Mundial de la Biodiversidad (MGB) del CDB posterior a 2020.\r\n\r\nEsta formación ofrece tres cursos complementarios -una serie de conferencias intermedias (Parte 1-3) y dos laboratorios avanzados- entre los que los participantes pueden elegir la experiencia de aprendizaje que mejor se adapte a sus necesidades. Nuestro curso intermedio integrará conferencias con sesiones de preguntas y respuestas para proporcionar a los alumnos una comprensión del poder de los datos espaciales para abordar los principales compromisos internacionales y una formación sobre cómo utilizar todas las funcionalidades clave de UNBL. Nuestro laboratorio avanzado en la plataforma pública de UNBL combinará deberes y ejercicios en directo para que los participantes confíen en su capacidad para utilizar todas las funciones de UNBL. Nuestro laboratorio avanzado sobre los espacios de trabajo de UNBL combinará deberes y ejercicios en directo para dotar a los participantes de las habilidades necesarias para aprovechar al máximo las funcionalidades de los espacios de trabajo.  Entre los ponentes se encontrarán científicos de la NASA, especialistas en políticas de la ONU y expertos en datos, así como responsables políticos nacionales .\r\n\r\nObjetivos de Desarrollo Sostenible de la ONU Relevantes:\r\n \r\nObjetivo 13: Tomar acciones urgentes para combatir el cambio climático y sus impactos\r\nObjetivo 14: Conservar y utilizar de manera sostenible los océanos, mares y recursos marinos para el desarrollo sostenible\r\nObjetivo 15: Proteger, restaurar y promover el uso sostenible de ecosistemas terrestres, gestionar bosques de manera sostenible, combatir la desertificación y detener y revertir la degradación de tierras y detener la pérdida de diversidad biológica\r\n\r\nFechas del Curso: \r\n\tpresentaciones intermedias: 14, 21, 28 de abril de 2022\r\n\tlaboratorios avanzados:  27 de abril y 4 de mayo de 2022\r\n \r\nHorarios e Información para la Inscripción:\r\n\tSesión en Español: 15h-16h30 Hora Este de EE.UU. (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3BWXyC5\r\n\tSesión en Inglés: 9h-10h30 Hora Este de EE.UU. (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3uGiV8Z\r\n\tSesión en Francés: 11h-12h30 Hora Este de EE.UU. (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3M978q9\r\n \r\nObjetivos de Aprendizaje: Para la conclusión de esta capacitación, los/las participantes podrán:\r\n \r\n\tEntender instrumentos políticos claves para la diversidad biológica global y el desarrollo sostenible (CDB, Convención Marco De Las Naciones Unidas Sobre el Cambio Climático (UNFCCC), la Agenda 2030 para el Desarrollo Sostenible) en lo que se refieren a campañas de conservación \r\n\tAdquirir conocimiento sobre datos espaciales sobre la diversidad biológica y el desarrollo sostenible, incluso datos generados por proyectos de la NASA\r\n\tEstar familiarizados con la estructura, datos y herramientas del UN Biodiversity Lab \r\n\tTener la capacidad de aplicar las herramientas del UN Biodiversity Lab a su región de interés\r\n\tUtilizar ejemplos de casos de estudio de múltiples países colaboradores como contexto para su trabajo \r\n\r\npúblico objetivo: \r\n\tResponsables políticos nacionales implicados en los compromisos de conservación y desarrollo sostenible del Convenio sobre la Diversidad Biológica, la Convención Marco de las Naciones Unidas sobre el Cambio Climático y la Agenda 2030 para el Desarrollo Sostenible.\r\n\tPersonal del sistema de la ONU que apoya proyectos y programas alineados con el Enfoque Común de la ONU sobre la Biodiversidad y la Agenda 2030.\r\n\tInvestigadores y actores de la sociedad civil interesados en utilizar datos espaciales para implementar, supervisar e informar sobre proyectos que operan en la intersección de la biodiversidad, el cambio climático y el desarrollo sostenible.\r\n\r\nFormato del Curso: \r\n\tpresentaciones intermedias: Tres sesiones de una hora y media, cada una incluye presentaciones, demostraciones y sesiones de preguntas y respuestas\r\n\tlaboratorios avanzados:  Dos sesiones de una hora y media,  cada una combinará tareas y ejercicios en vivo\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\r\n\r\n\tUnderstand key global biodiversity and sustainable development policy instruments as they relate to conservation efforts.\r\n\tUtilize examples from inspiring countries using spatial data to take action for people and planet as a basis for your work.\r\n\tSummarize the types of data available on UNBL, and be able to identify specific datasets related to these themes.\r\n\tExplore how different combinations of data available in UNBL collections can generate insight and inform action related to key policy questions.\r\n\tApply UNBL public platform features to search and visualize data, calculate dynamic metrics, create maps, and download data on the UNBL public platform. \r\n\tUse UNBL secure workspace features to add users and assign privileges, upload places, upload and style data layers, calculate dynamic metrics for your area of interest.","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: \r\n\tIntermediate Series: Three, one and half-hour parts that include lectures, demonstrations, and question & answer sessions. \r\n\tAdvanced Labs: Two, one and half-hour parts that include homework and live exercises.","targetAudience":"Audience: \r\n\tNational policymakers involved in conservation and sustainable development commitments to the Convention on Biological Diversity, UN Framework Convention on Climate Change, and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development.\r\n\tUN system staff supporting projects and programs aligned with the UN Common Approach on Biodiversity and 2030 Agenda. \r\n\tResearchers and civil society stakeholders interested in using spatial data to implement, monitor, and report on projects operating at the intersection of biodiversity, climate change, and sustainable development.\r\n\r\n","registrationHowto":"English Session: 9:00-10:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3uGiV8Z\r\nFrench Session: 11:00-12:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3M978q9\r\nSpanish Session: 15:00-16:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3BWXyC5\r\n\r\n ","registrationEndDate":1651156200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":4,"value":"French","iso639":"fr"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2147,"created":1648196272000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1649937600000,"endDate":1649941200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data.\r\nMaintaining a healthy basis of knowledge about satellite meteorology.\r\nUnderstanding the difference between RGBs as well as their benefits and limitations.\r\n","summary":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imag","content":"Weather briefings are recorded. Find a collection of past briefings at http://eumetrain.org/briefings.html and on YouTube at https://www.youtube.com/user/eumetrain/videos.","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are forecasters in shifts, although other meteorologists from different areas are also welcome.","registrationHowto":"In order to join the discussion, please go to http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html. For any questions, please use the Contact email.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2149,"created":1648743948000,"updated":1648746580000,"title":"Mapping Crops and their Biophysical Characteristics with Polarimetric SAR and Optical Remote Sensing","titleNative":"Mapping Crops and their Biophysical Characteristics with Polarimetric SAR and Optical Remote Sensing","startDate":1649772000000,"endDate":1651606200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/join-mission/training/english/arset-mapping-crops-and-their-biophysical-characteristics?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=CropMapping-II-22","description":"Mapping crop types and assessing their characteristics is critical for monitoring food production, enabling optimal use of the landscape, and contributing to agricultural policy. Remote sensing methods based on optical and/or microwave sensors have become an important means of extracting information related to crops. Optical data is related to the chemical properties of the vegetation, while radar data is related to vegetation structure and moisture. Radar can also image the Earths surface regardless of almost any type of weather condition.\r\n\r\nThis four-part, advanced training builds on the previous ARSET agricultural training. Here we present more advanced radar remote sensing techniques using polarimetry to extract crop structural information. We also present Sen4Stat  an open source system demonstrating the potential of optical and SAR satellite Earth observations for monitoring and reporting of the SDG targets related to agriculture. Sen4Stat also combines Earth observation data with national statistical data sets and surveys to support National Statistical Offices in the uptake of satellite Earth observations for agricultural statistics. \r\n\r\nThis series will focus on the use of dual polarization C-band SAR from Sentinel-1, fully polarimetric C-band SAR from the RADARSAT Constellation Mission (RCM), fully polarimetric L-band SAR from SAOCOM (SAtélite Argentino de Observación COn Microondas), and optical imagery from Sentinel-2 to map and monitor crop types and assess their biophysical characteristics. This series will also cover the theory of SAR Polarimetry and include a practical exercise using the Sentinel Application Platform (SNAP) and Python code written in JupyterNotebooks, a web-based interactive development environment for scientific computing and machine learning.\r\n\r\nThis webinar series is a collaboration between ARSET, Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada (AAFC), European Space Agency (ESA), United Nations Office for Outer Space Affairs (UNOOSA), University of Stirling, Université Catholique de Louvain (UCLouvain), and the CEOS Working Group on Capacity Building & Data Democracy (WGCapD). \r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\tGoal 2: End hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition and promote sustainable agriculture\r\n\tTarget 2.4: By 2030, ensure sustainable food production systems and implement resilient agricultural practices that increase productivity and production, that help maintain ecosystems, that strengthen capacity for adaptation to climate change, extreme weather, drought, flooding and other disasters and that progressively improve land and soil quality\r\n\r\nCourse Dates: April 12, 19, 26, & May 3, 2022\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will be able to:\r\n\r\n\tExplain the theory behind SAR Polarimetry, especially as related to crop characteristics\r\n\tGenerate polarimetric parameters using open source imagery/software and perform a time series analysis of crop growth\r\n\tIdentify how Sen4Stat can support National Statistical Offices in the uptake of satellite Earth observations for agricultural statistics\r\n\tPerform a time series analysis of crop types using Sentinel-2 derived LAI index \r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Four, 2.5 hour parts","targetAudience":"This webinar series is intended for local, regional, federal, and non-governmental organizations from agriculture and food security related agencies to use radar and optical remote sensing applications in the domain of agriculture for crop mapping and monitoring.","registrationHowto":"Times and Registration Information:  \r\n  \r\nEnglish Session: 10:00-12:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3hVAjir\r\nSpanish Session: 13:00-15:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3MPw8Da","registrationEndDate":1651604400000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":140,"value":"CEOS-WGCapD","visible":true},{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2109,"created":1638876783000,"updated":1646985813000,"title":"Convective storms - What can satellite data tell us? - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"Convective storms - What can satellite data tell us? - Series of online short courses","startDate":1649764800000,"endDate":1649772000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"Viewing at the storm clouds from above tells us about their interior dynamics. Join us to look at what the satellite images show about the processes happening in the storm clouds. We will reveal the structures seen on the storm tops with currently available satellites and peek into the future view that we will have with the sensors on Meteosat Third Generation (MTG). \r\n\r\nThis course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"Viewing at the storm clouds from above tells us about their interior dynamics. Join us to look at what the satellite images show about the processes happening in the storm clouds. ","content":"","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar at the course page: https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=444.\r\n\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2141,"created":1647519686000,"updated":1647524204000,"title":"High Impact Weather Summer Course Webinar #1","titleNative":"High Impact Weather Summer Course Webinar #1","startDate":1649667600000,"endDate":1649671200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"Eumetcal","contactPerson":"Eumetcal","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/Information/Tekstpagina?textpage=eumetcalwebinar","description":"First webinar as part of High Impact Weather Summer Course.\r\n\r\nThis HIW course is an online course for EUMETCAL and will focus on the use of a particular forecasting method based on Doswells ingredient-based forecasting method IBM (1996). This method assumes that three ingredients are needed for convection: moisture, lift, instability (and vertical wind shear to take the modes in convection into account).","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"1. Determine where and when deep convection will be initiated based on the convection scheme (IBM); they have to found the overlap regions of moisture, instability and lift.\r\n2. Determine which mode of convection can be expected; single cell/ pulse cell, multi cell, squall line or supercell.\r\n3. Determine the corresponding hazardous weather.","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Primary audience: forecasters working in the operation office of the National Weather Service or Meteorological Centres in Europe. The participants are expected to already have a basic experience in forecasting summer convection.\r\n\r\nSecondary audiences: meteorologists interested in synoptic and mesoscale phenomena (related to summer convection), involved in training, research or management, who would benefit from the course and also provide necessary feedback.","registrationHowto":"Please go to shorturl.at/iDLQR.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2103,"created":1637265389000,"updated":1648477348000,"title":"Dust monitoring with satellite and ground-based observations - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"Dust monitoring with satellite and ground-based observations - Series of online short courses","startDate":1649246400000,"endDate":1649246400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=439","description":"This course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"This course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate.","content":"This short course will give you an introduction to dust monitoring and forecasting. The course will give you first a high-level introduction to data observations (ground-based and satellite) and model forecasts that can be used to monitor and forecast dust events. The high-level introduction is followed by a practical data discovery part. Focussing on one dust event, the practical part will practically introduce you to satellite-, ground- and model-based data.  For each dataset featured, you will get a practical hands-on introduction on how to access, process, visualize and interpret the dataset. \r\n\r\nThe course outline is as follows:\r\n\r\nPart 1: High-level introduction to data observations and model forecasts (~ 30 min)\r\n\r\nPart 2: Practical hands-on data discovery part (~ 55 min)\r\n\r\nQ&A (~ 10 min)","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar at the course page: https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=439.\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2146,"created":1648104831000,"updated":1648105266000,"title":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting","titleNative":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting","startDate":1648519200000,"endDate":1648522800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Melbourne","host":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence","contactPerson":"Bodo Zeschke","contactEmail":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","contactUrl":"https://learn.bom.gov.au/mod/bigbluebuttonbn/view.php?id=13874 see instructions below","description":"The topic of discussion for the next Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting scheduled for 02UTC, Tuesday 29th March 2022 is as follows:\r\n\r\n\"On the rapid intensification and weakening of Tropical Cyclone's Vernon and Charlotte\": facilitator Mr Joe Courtney, Bureau of Meteorology, Perth.\r\n","country":{"id":"AU","name":"AUSTRALIA","region":"RA-V","type":"Rest of the World"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"\"On the rapid intensification and weakening of Tropical Cyclone's Vernon and Charlotte\": facilitator Mr Joe Courtney, Bureau of Meteorology, Perth.","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"For this session we will be using the Big Blue Button web conferencing system on our training site.\r\nTo register please follow the instructions below:\r\n1.\tThe session homepage is at https://learn.bom.gov.au/mod/bigbluebuttonbn/view.php?id=13874 \r\n2.\tYou can join the session either as a guest or as a registered user. We would prefer you to join as a registered user. There is no cost associated with this. \r\nA.\tFor guest users the session password is VLab@2020\r\nB.\tFor users who choose to join the session as a registered user for the first time, please use the link https://learn.bom.gov.au/login/signup.php to create an account. Please include your details including Country of Origin. After signing up you will receive an automated email with a link to confirm your registration within about 15 minutes. If you have not received the confirmation email within this time, check your spam/junk inbox. If there is no confirmation within your spam/junk inbox, contact moodle@bom.gov.au giving their name and email address, stating they had not received their site registration confirmation email for our training site.\r\nC.\tFor users who have already registered, please click \"Continue\" on the Self enrolment (Student) option. Log in using the above Username and Password. If you have forgotten the password then you can use the 'forgot password' function: https://learn.bom.gov.au/login/forgot_password.php\r\n\r\nPlease use either the Google Chrome or Modzilla Firefox web browsers when you join the session.\r\n\r\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5860,"name":"Bodo Zeschke","email":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","agency":"","country":"AU","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":5,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Australia"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":5,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Australia","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2125,"created":1643274768000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1648126800000,"endDate":1648130400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data.\r\nMaintaining a healthy basis of knowledge about satellite meteorology.\r\nUnderstanding the difference between RGBs as well as their benefits and limitations.\r\n","summary":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imag","content":"Weather briefings are recorded. Find a collection of past briefings at http://eumetrain.org/briefings.html and on YouTube at https://www.youtube.com/user/eumetrain/videos.","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are forecasters in shifts, although other meteorologists from different areas are also welcome.","registrationHowto":"In order to join the discussion, please go to http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html. For any questions, please use the Contact email.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2122,"created":1642751302000,"updated":1644591352000,"title":"Satellite Application Course: LEO satellite applications","titleNative":"Satellite Application Course: LEO satellite applications","startDate":1647757800000,"endDate":1648116000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"Public Authority for Civil Aviation (PACA)","contactPerson":"Hilal Al-Hajri","contactEmail":"h.alhajri@met.gov.om","contactUrl":"","description":"This course will focus on atmosphere, land and marine applications of LEO satellite data and products. Overview of existing LEO missions will be provided, together with examples of data utilisation. The benefits of future EPS-SG mission will also be covered. ","country":{"id":"OM","name":"OMAN","region":"RA-II","type":"Middle East"},"learningOutcome":"Participants will learn about:\r\n- Difference between LEO and GEO missions, and associated data;\r\n- Utility of LEO data for marine, atmosphere and land applications;\r\n- Perspective of the upcoming EPS-SG mission;\r\n- Different LEO data and products, and tools used to process and visualize these\r\n\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Information on LEO missions and data utility. Overview of next generation of EPS mission. ","targetAudience":"Operational weather forecasters, R&D staff, management ","registrationHowto":"By invitation letter.","registrationEndDate":1646092800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5845,"name":"Ivan Smiljanic","email":"ivan.smiljanic@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false},{"id":7,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Oman","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":1,"value":"Arabic","iso639":"ar"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"}],"qualification":[{"id":15,"value":"Training: Education and Training Providers "},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":10,"value":"PWS: Advisors Supporting Disaster Prevention and Mitigation and Other User Activities"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2102,"created":1637259172000,"updated":1644325144000,"title":"AI for atmospheric composition - how to enhance resolution using satellite data - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"AI for atmospheric composition - how to enhance resolution using satellite data - Series of online short courses","startDate":1647432000000,"endDate":1647439200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=438","description":"This course is part of the spring 2022 edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate. ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"This course presents how new Artificial Intelligence methods could be used in Earth Observation and numerical model for monitoring purposes. \r\nThe method that will be presented is about super resolution: it consists in the enhancement of the resolution of an image based on the higher resolution of another image. \r\n\r\nIn Earth Observation, a satellite rarely gets the capability to have a same scene at multiple resolutions: in this way the method will use two different data sources, on one side the Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service model for NO2 measurements and on another side the Sentinel-5p NO2 measurements on the tropospheric column.\r\n \r\nBoth datasets are measuring the same environmental variable, but at different resolution: following the concept of super resolution, we retrieve on one hand our low resolution image and on another hand our high resolution image of a same scene.\r\nMoreover, since Deep Learning is more and more available even without a large amount of knowledge in computer science, the course will also show how to use existing tools as FastAI and Pytorch to use state of the art Deep Learning architectures to build and use an own model.\r\n","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar at the course page: https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=438.\r\n\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2140,"created":1647426158000,"updated":1647426334000,"title":"Webinar on Nowcasting in Africa","titleNative":"Webinar on Nowcasting in Africa","startDate":1647334800000,"endDate":1647356400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Vincent Gabaglio","contactEmail":"Vincent.gabaglio@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://www.eumetsat.int/webinar-nowcasting-africa ","description":"This webinar aims at giving an overview of Nowcasting in Africa through concrete examples, to present results of recent initiatives to streghthen Nowcasting capacities, and to discuss the future of nowcasting in Africa, including the contribution of the upcoming Meteosat Third Generation (MTG).","country":null,"learningOutcome":"","summary":"This webinar aims at giving an overview of Nowcasting in Africa through concrete examples, to present results of recent initiatives to streghthen Nowcasting capacities, and to disc","content":"Nowcasting is defined as forecasting over a period from the present to six hours ahead. It is key to monitor Severe Weather Event and to adjust Warnings made to the population or specific user groups about imminent meteorological risks, with potential damages or causalities.\r\n\r\nClimate change increases the frequency and severity of weather-led disasters significant economic costs and human losses. Nowcasting is more and more critical for peoples safety as well as for key economic sectors such as aviation, agriculture, energy, water management and for emergency preparedness and response.\r\n\r\nDue to the scarcity of surface and upper-air observations in Africa, weather satellites are a key source of information to provide the high temporal and spatial data coverage needed by African forecasters to deliver efficient nowcasting and subsequently warn key users and the population.\r\n\r\nAgenda and all presentations are available for download here: https://www.eumetsat.int/webinar-nowcasting-africa ","targetAudience":"The Webinar is open to everyone. The target audience are the African stakeholders involved in the Disaster Risk Management cycle, from preparedness, weather forecasting to early warning and immediate response, as well as the professionals benefiting from short-term weather forecasts (e.g. aviation authorities, renewable energy production manager, etc.).\r\n\r\nThe video of the Webinar is available here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cvb8vo7lGF4 ","registrationHowto":"You can register here: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_a43a5uOmSk2CLjmHvF5T3A","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5841,"name":"Regina Hoefenmayer","email":"regina.hoefenmayer@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2134,"created":1646143548000,"updated":1646293105000,"title":"Data Access How-to Sessions - S3 Data on Data Store","titleNative":"Data Access How-to Sessions - S3 Data on Data Store","startDate":1646917200000,"endDate":1646920800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"","contactEmail":"","contactUrl":"","description":"This session will focus on how to get Sentinel-3 data from the EUMETSAT Data Store, and is scheduled on:\r\nThursday 10 March 2022, 13:00-14:00 UTC\r\nThis is part of a monthly series of open, online short sessions on the Data Access Services and their usage via GUIs and APIs, answering top questions posted by participants in an open sli.do (event code: #data-services, link https://app.sli.do/event/flsdivtc).\r\n\r\nWe encourage you to post your questions in sli.do now so that we can adapt the content of the next session to answer them.\r\n\r\nThe sessions are open to all and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to scientists and forecasters. Each session requires registration via Zoom. A great opportunity to remove any technical challenges in using the Data Access Services GUIs and APIs, and engage users in discussion with EUMETSAT experts and colleagues.\r\n\r\nFuture sessions are tentatively scheduled for:\r\n\r\nThursday 7 April 2022, 12:00-13:00 UTC\r\n\r\nThursday 12 May 2022, 12:00-13:00 UTC\r\n\r\nThursday 16 June 2022, 12:00-13:00 UTC","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Users should be able to use the Data Store service to browse, search for, download and eventually subscribe to, collections and products of interest using both GUI and API, as required.\r\n\r\nUsers should be able to use the Data Tailor Web Service to customise products to create bespoke data streams that fit their applications, via both GUI and API, as required.\r\n\r\nUsers should be able to use the Data Tailor Web Service to customise products to mirror existing processing chains that rely on EUMETCast and/or the Data Centre.\r\n\r\nUsers should be able to use the EUMETView OMS GUI to: i) discover layers, ii) craft their own views, iii) manipulate and share views, iv) export data from layers as time series, images and animations, and v) link layers to Data Store collections where appropriate.\r\n\r\nUsers should be able to use the EUMETView OMS API to i) discover capabilities, ii) exploit the WMS/WCS/WFS endpoints to construct customised data requests.\r\n\r\nUsers should know where to find resources to support the use of the new data access services.\r\n\r\nUsers should know where to go for further help in using the new data access services.","summary":"This session will focus on how to get Sentinel-3 data from the EUMETSAT Data Store, and is scheduled on:\r\nThursday 1