mmb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup.asp","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2801137129663911948","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2063,"created":1631525349000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1632139200000,"endDate":1632142800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data.\r\nMaintaining a healthy basis of knowledge about satellite meteorology.\r\nUnderstanding the difference between RGBs as well as their benefits and limitations.\r\n","summary":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imag","content":"Weather briefings are recorded. Find a collection of past briefings at http://eumetrain.org/briefings.html and on YouTube at https://www.youtube.com/user/eumetrain/videos.","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are forecasters in shifts, although other meteorologists from different areas are also welcome.","registrationHowto":"In order to join the discussion, please go to http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html. For any questions, please use the Contact email.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2055,"created":1628625583000,"updated":1628668770000,"title":"Monitoring Coastal and Estuarine Water Quality: Transitioning from MODIS to VIIRS","titleNative":"Monitoreo de la Calidad de Aguas Costeras y Estuarinas: La Transición de MODIS a VIIRS","startDate":1631635200000,"endDate":1632259800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3egZCtO","description":"Estuaries are water bodies where freshwater from rivers and streams meets with seawater. Coastal and estuarine waters are important to humans as they are highly productive habitats supporting a variety of fish and wildlife. These environments also provide resources, economic benefits, and ecosystem services. That being said, the water quality of these coastal and estuarine areas is of great importance. This is heavily influenced by the flow of nutrients and sediment from land-based sources. The flow of nitrogen and phosphorus in particular enhances algal bloom and hypoxia in the water, highly affecting benthic creatures like fish, shellfish, and crustaceans. Water quality in estuaries also depends on several factors such as their size, location, coastal land use, quality of freshwater runoff, and tidal effects from the sea.\r\n\r\nRemote sensing observations from Aqua/MODIS have enabled us to monitor coastal and estuarine water quality since mid-2000. As MODIS nears the end of its mission, it is important to transition to VIIRS (first launched in 2011) to provide continuity with the MODIS instrument and add to the moderate-resolution, long-term data record of water quality monitoring in estuaries. This intermediate-level webinar will provide an overview of recent satellites and sensors used for extending the MODIS long-term water quality time series, specifically focusing on VIIRS image processing using the NASA Ocean Color software, SeaDAS. This webinar will point out similarities and differences between MODIS and VIIRS and demonstrate water quality monitoring procedures using these sensors in selected coastal and estuarine regions.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goal: \r\n\tGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\r\n\tTarget 14.2: By 2020, sustainably manage and protect marine and coastal ecosystems to avoid significant adverse impacts, including by strengthening their resilience, and take action for their restoration in order to achieve healthy and productive oceans\r\n\tGoal 6: Ensure availability and sustainable management of water and sanitation for all\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\r\n\r\n\tIdentify recent satellite data useful for water quality monitoring\r\n\tProcess MODIS and VIIRS images using SeaDAS to get water quality parameters\r\n\tMonitor water quality in selected coastal and estuarine regions\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Three, 1.5 hour parts\r\n\r\nSeptember 14, 16, & 21, 2021","targetAudience":"Federal, state, regional, and local public and private organizations involved in water quality and estuarine ecosystem management.","registrationHowto":"Times and Registration Information:  \r\n  \r\nEnglish Session: 12:00-13:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3yWpLpR\r\nSpanish Session: 16:00-17:30 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3kMJPY7\r\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1842,"created":1576689357000,"updated":1626183363000,"title":"International Summer School on Applications with the Newest Multi-Spectral Environmental Satellites ","titleNative":"International Summer School on Applications with the Newest Multi-Spectral Environmental Satellites ","startDate":1631516400000,"endDate":1631883600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"ONLINE","host":"CNMCA/CIMSS, ITALY","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT Training Team","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"This course is a follow-up on postponed course from 2020. Valid applications will be those from registration process in year 2020.\r\n\r\nThe focus of this classroom course is on the methods and techniques used to extract information from environmental satellite data, with an emphasis on the latest measuring technologies. Additional focus will be on the upcoming MTG mission capabilities. \r\n","country":null,"learningOutcome":"","summary":"This course is a follow-up on postponed course from 2020. Valid applications will be those from registration process in year 2020.\r\n\r\nThe focus of this classroom course is on the m","content":"","targetAudience":"This courses addresses experienced professionals working with remote sensing data (forecasters, researchers, post-gradate students etc.) interested to update their knowledge and skills on the newest developments in remote sensing data. The applicants must have an academic degree either in Meteorology, Physics, Environmental Sciences or Environmental Engineering. Applicants should be proficients in the English language to attend the course.\r\n\r\nThe course is intended primarily for the European audience, with preference for the member states. Depending on availability, additional applicants might be accepted. Candidates will be selected on the basis of their proven experiences in environmental remote sensing. ","registrationHowto":"Applications are now closed.","registrationEndDate":1596240000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5845,"name":"Ivan Smiljanic","email":"ivan.smiljanic@external.eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2054,"created":1628200173000,"updated":1628495497000,"title":"Three-part webinar series on the use of GPM data products for applications","titleNative":"Three-part webinar series on the use of GPM data products for applications","startDate":1631109600000,"endDate":1632319200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"GPM, IPWG, NASA","contactPerson":"Andrea Portier","contactEmail":"andrea.m.portier@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"http://ipwg.isac.cnr.it/","description":"This consists of a three-part webinar series hosted by the Global Precipitation Measurement (GPM) Applications Program and IPWG. It will focus on the use of GPM data products for applications. Specifically, the participant will learn more about how to access GPM data and will be provided demonstrations on precipitation data access and analysis using NASA's Giovanni tool and Google Earth Engine.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Understand IMERG and the strengths and caveats of the long-term IMERG data record for applications\r\nLearn to access, analyze, and visualize IMERG precipitation data using NASA Giovanni tools and Google Earth Engine ","summary":null,"content":"Review the TRMM / GPM Missions\r\nBecome familiar with GPM data access and products and GPM applications\r\nFind previous training webinars related to GPM precipitation\r\n\r\n","targetAudience":"Users, instructors, students","registrationHowto":"More information for registering here : http://ipwg.isac.cnr.it/ and https://gpm.nasa.gov/data/training\r\nDates:  8, 15, 22 September 2021\r\nTime: Each session will be presented twice to accommodate different time zones\r\n14:00 to 15:30 UTC\r\n23:00 to 00:30 UTC","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":100,"value":"WMO","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":25,"value":"NASA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":19,"value":"PWS: Persons Engaged in the Development and Delivery of Products and Services"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1955,"created":1604930236000,"updated":1622016927000,"title":"Supporting Marine EO Educators: Professional development, knowledge exchange, and networking workshop","titleNative":"Supporting Marine EO Educators: Professional development, knowledge exchange, and networking workshop","startDate":1630281600000,"endDate":1631836800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Lauren Biermann","contactEmail":"Copernicus.training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://oceanexpert.org/event/3064","description":"The purpose of this training workshop, run by IOC/OTGA, EUMETSAT & Copernicus Marine Service, is to share knowledge, skills, resources and code so that attendees are independently able to train others on available EO tools. During the course, participants will work on creating their own (learning) artefact. This is an interactive knowledge exchange workshop, not a technical training event.\r\nThis is an online event with asynchronous and synchronous sessions. The estimated workload should be 5-8 hours/week, up to 25 hours in total across the 3 weeks. This is a hybrid workshop including 3 live (synchronous) sessions per week, complemented with materials and resources provided in asynchronous mode. It will also be possible to book 1-1 sessions with the trainers. The live sessions will include round table knowledge exchange sessions, allowing participants to share their EO teaching experiences.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"At the end of the course, learners should be able to/will have the skills to:\r\n- Teach the use of Copernicus Sentinel-3 and CMEMS data for marine applications, covering the use of sea surface temperature, altimetry and ocean colour data, as well as downstream products and model outputs.\r\n- Show others how to access the Copernicus Marine Data through online portals, APIs and other methods.\r\n- Explain the concepts of data quality (including basic principles of validation) and selection of appropriate products when helping students design workflows.\r\n- Understand the use of the following tools in training  SNAP, Remote Sensing Digital Teaching Tool, Git, Python (and Jupyter notebooks), cloud computing (WEkEO).\r\n- Select and adapt existing resources and develop new resources for their own training activities.\r\n","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"This course is specifically designed for, and aimed at, those who conduct teaching / training on remote sensing in academic, operational, and/or commercial environments. Participants need not be experts in use of Sentinel-3 but should be comfortable accessing, visualising and processing remote sensing data and also have a clear role in teaching.\r\nThis workshop is intended to university teachers, mentors and tutors, as well as staff from National Oceanographic Data Centres and marine research/management institutions with EO-related training responsibilities. \r\nAs a prerequisite, participants must:\r\n- Currently (or in very near future) have training or teaching responsibilities on marine EO: e.g. a university lecturer, a team manager responsible for in-house training, a research supervisor, a freelance consultant who runs customised training courses. \r\n","registrationHowto":"Applications are through the IODE Ocean Expert portal: https://oceanexpert.org/event/3064","registrationEndDate":1625011140000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":3925,"name":"Kim Hockley","email":"khock@pml.ac.uk","agency":"EUM","country":"GB","programme":"PML","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2062,"created":1631525310000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1629892800000,"endDate":1629896400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data.\r\nMaintaining a healthy basis of knowledge about satellite meteorology.\r\nUnderstanding the difference between RGBs as well as their benefits and limitations.\r\n","summary":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imag","content":"Weather briefings are recorded. Find a collection of past briefings at http://eumetrain.org/briefings.html and on YouTube at https://www.youtube.com/user/eumetrain/videos.","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are forecasters in shifts, although other meteorologists from different areas are also welcome.","registrationHowto":"In order to join the discussion, please go to http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html. For any questions, please use the Contact email.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2053,"created":1627922433000,"updated":1628495734000,"title":"Satellite Observations for Analyzing Natural Hazards on Small Island Nations","titleNative":"ARSET - Observaciones de Satélites para el Análisis de Peligros Naturales en los Pequeños Estados Insulares","startDate":1629298800000,"endDate":1629997200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3hPv5FI","description":"Small island nations are highly vulnerable to climate change and natural disasters; among them hurricanes, cyclones, and other violent storms. These disasters can lead to severe flooding, landslides, and, in the worst cases, can result in the loss of life and property. In addition, a rise in the global mean sea level places island nations at a higher risk for permanent submersion of land, coastal erosion, coastal ecosystem loss or change, salinization, and impeded drainage.\r\n\r\nThis three-part training series will focus on small island nations while introducing the data, methods, and tools useful for monitoring natural hazards. Case studies will be used to demonstrate methodologies applying satellite and model data and open access tools to analyze storm impacts, sea level rise, and landslides on small island nations.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\tSDG 13: Strengthen resilience and adaptive capacity to climate-related hazards and natural disasters in all countries\r\n\tSDG 11: Make cities and human settlements inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable\r\n\r\nCourse Dates: August 18, 24, 26, 2021\r\n\r\nRetweet option: https://twitter.com/NASAARSET/status/1420037281751179264\r\n\r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: After participating in this training, attendees should be able to:\r\n\r\n\tRecognize the applications of passive and active remote sensing for flooding, landslides, and sea level rise\r\n\tPerform an analysis pre- and post-storm event for a region of interest\r\n\tAssess the landslide hazard for a region of interest prior to and during an event\r\n\tInterpret satellite-derived products for characterizing sea level rise on a regional scale","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Three, 2 hour parts","targetAudience":"Local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations involved in pre, during, or post disaster assessment in small island nations.","registrationHowto":"Times and Registration Information:  \r\n  \r\nEnglish Session: 11:00-13:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3hPv5FI\r\nSpanish Session: 15:00-17:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3rI8luV","registrationEndDate":1629936000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEF