gorithm developments for exploiting SCA products, and\r\n\tStimulate early development of applications.\r\n\r\n","summary":"EUMETSAT is organizing a series of User preparation Webinars on MTG and EPS-SG Observation missions. This Webinar will be on Scatterometer (SCA) mission, its products and applicati","content":"The Webinar will cover an overview of the SCA instrument, measurement principles, Level 1B products and  their formats and dissemination.  The Webinar will also give information on the generation of  soil moisture, wind and sea ice products from SCA and their applications.","targetAudience":"The MTG and EPS-SG user preparation webinars are open to a wide range of audience - scientists, developers, operational forecasters, manufacturers interested in satellite data and their applications in weather, climate, ocean, land and atmosphere. ","registrationHowto":"Zoom-Webinar link for registration is here: https://eumetsat.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_vflLPBarQ_aXrfRj8wD6cg","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1999,"created":1612468266000,"updated":1612949078000,"title":"Exploring the EUMETSAT Land-Surface Temperature Data Records - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"Exploring the EUMETSAT Land-Surface Temperature Data Records - Series of online short courses","startDate":1621425600000,"endDate":1621436400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Christine Träger-Chatterjee","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=403","description":"This course is part of the new edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate running from January 2021.\r\n\r\nWhat is Land Surface Temperature (LST)?  How is it measured? What is it used for? Why are there two similar data records of LST offered in the EUMETSAT portfolio?  Can we do climate analysis by combing the different LST data? These and more questions will be answered in this short course. \r\n\r\nYou will also be given the chance to get your hands on the data and explore them. \r\n\r\nA feedback session will take place on the same day at 15:00 UTC. Both sessions will be recorded.\r\n\r\nTo get ready for the course, please visit the course page at https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=403.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"The participants will have the opportunity to learn:\r\n- which are the EUMETSAT and Copernicus free and open data;\r\n- where to access the data and where to find information about the data;\r\n- how to handle the data using the tools proposed.","summary":"This course is part of the new edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate running from January 2021.\r\n\r","content":"","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=403.\r\n\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.\r\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5847,"name":"Christine Traeger Chatterjee","email":"christine.traeger@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1836,"created":1576665516000,"updated":1620390056000,"title":"Baltic+ workshop on the application of the satellite products for diagnosis of southerly flow environment ","titleNative":"Baltic+ workshop on the application of the satellite products for diagnosis of southerly flow environment ","startDate":1621296000000,"endDate":1621382400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"IMGW","contactPerson":"Bozena Lapeta","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"The Baltic+ course is a joint initiative of the National Meteorological Services of Latvia, Lithuania, Estonia and Poland, who join the forces in order to increase the knowledge in skills in the use of satellite observation data and to create a platform for exchanging ideas, applications and knowledge. \r\n\r\nThis is a two-part course, composed of a 3 weeks online, mostly asynchronous course and a two days follow-up workshop. The first part will take place from 12 April to 30 April 2021. The follow-up workshop will take place 18-20 May and be a venue for the participants to present their collaborative case studies. The online (asynchronous) component will include reading materials, exercises and collaborative case study preparatory work. This will take three weeks, including approximately 4 hours of individual work per week. The two-day follow-up workshop will summarize the lessons learnt in the form of exercises and final presentations of collaborative case studies started during the online phase.\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"In the context of a summer heavy precipitation event, participants will be able to: \r\n\r\n- select the appropriate satellite products for identification of the environments favourable to pre-convection and summer heavy precipitation;\r\n\r\n- recognize the features on the satellite products that indicate the development of the event using the weather chart, as well as NWP, radar and soundings data;\r\n\r\n- get the knowledge on the usefulness of the satellite data in issuing warnings for the event,\r\n\r\n- represent and communicate the warning in a visual, graphical form for the public reinforcing a simple and actionable style.","summary":null,"content":"The objective of the course is to develop and deliver a training program that brings the new developments in satellite meteorology and in tools and methodology for short range weather forecasting, as well as promote networking among meteorologists and experts from the participating countries.\r\n","targetAudience":"The course is primarily aimed at weather forecasters working in shifts with at least six months professional experience.","registrationHowto":"Successful participation in the Baltic+ online course is mandatory. Find more information on the online phase at http://trainingevents.eumetsat.int/#!details/1835. ","registrationEndDate":1612051200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":70,"value":"NHMS","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":8,"value":"PWS: Weather Broadcasters and Communicators"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1960,"created":1605880510000,"updated":1619428121000,"title":"Copernicus Marine Data Stream for Ocean Applications - Liège Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics","titleNative":"Copernicus Marine Data Stream for Ocean Applications - Liège Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics","startDate":1621209600000,"endDate":1621555200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Lauren Biermann","contactEmail":"Copernicus.training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"EUMETSAT will be offering training on the Copernicus Marine Data Stream (CMDS) to participants of the Liege Colloquium on Ocean Dynamics. \r\n\r\nThis training will be valuable to those working in and/or between coastal and open ocean waters, particularly in areas affected by human activities, high sediment, and high variability. The purpose of this training is to demonstrate the differences, strengths and weaknesses of the two different Chlorophyll algorithms, namely OC4ME and NN, delivered as standard Level 2 OLCI products. ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"After the course participants will have the skills to:\r\n\tUnderstand what data and products the Copernicus Marine Data Stream provides (variables, timeliness, resolution, data formats)\r\n\tSelect, access and download relevant L2 OLCI data products for specific tasks from the Copernicus Marine Data Stream (CMDS)\r\n\tVisualise and process CMDS data in SNAP and/or Python (Jupyter Notebooks)","summary":null,"content":"Educational resources will be made available on the Colloquium website from 3rd May for attendees to work through at their own pace. It is expected that this will require approximately 5 hours of engagement (reading resources, watching demo videos, etc) in total. Additionally, Jupyter Notebook exercises will be available for those interested in programming. No prior Python knowledge is required. At the same time, attendees will be able to pose questions to the trainers via a dedicated Slido platform. \r\n\r\nDuring the week of the Colloquium, there will be two 1.5-hour live sessions (9:00 CEST on 19th May and 11:00 CEST on 21st May) which will address the questions asked on Slido and demonstrate how to visualise OLCI Level 2 ocean colour data in SNAP and Python. Our aim is to assist users with assessing differences between Chl-a and sediment retrieval algorithms in coastal zones, around wind farms, and in open ocean waters. Attendees will also have opportunities to ask questions live.","targetAudience":"This training is open and free to all participants of the Colloquium. ","registrationHowto":"The training will be open to all registered attendees of the Colloquium. Registration form for the conference: http://labos.ulg.ac.be/gher/home/colloquium/colloquium-2021/registration/","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":3925,"name":"Kim Hockley","email":"khock@pml.ac.uk","agency":"EUM","country":"GB","programme":"PML","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2004,"created":1613658445000,"updated":1614004585000,"title":"Online RA-I Meteorological Satellite Applications 2021","titleNative":"Online RA-I Meteorological Satellite Applications 2021","startDate":1621209600000,"endDate":1623369600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT Training Team","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"This satellite applications course will help you to improve your skills in identifying key weather systems and features found in Africa and their characteristics in satellite imagery and products. You will develop professional relationships with course participants  and explore the use of a variety of web and mobile-based applications for communication and learning together.  ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Improved skills in: (a) Identifying key weather systems and features found in Africa and their characteristics in Satellite imagery and products; (b) Being a competent user of virtual course technology; (c) Developing relationships with course participants via the discussion forums and online briefings.\r\n","summary":null,"content":"In this course selected synoptic scale as well as mesoscale weather systems are addressed. The course addresses system/feature identification in satellite imagery and, to a lesser extent, it covers forecasting. ","targetAudience":"Instructors in satellite meteorology, operational forecasters or experienced satellite data users with the ability to pass on skills acquired at the course to other operational staff.","registrationHowto":"Candidates are requested to apply to the online course by completing an online questionnaire. Applications open in early March 2021 at <a href=\"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158\">http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158</a>.","registrationEndDate":1619917200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa","visible":false},{"id":8,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Kenya","visible":false},{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2005,"created":1613658757000,"updated":1613662149000,"title":"Cours enligne: Applications de la météorologie satellitaire pour RA-I","titleNative":"Cours enligne: Applications de la météorologie satellitaire pour RA-I","startDate":1621209600000,"endDate":1623369600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Jose Prieto","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"Ce cours sur les applications satellitaires vous aidera à améliorer vos compétences en matière d'identification des phénomènes et systèmes météorologiques clés que l'on trouve en Afrique et de leurs caractéristiques dans les images et produits satellitaires. Vous développerez des relations professionnelles avec les participants au cours et explorerez l'utilisation d'une variété d'applications web et mobiles pour communiquer et apprendre ensemble.   ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Amélioration des compétences en : (a) Identifier les principaux systèmes et caractéristiques météorologiques en Afrique et leurs caractéristiques dans l'imagerie et les produits satellitaires ; (b) Être un utilisateur compétent de la technologie des cours virtuels ; (c) Développer des relations avec les participants aux cours via les forums de discussion et les briefings en ligne.","summary":null,"content":"Dans ce cours, certains situations météorologiques à échelle synoptique et à méso-échelle sont abordés.\r\nLe cours traite l'identification des systèmes/fonctions sur les images satellites, dans une moindre mesure, il couvre la prévision. ","targetAudience":"Instructeurs en météorologie satellitaire, des prévisionnistes opérationnels ou des utilisateurs de données satellitaires, avec la possibilité de transmettre les compétences acquises lors du cours à d'autres membres du personnel opérationnel.","registrationHowto":"Les candidats sont invités à s'inscrire au cours en ligne en remplissant un questionnaire en ligne. Les candidatures sont ouvertes début mars 2021 à l'adresse suivante <a href=\"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158\">http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158</a>","registrationEndDate":1619989200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":4,"value":"French","iso639":"fr"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2026,"created":1618314687000,"updated":1618928098000,"title":"ECMWF Online Computing Training Week 2021","titleNative":"ECMWF Online Computing Training Week 2021","startDate":1621209600000,"endDate":1621555200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"ECMWF","contactPerson":"Eumetcal","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/app#/catalog/course/2ccd4de6-1830-4806-b0ea-213bf0385fe1","description":"Online computing training week offers webinars on a variety of topics related to software and applications used at ECMWF in both operational production and non-operational environments. The aim of the week is to provide updates on software and introduce new tools and computing activities at ECMWF. The webinars will familiarise participants with how these applications and software are used at ECMWF and provide attendees with information on how to use them effectively. The webinars are open to all, however, some products and services that will be showcased are only accessible to Member and Co-operating States.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"The webinars to be held during Online Computing Training Week 2021 are:\r\n1. Jupyter Notebooks at ECMWF, presented by Milana Vuckovic (ECMWF) - Monday 17th May 2021, 9am - 10am UTC\r\n2. Working with GRIB and BUFR made easier with ecCodes, presented by Paul Dando (ECMWF) - Monday 17th May 2021, 1pm - 2pm UTC\r\n3. Introduction to the Atos HPC, presented by Christophe Berthelot (Atos) - Tuesday 18th May 2021, 9am - 10am UTC\r\n4. Introduction to the Atos Test and Early Migration System (TEMS), presented by Xavi Abellan (ECMWF) - Tuesday 18th May 2021, 1pm - 2pm UTC\r\n5. European Weather Cloud, presented by Vasilis Baousis (ECMWF) - Wednesday 19th May 2021, 9am - 10am UTC\r\n6. Ask us Anything! Session - Wednesday 19th May 2021, 1pm - 2pm UTC\r\n7. A trip around MARS: Getting to know our Meteorological Archival and Retrieval System, presented by Daniel Varela Santoalla and Dominique Lucas (ECMWF) - Thursday 20th May 2021, 9am - 10am UTC\r\n8. Stop, think, interpolate! The power of regridding in Metview, presented by Pedro Maciel (ECMWF) - Thursday 20th May 2021, 1pm - 2pm UTC\r\n9. CliMetLab: shared boilerplate code to get data and plot data, presented by Florian Pinault and Baudouin Raoult (ECMWF) - Friday 21st May 2021, 9am - 10am UTC\r\n","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"The webinars will be held online and are open to all. More information on the webinars, a link to register your interest and be kept informed about the webinars and the opportunity to ask a question for \"Ask us Anything!\" can be found on the Online Computing Training Week events page:\r\nhttps://events.ecmwf.int/event/210/.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":106,"value":"Seminar"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2002,"created":1612984235000,"updated":1613028051000,"title":"Spot atmospheric convection from satellite: What satellite data is used in convection analysis - Series of online short courses","titleNative":"Spot atmospheric convection from satellite: What satellite data is used in convection analysis - Series of online short courses","startDate":1620820800000,"endDate":1620777600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=402","description":"This course is part of the new edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate running from January 2021.\r\n\r\nDiscover what satellite data is used in convection analysis and try yourself in a hands-on data exercise in a convective case.\r\n\r\nPlease visit the course page at https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=402. \r\n\r\nThe webinar will be recorded. ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":"This course is part of the new edition of EUMETSAT series of online short courses on data discovery for weather, oceanography, air quality and climate running from January 2021.\r\n\r","content":"","targetAudience":"The short courses are open and address a wide range of attendees, from service providers to young (and less young) scientists and forecasters.","registrationHowto":"Register in advance for this webinar at https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=402.\r\n\r\nAfter registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the webinar.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5844,"name":"Natasa Strelec Mahovic","email":"natasa.strelecmahovic@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2031,"created":1619612382000,"updated":1619616868000,"title":"Learning Resources at ECMWF: Forecasting, Research and Computing","titleNative":"Learning Resources at ECMWF: Forecasting, Research and Computing","startDate":1620810000000,"endDate":1620813600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"ECMWF","contactPerson":"Eumetcal","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.trainingscatalogus.nl/en/Information/Tekstpagina?textpage=TextPage5","description":"ECMWF provides a variety of online and in-person training courses for ECMWF users across the world for forecasting, research and computing. A public page has recently developed where all the learning resources available at ECMWF are displayed in a user-friendly way providing quick access and information from a number of sources.\r\n\r\nThis webinar will explore the resources ECMWF has available, how to find them, and how they can improve knowledge and efficacy when using ECMWF products, services and data. It will be particularly interesting for trainers within organisations however forecasters, researchers and those using ECMWF's computing facilities may also find it useful.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"This webinar will explore the resources ECMWF has available, how to find them, and how they can improve knowledge and efficacy when using ECMWF products, services and data. It will explore some ECMWF products and services in detail and how the learning resources available can help understanding for these. Future plans of ECMWF learning and training will also be touch on.","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"The webinar will be particularly interesting for trainers within organisations however forecasters, researchers and those using ECMWF's computing facilities may also find it useful. We will also welcome feedback during the webinar on ECMWF training and learning and how it can be improved for the future.","registrationHowto":"https://bluejeans.com/420438694","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2027,"created":1618431417000,"updated":1618472591000,"title":"Satellite Observations and Tools for Fire Risk, Detection, and Analysis","titleNative":"Satellite Observations and Tools for Fire Risk, Detection, and Analysis","startDate":1620745200000,"endDate":1622134800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3fpqARC","description":"Fires are a growing concern, especially in regions with longer fire seasons, expanded wildland/urban interfaces, and severe and frequent droughts. Anthropogenic fires are commonly used to clear grassland and agricultural land prior to the planting season, and forests are often cleared using fires so the land can be repurposed for other uses. Whether naturally-occurring or anthropogenic, fires produce a significant change in the structure and reflectance of vegetation and soil properties and atmospheric chemistry. Remote sensing can be used to monitor pre-, during-, and post-fire conditions; including weather and climate conditions, fuel characterization, fire risk, smoke detection, monitoring, and forecasting, fire behavior, and the post-fire landscape. This 6-part, intermediate training will provide lectures and case studies focused on the use of Earth observations for operational fire monitoring: pre-, during-, and post-event.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\n\tTarget 13.1: Strengthen resilience and adaptive capacity to climate-related hazards and natural disasters in all countries\r\n\tTarget 3.9: By 2030, substantially reduce the number of deaths and illnesses from hazardous chemicals and air, water and soil pollution and contamination\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training attendees will understand:\r\n\r\n\tTerminology regarding type and components of fire (pre, during, post)\r\n\tClimatic and biophysical conditions pre-, during-, and post-fire\r\n\tThe satellites and instruments used in conducting fire science\r\n\tThe applications of passive and active remote sensing for fires\r\n\tHow to visualize fire emissions and particulate matter\r\n\tThe use of tools for active fires, emissions, and burned areas\r\n\tHow to acquire data for conducting analysis in a given study area\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Six, 2-hour Parts","targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations involved in resource and ecosystem management, health and air quality, disaster risk management, disaster response, and those with an interest in applying remote sensing to fire science.","registrationHowto":"English Session: 11:00-13:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3mak1DS\r\nSpanish Session: 15:00-17:00 EDT (UTC-4): https://go.nasa.gov/3wfzlUf\r\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2030,"created":1619516551000,"updated":1619517189000,"title":"Can we use the operational MetCoOp ensemble to generate background error statistics?","titleNative":"Can we use the operational MetCoOp ensemble to generate background error statistics?","startDate":1620385200000,"endDate":1620388800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"SMHI","contactPerson":"Eumetcal","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.trainingscatalogus.nl/en/Information/Tekstpagina?textpage=TextPage6","description":"In atmospheric data assimilation observation information is distributed in space and between the model state variables using statistical relationships. The currently used methodology to generate data to derive this statistics is similar to the perturbation techniques used in an ensemble. This study investigates the possibility to use data from the MetCoOp operational ensemble MEPS to derive this statistics. The impact on the derived statistics, possible problems and a view on future possibilities will be discussed.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"Find more information and join at https://eumetcal.trainingscatalogus.nl/en/Information/Tekstpagina?textpage=TextPage6.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2037,"created":1620092077000,"updated":1620112938000,"title":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting","titleNative":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence Regional Focus Group meeting","startDate":1620351000000,"endDate":1620354600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Melbourne","host":"Australian VLab Centre of Excellence","contactPerson":"Bodo Zeschke","contactEmail":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","contactUrl":"https://learn.bom.gov.au/mod/bigbluebuttonbn/view.php?id=13874  instructions given in the ","description":"An overview of the Atypical Rapid Intensification Process of Tropical Cyclones (Dr. David Ryglicki, Marine Meteorology Division, Naval Research Laboratory, Monterey, CA)\r\n","country":{"id":"AU","name":"AUSTRALIA","region":"RA-V","type":"Rest of the World"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"Traditionally, tropical cyclone (TCs) intensification is generally favorable when the following environmental conditions are met: the presence of a deep and warm ocean mixed layer, high low- and/or mid-level relative humidity, high upper-level divergence, and low vertical wind shear. More recent research has highlighted a pathway to rapid intensification, henceforth called Atypical Rapid Intensification (RI), that requires the presence of moderate (5-10 m/s) vertical wind shear caused by an upper-level synoptic feature that is vertically shallow  such as an upper-level ridge. This specific environmental set up creates the potential to excite a wobble of the vertical tilt of the TC, thus creating a pulsing convective effect in satellite imagery. The outflow of these wobble-induced convective pulses serves to block the environmental flow, thus lowering the shear and permitting alignment of the TC, ultimately leading to rapid intensification. Current numerical guidance often struggles with accurate forecasts of this process. This talk will highlight observations of features of Atypical RI in various ocean basins, analyze idealized modeling simulations that replicate the most salient features seen in geostationary satellite imagery, and then discuss how these findings can be applied in an operational context. ","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"To register please follow the instructions below:\r\n1.\tThe session homepage is at https://learn.bom.gov.au/mod/bigbluebuttonbn/view.php?id=13874 \r\n2.\tYou can join the session either as a guest or as a registered user. We would prefer you to join as a registered user. There is no cost associated with this. \r\nA.\tFor guest users the session password is VLab@2020\r\nB.\tFor users who choose to join the session as a registered user for the first time, please use the link https://learn.bom.gov.au/login/signup.php to create an account. Please include your details including Country of Origin. After signing up you will receive an automated email with a link to confirm your registration within about 15 minutes. If you have not received the confirmation email within this time, check your spam/junk inbox. If there is no confirmation within your spam/junk inbox, contact moodle@bom.gov.au giving their name and email address, stating they had not received their site registration confirmation email for our training site.\r\nC.\tFor users who have already registered, please click \"Continue\" on the Self enrolment (Student) option. Log in using the above Username and Password. If you have forgotten the password then you can use the 'forgot password' function: https://learn.bom.gov.au/login/forgot_password.php\r\nD.\tAustralian Bureau of Meteorology staff please use the 'Bureau staff log in' button to log in to the site. \r\n\r\nPlease use either the Google Chrome or Modzilla Firefox web browsers when you join the session.\r\n","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5860,"name":"Bodo Zeschke","email":"bodo.zeschke@bom.gov.au","agency":"","country":"AU","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":5,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Australia"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":5,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Australia","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id"