rse","titleNative":"High Impact Weather Summer Course","startDate":1617580800000,"endDate":1620000000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"Eumetcal","contactPerson":"Eumetcal","contactEmail":"info@eumetcal.eu","contactUrl":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/app#/catalog/course/bf6cad61-bc88-4eb5-b0d1-3dab2abaa5b6","description":"This HIW course is an online course for EUMETCAL and will focus on the use of a particular forecasting method based on Doswells ingredient-based forecasting method  IBM (1996). This method assumes that three ingredients are needed for convection: moisture, lift, instability (and vertical wind shear to take the modes in convection into account).\r\n\r\nThese convection modes each have their own distinctive characteristics as regards duration, radar images and, in particular, hazardous weather phenomena (lightning, wind gusts, hail and heavy precipitation). This method has been incorporated into a convection scheme that is currently used at the KNMI forecasting office; it leads, via a few steps, to one of four convection modes. This course e.g. explain the method and describe a number of typically synoptic situations that yield severe convection with some regularity.\r\n\r\nThe use of the convection prediction scheme in forecasting convection leads to a better physical understanding of convection, more objective forecasting methodologies and more accurate forecasts of hazardous weather phenomena. The convection prediction scheme can be used to make a good assessment of the probability of hazardous weather phenomena. The latter are rare in the case of single cells, for example, whereas pulse storms are frequently accompanied by strong downdrafts (wind gusts) and heavy precipitation and supercells are infamous for their prolonged duration, large hail, strong lightning activity and wind gusts.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"1. Determine where and when deep convection will be initiated based on the convection scheme (IBM); they have to found the overlap regions of moisture, instability and lift.\r\n2. Determine which mode of convection can be expected; single cell/ pulse cell, multi cell, squall line or supercell\r\n3. Determine the corresponding hazardous weather","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Primary audience: forecasters working in the operation office of the National Weather Service or Meteorological Centres in Europe. The participants are expected to already have a basic experience in forecasting summer convection.\r\n\r\nSecondary audiences: meteorologists interested in synoptic and mesoscale phenomena (related to summer convection), involved in training, research or management, who would benefit from the course and also provide necessary feedback.","registrationHowto":"https://eumetcal.eu/en/app#/catalog/course/bf6cad61-bc88-4eb5-b0d1-3dab2abaa5b6\r\n\r\nAccount on Eumetcal portal is needed.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5854,"name":"Tomislav Marekovic","email":"marekovic@cirus.dhz.hr","agency":"","country":"HR","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":30,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":1,"value":"Aviation: Aeronautical Meteorological Forecaster"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2020,"created":1616661846000,"updated":1616751478000,"title":"Webinar on Meteosat Third Generation in Africa - Série de webinaires sur l'observation de la Terre en Afrique","titleNative":"Webinar on Meteosat Third Generation in Africa - Série de webinaires sur l'observation de la Terre en Afrique","startDate":1617177600000,"endDate":1617199200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://www.eventsforce.net/eumetsat/24/10401","description":"The Webinar will address some of the challenges related to the transition to MTG in Africa, such as ensuring data access, extraction of suitable science-based products from satellite observations and their integration in actionable information services tailored to African needs. Need for capacity strengthening (user infrastructure and training) will be discussed. \r\n\r\nThis webinar aims at informing stakeholders about Meteosat Third Generation and the benefits of new generation products in various applications in Africa. \r\n\r\nThe Webinar is divided in two parts:\r\n- The first one is dedicated to decision makers;\r\n- The second part is dedicated to National Meteorological Services and Regional Centers. \r\n\r\nLe webinaire abordera certains des défis liés à la transition vers le MTG en Afrique : sécuriser laccès aux données, extraire des produits scientifiques pertinents à partir dobservations satellitaires et les intégrer dans des services dinformation pratiques et adaptés aux besoins du continent africain. La nécessité de renforcer les capacités (infrastructure et formation des utilisateurs) sera également examinée.\r\n\r\nCe webinaire vise à informer les parties prenantes sur le programme Meteosat Troisième Génération et les bénéfices que présentent les produits de cette nouvelle génération de satellites dans diverses applications en Afrique. \r\n\r\nLe webinaire est en deux parties:\r\n- La première partie est dédiée aux décisionnaires;\r\n- La seconde est consacrée aux services météorologiques nationaux et aux centres régionaux.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"- Highlight the socioeconomic benefits of MTG in various weather and climate sensitive socio-economic sectors in Africa;\r\n- Present the new MTG products and their impact in various applications;\r\n- Inform practitioners schedule for the transition from MSG to MTG and on data access mechanisms.\r\n\r\n\r\n- Mettre en évidence les bénéfices socio-économiques du programme MTG dans les différents secteurs socio-économiques sensibles aux conditions météorologiques et climatiques en Afrique\r\n- Présenter les nouveaux produits MTG et leurs impacts dans diverses applications\r\n- Informer les usagers du calendrier de transition de MSG vers MTG et des mécanismes daccès aux données.\r\n\r\n","summary":"The Webinar will address some of the challenges related to the transition to MTG in Africa, such as ensuring data access, extraction of suitable science-based products from satelli","content":"","targetAudience":"Stakeholders and users of MTG data in Africa. ","registrationHowto":"Register to the webinar at https://www.eventsforce.net/eumetsat/24/10401.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":4,"value":"French","iso639":"fr"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2012,"created":1615223164000,"updated":1615225230000,"title":"Introduction to Population Grids and their Integration with Remote Sensing Data for Sustainable Development and Disaster Management","titleNative":"Introduction to Population Grids and their Integration with Remote Sensing Data for Sustainable Development and Disaster Management","startDate":1617112800000,"endDate":1617742800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/3siVcHD","description":"Population distribution data are essential for planning and decision-making in regions faced by crisis or conflict, and they are also useful for monitoring progress towards the sustainable development goals (SDGs). Decision-makers need to know where people are located, what conditions they are facing, what infrastructure is available, and what basic services they can access. And we also need to ensure that no one is left off the map in pursuit of the SDGs or in response to disasters.\r\n\r\nGridded population data, which often uses remote sensing inputs to improve the spatial allocation of population within a country, are vital for all these purposes. Together with the  growing variety of applications that require spatial population data, there is now a bewildering array of population grids, and users need to know which ones are most suitable for their applications.\r\n\r\nThis training will focus on the different global population grids and their application to a range of topics related to development planning and monitoring of the SDGs (e.g.,  environment, hazards, and access to resources). Attendees will be exposed to the latest data and methods used to produce global grids, how the grids incorporate remote sensing inputs, and how population grids can be used in conjunction with other types of data.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goal: \r\n\tGoal 9: Build resilient infrastructure, promote inclusive and sustainable industrialization and foster innovation\r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, participants will:\r\n\r\n\tIdentify what are and what distinguishes the various population grids\r\n\tBecome familiar with POPGRIDs online for visualization and intercomparison of population grids for specific regions and types of data of interest\r\n\tIdentify uses of population grids in different application areas (sustainable development, health, environment, emergency management, and humanitarian relief)\r\n\tRecognize what disaster risk reduction exposure data is and how it is used in the human and economic loss due to disaster\r\n\tLearn the basic process of developing exposure data and how remote sensing data could contribute to it\r\n\tUnderstand disaster risk reduction uncertainty and the appropriate use of results\r\n\tDescribe SDG indicator 9.1.1 (Rural Access Index) and how it is measured\r\n\tIdentify the inputs used to calculate the Rural Access Index (RAI)\r\n\tRecognize the advantages and the limitations of using currently available open global data sources to calculate RAI\r\n\tExplain why the choice of gridded population input can lead to different results for this simple indicator\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Two, 2-hour parts","targetAudience":"National Statistical Office representatives, government agency technical staff, national policymakers involved in sustainable development, health, environment, emergency management, and humanitarian relief.","registrationHowto":"Times: 10:00-12:00 & 15:00-17:00 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day. Participants need only to register and attend one daily session.\r\n\r\nRegister Here: https://go.nasa.gov/3siVcHD\r\n","registrationEndDate":1617667200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":2014,"created":1615276503000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1617105600000,"endDate":1617109200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining kno