services and data offers.","summary":null,"content":"Overview of the WEkEO service (which data and cloud processing capabilities are available, what are the free and paid plans, user support and training agenda)  30 mins\r\nOnline demonstration of the WEkEO web portal (view, discover, select and download data, register for free plans, register for paid plans, run a Jupyter Notebook, ask for virtual machines, and run my application on virtual machines)  40 mins\r\nQ&A  20 mins\r\n","targetAudience":"New users","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://www.wekeo.eu/web/guest/news/-/blogs/wekeo-online-demo-july-2nd","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5850,"name":"Hayley Evers-King","email":"Hayley.EversKing@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1898,"created":1591649118000,"updated":1591697709000,"title":"Understanding Phenology with Remote Sensing","titleNative":"Understanding Phenology with Remote Sensing","startDate":1593529200000,"endDate":1594742400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/2Z1Zdoq","description":"This training will focus on the use of remote sensing to understand phenology: the study of life-cycle events. Phenological patterns and processes can vary greatly across a range of spatial and temporal scales, and can provide insights about ecological processes like invasive species encroachment, drought, wildlife habitat, and wildfire potential. This training will highlight NASA-funded tools to observe and study phenology across a range of scales. Attendees will be exposed to the latest in phenological observatory networks and science, and how these observations relate to ecosystem services, the carbon cycle, biodiversity, and conservation.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\r\nGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts Goal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training series, attendees will be able to:\r\n\r\nSummarize NASA satellites and sensors that can be used for monitoring global phenology patterns\r\n\r\nOutline the benefits and limitations of NASA data for phenology\r\n\r\nDescribe the multi-scalar approach to vegetation life cycle analyses\r\n\r\nCompare and contrast data from multiple phenology networks\r\n\r\nEvaluate various projects and case-study examples of phenological data","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Three, one-hour sessions\r\n","targetAudience":"This training is designed for individuals and organizations interested in using satellite imagery for mapping vegetation health and seasonal patterns. ","registrationHowto":"Register Here: https://go.nasa.gov/2Z1Zdoq","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1897,"created":1591648693000,"updated":1591698208000,"title":"Groundwater Monitoring using Observations from NASAs Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) Missions","titleNative":"El Monitoreo de Aguas Subterráneas Usando Observaciones de las Misiones Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) de la NASA","startDate":1593108000000,"endDate":1593113400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/2ZNDBMU","description":"Groundwater makes up roughly 30% of global freshwater. It also provides drinking water for the worlds population, and irrigation for close to 1/3rd of global agricultural land. Because of this level of reliance, monitoring groundwater is crucial for water resources and land management. The Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) and GRACE-Follow On (GRACE-FO) missions from NASA and the German Research Centre for Geosciences (GFZ) provide large-scale terrestrial water storage estimation from mid-2000 to present. The mission uses twin satellites to accurately map variations in the Earth's gravity field and surface mass distribution.\r\n\r\nGRACE observations have been used for detecting groundwater depletion and for drought and flood predictions. This lightning-style training is designed to answer the demand and interest from the applications community in technologies that can be used to support water resources management. The webinar will provide an overview of the GRACE missions, groundwater data availability, and their applications in the monitoring and management of water resources. This lightning webinar will also serve as the foundation for the upcoming advanced webinar: Using Earth Observations to Monitor Water Budgets for River Basin Management II.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\nTarget 6.4: By 2030, substantially increase water-use efficiency across all sectors and ensure sustainable withdrawals and supply of freshwater to address water scarcity and substantially reduce the number of people suffering from water scarcity","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, attendees will be able to access GRACE data and analyze regional groundwater changes","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: A single, 1.5-hour webinar that includes a lecture and a question & answer session; One session offered in English (11:00-12:30 ET) and one in Spanish (14:00-15:30 ET)\r\n","targetAudience":"Audience: The content of this training was developed for local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations engaged in the management of water resources, irrigation, and agricultural management.","registrationHowto":"Register Here: Spanish (14:00-15:30 ET): https://go.nasa.gov/2ZNDBMU","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1896,"created":1591647969000,"updated":1591698303000,"title":"Groundwater Monitoring using Observations from NASAs Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) Missions ","titleNative":"Groundwater Monitoring using Observations from NASAs Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) Missions ","startDate":1593097200000,"endDate":1593102600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/2z7Blp3","description":"Groundwater makes up roughly 30% of global freshwater. It also provides drinking water for the worlds population, and irrigation for close to 1/3rd of global agricultural land. Because of this level of reliance, monitoring groundwater is crucial for water resources and land management. The Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) and GRACE-Follow On (GRACE-FO) missions from NASA and the German Research Centre for Geosciences (GFZ) provide large-scale terrestrial water storage estimation from mid-2000 to present. The mission uses twin satellites to accurately map variations in the Earth's gravity field and surface mass distribution.\r\n\r\nGRACE observations have been used for detecting groundwater depletion and for drought and flood predictions. This lightning-style training is designed to answer the demand and interest from the applications community in technologies that can be used to support water resources management. The webinar will provide an overview of the GRACE missions, groundwater data availability, and their applications in the monitoring and management of water resources. This lightning webinar will also serve as the foundation for the upcoming advanced webinar: Using Earth Observations to Monitor Water Budgets for River Basin Management II.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals: \r\nTarget 6.4: By 2030, substantially increase water-use efficiency across all sectors and ensure sustainable withdrawals and supply of freshwater to address water scarcity and substantially reduce the number of people suffering from water scarcity","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, attendees will be able to access GRACE data and analyze regional groundwater changes\r\n","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: A single, 1.5-hour webinar that includes a lecture and a question & answer session; One session offered in English (11:00-12:30 ET) and one in Spanish (14:00-15:30 ET)","targetAudience":"The content of this training was developed for local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations engaged in the management of water resources, irrigation, and agricultural management.","registrationHowto":"Register Here: English (11:00-12:30 ET); https://go.nasa.gov/2z7Blp3","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":110,"value":"NASA","visible":true},{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true},{"id":130,"value":"GEOS","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1900,"created":1592517572000,"updated":1592767065000,"title":"Tropical Waves and TUTT Induced Perturbations - 15:00 UTC","titleNative":"Tropical Waves and TUTT Induced Perturbations - 15:00 UTC","startDate":1593010800000,"endDate":1593102600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NOAA/WPC International Desk","contactPerson":"Michel Davison","contactEmail":"michel.davison@noaa.gov","contactUrl":"https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/3590440234827057680","description":"The session is for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean) and useful for other Regions. This is a two part training seminar, with the first part on June 24 at 15 UTC covering theoretical aspects and characteristics of the Tropical Waves and TUTT induced perturbations in the easterly trades.  The second part is scheduled on June 25 at 15 UTC, and we will engage you to apply the information from the first day in practical exercises. The sessions will open 15 minutes earlier (at 14:45 UTC for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection).  GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet.  ","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Utilize conceptual models, numerical weather model output, and satellite imagery to identify and distinguish between easterly/tropical waves and Tropical Upper Tropospheric Troughs (TUTT).","summary":null,"content":"As we transition to the rainy season across the Caribbean Basin and northern South America, meteorologists will pay particular attention to the impact easterly/tropical waves and Tropical Upper Tropospheric Troughs (TUTT) might have on local weather conditions.  In this special session we will explore conceptual models, numerical weather applications and satellite meteorology techniques to differentiate between the two different atmospheric features.","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/3590440234827057680\r\nThis training seminar is intended for meteorologists and meteorological technicians experienced in the analysis and interpretation of satellite images/boundary detection techniques.  As a prerequisite for the course, we recommend the MetEd (COMET) training module on African Easterly Waves: https://www.meted.ucar.edu/training_module.php?id=997","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":8,"value":"PWS: Weather Broadcasters and Communicators"},{"id":11,"value":"Marine: Marine Weather Forecaster"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":1,"value":"Aviation: Aeronautical Meteorological Forecaster"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1869,"created":1581595127000,"updated":1588069236000,"title":"Train the Trainers: Tools & Techniques for Teaching about Sentinel-3 Marine Data","titleNative":"Train the Trainers: Tools & Techniques for Teaching about Sentinel-3 Marine Data","startDate":1592784000000,"endDate":1594339200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Hayley Evers-King","contactEmail":"hayley.eversking@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"COVID-19/SARS-2: Due to the situation with COVID-19, this training course will now take place entirely online through webinars, tutorials, demos and forums (particularly for software installations), and 1:1 meetings.\r\n\r\nThis course offers participants the opportunity to work with other trainers to incorporate the use of Copernicus marine data from EUMETSAT in to their own educational activities.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"After the course participants will have the knowledge, skills and experience to be able:\r\n- to teach the use of Copernicus Sentinel-3 and CMEMS data for marine applications, covering the use of sea surface temperature, altimetry and ocean colour data, as well as downstream products and model outputs.\r\n- to teach others how to access the Copernicus Marine Data through online portals, APIs and other methods.  \r\n- to teach the basic principles of the processing methods applied to the Copernicus Sentinel-3 instruments (and similar sensors) to derive the marine products.\r\n- to explain how to assess data quality (including basic principles of validation) and select appropriate products for different tasks. \r\nto understand the use of the following tools in training  SNAP, Remote Sensing Digital Teaching Tool, Git, Python (and Jupyter notebooks), cloud computing (WEkEO).\r\n- Select and adapt existing resources and develop new resources for their own training activities.","summary":null,"content":"Participants will learn how to use open access resources available through EUMETSAT and the Copernicus programme, and co-develop their own resources with a network of fellow participants and trainers from IODE and EUMETSAT. ","targetAudience":"This course is aimed at:\r\n- University teachers, mentors and tutors\r\n- Staff from National Oceanographic centres and marine research/management institutions with teaching/training responsibilities\r\n\r\nApplicants are expected to:\r\n- have an understanding of marine science and satellite based Earth Observation\r\n- have teaching experience or currently preparing for a teaching / training roles\r\n- have a working knowledge of English","registrationHowto":"Applications are welcomed through the UNESCO/IOC/IODE Ocean Teacher Global Academy: https://www.oceanexpert.net/event/2627#overview","registrationEndDate":1589068800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":3925,"name":"Kim Hockley","email":"khock@pml.ac.uk","agency":"EUM","country":"GB","programme":"PML","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1881,"created":1586938372000,"updated":1586944172000,"title":"The International Distance Training Course on The Basic Principles of Satellite Remote-sensing","titleNative":"&#31532;14&#26399;&#39118;&#20113;&#21355;&#26143;&#20135;&#21697;&#24212;&#29992;&#36828;&#31243;&#22269;&#38469;&#22521;&#35757;&#29677;","startDate":1592784000000,"endDate":1593734400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"CMATC/WMO RTC-Beijing","contactPerson":"DENG Jingmian","contactEmail":"applycmatc@cma.gov.cn","contactUrl":"http://mooc.cmatc.cma.cn/course/view.php?id=32&isStay=true&lang=en","description":"The course is aimed to enhance the trainees' basic knowledge and skills in the interpretation of meteorological satellite data, and the application of products in supporting weather analysis, weather forecasting, environmental monitoring and disaster risk management. Trainees will be expected to improve the capabilities of data acquisition and assimilation, utility of satellite monitoring system, as well as the ability in satellite remote-sensing application.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By studying the online courseware, participants will be able to: \r\n1) apply the basic theory and principles of radiation to the interpretation of meteorological satellite imagery and products;\r\n2) access, select, display and manipulate satellite data;\r\n3) carry out weather analysis and forecasting by using meteorological satellite imagery and data;\r\n4) use Satellite Weather Application Platform (SWAP) and Satellite Monitoring Analysis Remote-sensing Toolkit (SMART) which will cover laboratory practice for torrential rain and strong convective weather monitoring, wild fire monitoring, and drought monitoring;\r\n5) have a better understanding of the Fengyun (FY) satellites and the current and future products of geostationary satellite products such as wind profiling radar (WPR), cloud spectral image, lightning image observation, space weather monitoring.","summary":null,"content":"The training course includes:\r\n1) Satellite cloud imagery recognition and interpretation;\r\n2) Satellite Weather Application Platform (SWAP) and Satellite Monitoring Analysis Remote-sensing Toolkit (SMART);\r\n3) Case study on satellite products application in disaster mitigation and prevention;\r\n4) Introduction to the latest development of Fengyun satellites and the application of FY products, especially the geostationary satellite products.","targetAudience":"Officials, specialists, forecasters engaged in satellite meteorology and disaster risk management at National Hydrological and Meteorological Services or equivalent institutions.","registrationHowto":"1) Please visit http://mooc.cmatc.cma.cn/course/view.php?id=32&isStay=true&lang=en and create your own account. Use CMATC2020 as the enrollment key to get access to the course.\r\n2) Please note that the applicants should register f