"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"After the course participants will have the skills to:\r\n Understand what data and products the Copernicus Marine Data Stream provides (variables, timeliness, resolution, data formats);\r\n Select from the Copernicus Marine Data Stream appropriate products for specific tasks;\r\n Access / download data and products provided in the Copernicus Marine Data Stream (CODA, EUMETSAT Data archive, EumetCAST)\r\n Know how to work with CMDS data in SNAP, GIS, and Python (Jupyter Notebooks)\r\n Use data / products from the Copernicus Marine Data Stream for their purposes. ","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"The workshop is aimed at, but not restricted to, early career scientists: Masters / PhD students, Post-Docs, and/or experienced scientists from any relevant sector (industry, government, environment, research) who have clear motivations for wanting to work with satellite-based ocean observation data. Participants should have introductory experience using SNAP and/or a scripting language such as Python (or similar), but this is not a prerequisite.","registrationHowto":"Applications are now closed.","registrationEndDate":1585353600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":3925,"name":"Kim Hockley","email":"khock@pml.ac.uk","agency":"EUM","country":"GB","programme":"PML","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1891,"created":1589208323000,"updated":1589227064000,"title":"WMO 2020 Online Course on Education and Training Innovations","titleNative":"WMO 2020 Online Course on Education and Training Innovations","startDate":1590364800000,"endDate":1596153600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"World Meteorological Organisation (WMO)","contactPerson":"Patrick Parrish","contactEmail":"PParrish@wmo.int","contactUrl":"https://www.wmo.int/edistrib_exped/grp_prs/_en/08226-2020-SEM-120_en.pdf","description":"A special online training course for trainers in all WMO Regions will be offered, in English, and will focus on the implementation of innovations in education and training. The course is based on the forthcoming publication, the WMO Global Campus Innovations, and is offered in both open and certificate paths.  \r\n\r\nThe course will be offered over 9 weeks (Annex I), from 25 May to 31 July 2020, with the core period running from 1 June to 24 July 2020, including a pre-course preparatory period from 25  to 29 May 2020, and a final closing week from 27 to 31 July 2020 for those wishing to obtain a Certificate of Completion.\r\n\r\nThe two possible paths are as follows:\r\n- An open path, for those wishing to participate only in sessions of interest, and do not require a certificate of completion,\r\n- A certificate path, which will require several short written Reflection Papers and assigned readings, as well as a short project, in the form of an Innovation Implementation plan for their organization.\r\n\r\nWorkplace changes are occurring more rapidly each day, calling for significant innovations in the way we educate and train people for their professional careers. The new global and digital economies call for fluidity, resilience, and readiness for rapid reskilling to meet changing demands. The goal of this online course is to promote innovation in education and training practices by developing skills and strategies for implementing innovations. You will do this primarily by reviewing stories of innovations implemented by WMO Member institutions, which have been collected in the WMO Global Campus Innovations, as well as by reviewing innovation processes and using a template for planning to implement an innovation. After participating in this online course, participants will have gained ideas for innovations that may be useful in their organization, and developed  skills for planning and implementing an innovation. The knowledge and skills gained will contribute to the development of the competencies identified in the WMO Competency Requirements for Education and Training Providers for Meteorological, Hydrological and Climate Services, as approved by EC-65, and as described in WMO-No. 1209, Compendium of WMO Competency Frameworks.  \r\n\r\nThe course is designed to be practical and highly active, but will rely on participant contributions to make it so. It will include many opportunities for interaction with facilitators, authors of the publication and fellow participants.\r\n\r\nDuring the course  participants will review and discuss chapters of interest. Those in the Certificate Path will have short, one-page writing assignments about selected readings and a short final project. For final projects, participants will be expected to work either independently or in small groups from the same institution.\r\n\r\nBoth trainers and managers responsible for planning, designing and decision-making regarding training will benefit from this online event. Spaces for participants in the Certificate Path will be limited to 50.  \r\n\r\nAll Certificate Path participants will be expected to dedicate a minimum of 3 hours each week during the 8 core weeks to enable completion of readings, writings and project tasks. It is critical to the success of this course that this time is made available to participants apart from within their weekly work responsibilities. For this reason, all nominations from Permanent Representatives will require acknowledgement of this time requirement for the Certificate Path. Participants will be enrolled in the WMO Education and Training Programme e-Learning Website and also expected to undertake the short Pre-Course preparatory work.  \r\n\r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"1. Describe a variety of education and training innovations being applied in\r\ncontemporary education and training institutions.\r\n2. Link education and training innovations described in the chapters to your own\r\npractice.\r\n3. Apply a systems approach to analyzing the context of learning and their education\r\nand training development processes.\r\n4. Use a systematic process to write an implement plans for innovations.\r\n5. Create learning opportunities utilizing new methods, curricula revisions, or new\r\ntechnologies.\r\n6. Recognize the value of collaboration in education and training development and\r\ndelivery","summary":null,"content":"Content: \r\n- Volume I: New Pedagogical Approaches\r\n- Volume II: Curriculum Advancements\r\n- Volume III: Collaboration in Education and Training\r\n- Volume IV: Technology-Enhanced Learning\r\n- Certificate Track only: Innovation Implementation Plan sharing and discussion","targetAudience":"Trainers and managers responsible for planning, designing and decision-making regarding training in all WMO Regions. ","registrationHowto":"A Nomination Form for participants in the Online Course is available at https://www.wmo.int/edistrib_exped/grp_prs/_en/08226-2020-SEM-120_en.pdf. \r\n\r\nNote that only the first section of the form is required for those in the Open Path. For Certificate Path participants, when completing the Nomination Form, you are requested to provide information on how participation will benefit your Service, how the skills and knowledge will be used upon completion of the course and whether agreement has been made to provide the time to complete the course. A note containing more specific information for participants will be distributed to selected candidates at a later date.\r\n\r\nThe Nomination Form should be completed and returned to reach the WMO Secretariat (tra@wmo.int) as soon as possible and not later than 20 May 2020. ","registrationEndDate":1589932800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":100,"value":"WMO","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":15,"value":"Training: Education and Training Providers "}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1879,"created":1586827578000,"updated":1586856453000,"title":"RFG of Americas and the Caribbean - 15:00 UTC","titleNative":"RFG of Americas and the Caribbean - 15:00 UTC","startDate":1589986800000,"endDate":1589990400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NOAA, CIRA, and CoEs: Costa Rica/Barbados/Argentina/Brazil","contactPerson":"Bernie Connell","contactEmail":"bernie.connell@colostate.edu","contactUrl":"https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/3172853413762613516","description":"The sessions are bilingual (English and Spanish) and for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean).  Session time is 15:00 UTC, but as usual, it will start 15 minutes earlier (at 14:45 UTC for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection).  GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet.  Images during the session are from SLIDER: http://rammb-slider.cira.colostate.edu  More Information: http://rammb.cira.colostate.edu/training/rmtc/focusgroup.asp","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/3172853413762613516","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1889,"created":1588945154000,"updated":1589210291000,"title":"African Weather Briefing","titleNative":"African Weather Briefing","startDate":1589972400000,"endDate":1589976000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"South African Weather Service","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://eumetsat.zoom.us/meeting/register/tJUqdeyvqz4qH9c5MISYC59KXgxqcxsCMvOY","description":"Hosted by Lee-ann Simpson, SAWS, this weather briefing will bring together African forecasters. This briefing is associated with the ongoing online Satellite Application Course, and is open for anyone. ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Being able to interpret the current weather systems in Africa with the help of satellite imagery.","summary":null,"content":"Weather briefing will be recorded. ","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are operational meteorologists.","registrationHowto":"Use the link provided to register for the session. ","registrationEndDate":1589932800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1850,"created":1578387392000,"updated":1588000717000,"title":"Short Course on EUMETSATs Climate Data Records: Using satellite data for climate applications","titleNative":"Short Course on EUMETSATs Climate Data Records: Using satellite data for climate applications","startDate":1589877000000,"endDate":1589895000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Christine Traeger-Chatterjee","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"Link to the registration will be provided here by 1 May 2020","description":"The course will be accessible from online. The link to the registration page will be provided by 1 May 2020.\r\nThe number of participants is limited to 30. First come first serve.\r\n\r\nSatellite based climate data records play an increasing role in climate monitoring and help to answer climate related questions. Nowadays satellite based climate data records cover a time period of several decades. EUMETSAT and its Satellite Application Facilities (SAF) provide a number of high quality climate data records for various geophysical variables, such as solar radiation, land surface temperature, cloud fractional cover, cloud microphysical variables, and many more, derived from both, geostationary and polar orbiting satellites.\r\n\r\nThese climate data records are free and open to everyone. They continue to be reprocessed to account for improvements of the algorithm and to include recent time periods. In addition to the data, free software tools, such as the CM SAF R Toolbox, are developed and provided by the SAFs for users to work with the data.\r\n\r\nThis short course is an opportunity to get an overview about the climate data records available from the EUMETSAT Satellite Application Facilities, learn how to access them and gain some first experiences in how to work with the software tools provided. Participants will have the opportunity do some hands-on exercises using the data and tools provided. Participants are also welcome to bring their own scientific questions, for which the satellite based CDRs may help to find the answer. Data and software developers will be around to help and answer questions.\r\n\r\nParticipants should be prepared to bring a laptop to the course, to install the software tools beforehand and to download some data of interest from the CM SAF.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"After this short course participants will be able to:\r\n- find climate data records provided by EUMETSAT and its Satellite Application Facilities\r\n- visualise maps and time series of gridded climate data records using the CM SAF R Toolbox","summary":"The course will be accessible from online. The link to the registration page will be provided by 1 May 2020.\r\nThe number of participants is limited to 30. First come first serve.\r\n","content":"PLEASE NOTE: we are currently re-designing the course to make it available online.\r\n\r\nParticipants will learn about climate data records from satellites, where to find and download them and how to work with them using the CM SAF R Toolbox.","targetAudience":"(Early Career) Scientists interested in working with Climate Data Records from satellites.","registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5847,"name":"Christine Traeger Chatterjee","email":"christine.traeger@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":9,"value":"Climate Services: Provision of Climate Services"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1807,"created":1569234758000,"updated":1588164289000,"title":"EGU Short Course: Using Copernicus Marine Data: Satellite data for ocean applications","titleNative":"EGU Short Course: Using Copernicus Marine Data: Satellite data for ocean applications","startDate":1589839200000,"endDate":1588896000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Hayley Evers-King","contactEmail":"hayley.eversking@external.eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"The course will be accessible from online from\r\nhttps://egu2020.eu/sharing_geoscience_online.html\r\n\r\nUsing Copernicus Marine Data: Satellite data for ocean applications. We are running this short course on data access and processing, in the form of an online workshop. More information will be provided here as soon as they become available. ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"How to access, download and process Sentinel-3 data\r\nIntroduction to WekEO and remote processing systems","summary":null,"content":"PLEASE NOTE: we are currently re-designing the course to make it available online.\r\n\r\nThe sessions make use of the WeKEO DIAS hosted processing, Sentinel Applications Platform (SNAP) software, and Python programming. No experience is necessary as various exercises will be provided for a wide range of skill levels and applications, however participants should bring their own laptops and be prepared to install open source software in advance.\r\n","targetAudience":"Academia - students, early career scientists, trainers, lecturers, senior academics.","registrationHowto":"Please register at:  https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158#section-1. The number of participants is limited to 30 (first come first served).","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":3864,"name":"Lauren Biermann","email":"lbi@pml.ac.uk","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"GB","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1888,"created":1588254298000,"updated":1588255840000,"title":"Forest Mapping and Monitoring with SAR Data","titleNative":"Forest Mapping and Monitoring with SAR Data","startDate":1589292000000,"endDate":1588269600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA ARSET ","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://go.nasa.gov/2vtQn6t","description":"Measurements of forest cover and change are vital to understanding the global carbon cycle and the contribution of forests to carbon sequestration. Many nations are engaged in international agreements, such as the Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Degradation (REDD+) initiative, which includes tracking annual deforestation rates and developing early warning systems of forest loss. Remote sensing data are integral to data collection for these metrics, however, the use of optical remote sensing for monitoring forest health can be challenging in tropical, cloud-prone regions.\r\n\r\nRadar remote sensing overcomes these challenges because of its ability to see the surface through clouds or regardless of day or night conditions. In addition, the radar signal can penetrate through the vegetation canopy and provide information relevant to structure and density.\r\n\r\nThis advanced webinar series will introduce participants to 1.) SAR time series analysis of forest change using Google Earth Engine (GEE), 2.) land cover classification with radar and optical data with GEE, 3.) mapping mangroves with SAR, and 4.) forest stand height estimation with SAR. Each training will include a theoretical portion describing the use of SAR for landcover mapping as related to the focus of the session followed by a demonstration that will show participants how to access, download, and analyze SAR data for forest mapping and monitoring. These demonstrations will use freely-available, open-source data and software.\r\n\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\r\n\r\nTarget 15.1: By 2020, ensure the conservation, restoration and sustainable use of terrestrial and inland freshwater ecosystems and their services, in particular forests, wetlands, mountains and drylands, in line with obligations under international agreements\r\nTarget 15.2: By 2020, promote the implementation of sustainable management of all types of forests, halt deforestation, restore degraded forests and substantially increase afforestation and reforestation globally\r\n\r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, attendees will be able to:\r\n\tInterpret radar data for forest mapping\r\n\tUnderstand how radar data can be applied to land cover mapping\r\n\tBecome familiar with open source tools used to analyze radar data\r\n\tConduct a land cover classification with radar and optical data\r\n\tMap mangrove forests with radar data\r\n\tUnderstand how forest stand height can be mapped using radar data\r\n\tApply SAR time-series analysis to map forest change\r\n\tLearn about upcoming radar missions at NASA","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: \r\n\tFour parts with sessions offered in English and Spanish\r\n\tFour exercises\r\n\tOne Google Form homework","targetAudience":"Local, regional, state, federal, and non-governmental forest and environmental managers already working with satellite remote sensing datasets for forest monitoring.","registrationHowto":"Register Here: https://go.nasa.gov/2vtQn6t","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1865,"created":1580292679000,"updated":1752481132000,"title":"User workshop and training on fire monitoring products","titleNative":"User workshop and training on fire monitoring products","startDate":1589155200000,"endDate":1589846400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Federico Fierli and Anu-Maja Sundstrom","contactEmail":"federico.fierli@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://ec.europa.eu/eusurvey/runner/Fire_Workshop_EUM_SAFs","description":"This is an on-line course\r\n\r\nFire emissions pose serious concerns for public health, natural resources, ecosytems and climate change. In turn, a changing climate with prolonged and more frequent heatwaves, changes in atmospheric regimes, and land aridity increases the risk of fires and the regional extent and duration of the fire season. This leads to an increased need for National Services to provide and communicate about fire risk, nowcasting and impacts to a wide range of users. Some of these needs are tackled with dedicated Copernicus services for emergency management and monitoring the atmosphere at the global and Europe-wide scale. Services such as the European Forest Fire Information System (EFFIS), which also contributes to the Global Wildfire Information System (GWIS), a joint initiative between Copernicus, GEO and NASA, provide a comprehensive view and evaluation of fire regimes and at global level including selected data from satellite. Use of emissions from Global Fire assimilation System (GFAS) is implemented in GWIS. Satellite observations can respond to these needs thanks to their great potential for nowcasting, decision support and providing key information on the fire impact assessment. \r\nData engaged are Multi thematic products relevant for fire monitoring and assessment:\r\n\tVegetation and land (LAND-SAF)\r\n\tAtmospheric aerosol and compounds (AC-SAF, S5P)\r\n\tCopernicus Services:\r\no\tCAMS fire and smoke products\r\no\tCAMS Atmospheric Data\r\no\tForest Fire information system EFFIS\r\no\tCopernicus satellite products (Sentinel-3 FRP)\r\nTools & Platform: \r\n\tWeb-distributes services and resources \r\n\tExample on advanced data handling data with notebook embedding Python code (LTPY)\r\n\tTentative: use of the WEkEO, Atmospheric Data Store \r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"\tFamiliarization with concepts on detection of Fire, related emissions and impacts\r\n\tIdentify fire signature(s) in data\r\n\tHandle products with tools\r\n\tMaintain a two-way interaction with users gathering methods and needs\r\n","summary":null,"content":"\tLectures with interactive data discovery and User practices\r\no\t1 hour per dataset/service with live demonstration on data\r\n\tUse case implementation\r\no\tDefinition of a flow to reproduce an event, from risk / forecast to post-fire assessment using all data presented\r\n\tUser feedback round-table\r\no\tWash-up and consultation on the emerging needs around data access, handling, expert advice, training\r\n","targetAudience":"Service providers (Forecasters, Fire monitoring)\r\nNumber of attendees: up to 20\r\nRegion: Nordic and Scandinavian Area (Sweden, Finland, Denmark, Norway, Iceland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania)\r\n","registrationHowto":"Contact: training@eumetsat.int","registrationEndDate":1585612800000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email":"deleted.trainer@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"--","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":180,"value":"Copernicus","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1887,"created":1588020823000,"updated":1588025365000,"title":"New Set of Forecasting Tools developed by the NOAA/WPC International Desks - 15:00 UTC","titleNative":"Nuevo grupo de herramientas de pronóstico desarrollado por los Escritorios Internacionales del NOAA/WPC","startDate":1588777200000,"endDate":1588780800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NOAA/WPC International Desk","contactPerson":"Jose Galvez","contactEmail":"jose.galvez@noaa.gov","contactUrl":"https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2539627075444206603","description":"The session is for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean) and usefull for others.  Session time is 15:00 UTC, it will start 15 minutes earlier (at 14:45 UTC for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection).  GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet.  ","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Using 5 new diagnostic tools derived from numerical model output, assess 1) the strength of the trade wind inversion, 2) the potential for severe weather and hail, 3) the location of surface fronts, 4) the potential for heavy rainfall and thunderstorms, and 5) the rainfall accumulation potential for the Andes.","summary":null,"content":"Como parte de la misión de mejorar las prácticas de pronóstico del tiempo en las Américas, los Escritorios Internacionales del WPC han desarrollado cinco herramientas diagnósticas adicionales usando el software Wingridds. Luego de evaluación rutinaria informal, la consideración de estas herramientas ha producido una mejora notable en la calidad de los pronósticos de los Escritorios, así como en los pronósticos de algunos de nuestros socios nacionales e internacionales. Estos algoritmos incluyen: (1) TWIN: Intensidad y elevación de la inversión termal de los alisios; (2) GR02T: Riesgo de convección severa y específicamente de granizo; (3) FRONT: Detección de frentes en superficie; (4) EGDI: Potencial de lluvias intensas y tipos de convección; (5) CAPI: Potencial de acumulaciones de precipitación en los Andes Centrales. Se ofrecen dos presentaciones de una hora de duración cada una: La versión en inglés se presentará el 29 de abril de 2020 a las 15 UTC y la versión en español el 6 de mayo de 2020 a las 15 UTC. Dadas las limitaciones de tiempo para tanta cantidad de material, cada presentación será introductoria. Se cubrirán los conceptos básicos detrás de cada algoritmo, interpretación de los productos, métodos para considerar los productos para mejorar los pronósticos y acceso a productos a tiempo real programados usando salidas del modelo GFS.: https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/international/wng/ .","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Regístrese: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2539627075444206603","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":8,"value":"PWS: Weather Broadcasters and Communicators"},{"id":11,"value":"Marine: Marine Weather Forecaster"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":1,"value":"Aviation: Aeronautical Meteorological Forecaster"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1838,"created":1576672333000,"updated":1585754497000,"title":"Online RA-I Meteorological Satellite Applications 2020","titleNative":"Online RA-I Meteorological Satellite Applications 2020","startDate":1588550400000,"endDate":1593129600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"EUMETSAT Training Team","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"This satellite applications course will help you to improve your skills in identifying key weather systems and features found in Africa and their characteristics in satellite imagery and products. You will develop professional relationships with course participants  and explore the use of a variety of web and mobile-based applications for communication and learning together.  ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Improved skills in: (a) Identifying key weather systems and features found in Africa and their characteristics in Satellite imagery and products; (b) Being a competent user of virtual course technology; (c) Developing relationships with course participants via the discussion forums and online briefings.\r\n","summary":null,"content":"In this course selected synoptic scale as well as mesoscale weather systems are addressed. \r\nThe course only addresses system/feature identification in satellite imagery, it does not cover forecasting.","targetAudience":"Instructors in satellite meteorology, operational forecasters or experienced satellite data users, with the ability to pass on skills acquired at the course to other operational staff.","registrationHowto":"With the WMO/VLab Centres of Excellence in training on satellite meteorology, the European Organisation for the Exploitation of Meteorological Satellites (EUMETSAT) will organize an online training course for the benefit of the African meteorological user community.  \r\n\r\nIn previous years this Satellite Application Courses would include face-to-face workshops at the various CoEs, but in light of the current global call for social distancing, there will be no face-to-face workshops considered for the 2020 course. \r\n\r\nThe 2020 course will be conducted exclusively online, with facilitators adjusting the delivery method and the assessment methods and tools to continue to engage with participants and to effectively teach satellite applications and skills.   \r\n\r\nThe online course will take place from 4 May to 26 June 2020.  Candidates are requested to apply to the online course by completing an online questionnaire. Applications open in early April 2020 at <a href=\"http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158\">http://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158</a>","registrationEndDate":1587949200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":11,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence South Africa","visible":false},{"id":8,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Kenya","visible":false},{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1877,"created":1585755033000,"updated":1736352746000,"title":"Satellite Application online course for RA-I","titleNative":"Cours enligne: Applications de la météorologie satellitaire pour RA-I","startDate":1588550400000,"endDate":1593129600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EAMAC","contactPerson":"Jose Prieto","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://training.eumetsat.int/course/view.php?id=158","description":"Ce cours sur les applications satellitaires vous aidera à améliorer vos compétences en matière d'identification des phénomènes et systèmes météorologiques clés que l'on trouve en Afrique et de leurs caractéristiques dans les images et produits satellitaires. Vous développerez des relations professionnelles avec les participants au cours et explorerez l'utilisation d'une variété d'applications web et mobiles pour communiquer et apprendre ensemble.   ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Amélioration des compétences en : (a) Identifier les principaux systèmes et caractéristiques météorologiques en Afrique et leurs caractéristiques dans l'imagerie et les produits satellitaires ; (b) Être un utilisateur compétent de la technologie des cours virtuels ; (c) Développer des relations avec les participants aux cours via les forums de discussion et les briefings en ligne.","summary":"Ce cours sur les applications satellitaires vous aidera à améliorer vos compétences en matière d'identification des phénomènes et systèmes météorologiques clés que l'on trouve en A","content":"Dans ce cours, certains situations météorologiques à échelle synoptique et à méso-échelle sont abordés.\r\nLe cours traite uniquement de l'identification des systèmes/fonctions sur les images satellites, il ne couvre pas la prévision.","targetAudience":"Instructeurs en météorologie satellitaire, des prévisionnistes opérationnels ou des utilisateurs de données satellitaires, avec la possibilité de transmettre les compétences acquises lors du cours à d'autres membres du personnel opérationnel.","registrationHowto":"En coopération avec les centres d'excellence (laboratoire virtuel de la formation satellitaire de lOMM), l'Organisation Européenne pour l'Exploitation de Satellites Météorologiques (EUMETSAT) organise une  formation au profit des utilisateurs de la communauté météorologique africaine. Le cours sera dédié pour des participants francophones. \r\n\r\n \r\nLes années précédentes, ce cours sur les applications satellitaires comprenait des ateliers face-à-face dans les différents CdE's, mais à la lumière de l'appel mondial actuel à la distance sociale, aucun atelier face à face ne sera envisagé pour le cours 2020. Le cours 2020 sera dispensé exclusivement en ligne, les animateurs ajustant la méthode de prestation et les méthodes et outils d'évaluation afin de continuer à s'engager auprès des participants et à enseigner efficacement les applications et compétences satellitaires. \r\n\r\nLe cours en ligne se déroulera du 4 mai au 26 juin 2020.\r\n","registrationEndDate":1588021200000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":10,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Niger","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":4,"value":"French","iso639":"fr"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1866,"created":1580293251000,"updated":1752481132000,"title":"EGU Short Course: Satellite Data for Atmospheric Applications","titleNative":"EGU Short Course: Satellite Data for Atmospheric Applications","startDate":1588464000000,"endDate":1588809600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"European Geophysical Union","contactPerson":"Federico Fierli, Christian Retascher (ESA), Mark Parrington (CAMS)","contactEmail":"federico.fierli@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://meetingorganizer.copernicus.org/EGU2020/session/36365","description":"The course will be accessible from the EGU online page https://egu2020.eu/sharing_geoscience_online.html\r\n\r\nThis ON-LINE short course is an opportunity to learn about Copernicus data for Atmospheric Composition and to get examples how to develop your own workflows based on sample applications.\r\nThe European Union Copernicus programme is open and free for everyone - whether from academic, governement, or commercial backgrounds. The programme has an operational focus, with satellite constellations and services. Satellite data provides composition vital information on key atmospheric constituents at different spatial and temporal scales with a continuous improvements in observational spatial and temporal resolution, coverage and measured species as well as a constantly evolving added value products from the Copernicus Atmospheric Monitoring Services.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"The sessions will be hands-on and supported by Earth Observation and Model experts to discover data, handle them and produce plots out of a sample of the Copernicus data. You will make use of a series of freely available tools specifically developed for these applications including Jupyter Notebook modules, to have an easy and intuitive way to make use of Python programming. No experience is necessary as various exercises will be provided for a wide range of skill levels and applications.\r\n","targetAudience":"Academic, Young Researches, PhD, Senior Scientists, Services\r\n","registrationHowto":"The course is held within the 2020 EGU conference. The attendance is free for on-line participants","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":9985,"name":"Deleted Trainer","email":"deleted.trainer@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"--","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1886,"created":1588020619000,"updated":1588025383000,"title":"New Set of Forecasting Tools developed by the NOAA/WPC International Desks - 15:00 UTC","titleNative":"New Set of Forecasting Tools developed by the NOAA/WPC International Desks - 15:00 UTC","startDate":1588172400000,"endDate":1588176000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NOAA/WPC International Desk","contactPerson":"Jose Galvez","contactEmail":"jose.galvez@noaa.gov","contactUrl":"https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2170818989560713485","description":"The session is for WMO regions III (South America) and IV (Central America/Caribbean) and usefull for others.  Session time is 15:00 UTC, it will start 15 minutes earlier (at 14:45 UTC for those who want to check their microphone and voice connection).  GoToWebinar is used for both images and voice through the Internet.  ","country":null,"learningOutcome":"Using 5 new diagnostic tools derived from numerical model output, assess 1) the strength of the trade wind inversion, 2) the potential for severe weather and hail, 3) the location of surface fronts, 4) the potential for heavy rainfall and thunderstorms, and 5) the rainfall accumulation potential for the Andes.","summary":null,"content":"As part of our mission of enhancing weather forecasting practices in the Americas, the WPC International Desks have developed five additional diagnostic tools using Wingridds software. After informal routine evaluation, consideration of these tools has produced noticeable positive impacts on the quality of the forecasts produced by the International Desks, and by those of our national and international partners. These diagnostic tools include: (1) TWIN: strength and elevation of the trade wind temperature inversion; (2) GR02T: risk for severe convection and specifically hail; (3) FRONT: detection of surface fronts; (4) EGDI: potential for heavy rainfall and thunderstorms; (5) CAPI: rainfall accumulation potential for the Central Andes. These tools will be introduced to the community via an hour-long presentation on April 29, 2020 at 15UTC. A Spanish version of this presentation will be offered on May 6, 2020 at 15 UTC. Given the limited time for such amount of material, each presentation will be introductory to the basics behind each algorithm, output interpretation, methods to consider their output during forecasting and nowcasting practices, and access to operational loops using GFS model output: https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/international/wng/ .","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"Register here: https://attendee.gotowebinar.com/register/2170818989560713485","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true},{"id":210,"value":"NOAA","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":6,"value":"BIP-M: Basic Instructional Package for Meteorologists"},{"id":8,"value":"PWS: Weather Broadcasters and Communicators"},{"id":11,"value":"Marine: Marine Weather Forecaster"},{"id":13,"value":"PWS: Public Weather Forecasters"},{"id":1,"value":"Aviation: Aeronautical Meteorological Forecaster"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1894,"created":1590993929000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1588165200000,"endDate":1588168800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"TSO EUMeTrain","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html","description":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations and features. Every month the weather briefing is hosted by a different European country.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data.\r\nMaintaining a healthy basis of knowledge about satellite meteorology.\r\nUnderstanding the difference between RGBs as well as their benefits and limitations.\r\n","summary":"Hosted by EUMeTrain, weather briefings are monthly online weather discussions about the phenomena that occurred over the past month, since the last weather briefing. Satellite imag","content":"Weather briefings are recorded. Find a collection of past briefings at http://eumetrain.org/briefings.html and on YouTube at https://www.youtube.com/user/eumetrain/videos.","targetAudience":"The primary target audience are forecasters in shifts, although other meteorologists from different areas are also welcome.","registrationHowto":"In order to join the discussion, please go to http://eumetrain.org/weather_briefings.html. For any questions, please use the Contact email.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1883,"created":1586949242000,"updated":1587119944000,"title":"European research opportunities and priorities for the next generation of meteorological satellites","titleNative":"European research opportunities and priorities for the next generation of meteorological satellites","startDate":1588159800000,"endDate":null,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMETSAT","contactPerson":"Stephan Bojinski","contactEmail":"Stephan.Bojinski@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://eumetsat.zoom.us/j/95826377721?pwd=N2tDR3JwVVRFeUw0Q0NKOXJNYXd0Zz09 ","description":"As part of the EGU2020 Sharing Geoscience Online Townhall events, Bojan Bojkov (Head of Remote Sensing and Products Division, EUMETSAT) presents on \"European research opportunities and priorities for the next generation of meteorological satellites\", an open online event.\r\n \r\nIn the first half of the 2020s, the European Organisation for the Exploitation of Meteorological Satellites (EUMETSAT) and the European Space Agency (ESA) will launch the next generation of meteorological satellites. These provide a continuity of service for the current Meteosat and European Polar System satellites, and extensive opportunities for innovation in meteorological and environmental research.\r\n \r\nFrom geostationary orbit Europe will have for the first time, in addition to higher spectral, spatial and time sample images, an infrared sounder, a lightning imager and the Copernicus Sentinel-4 sounder in the ultraviolet, visible and near infrared spectrum. From polar orbit we will have global coverage from the novel ice cloud imager (ICI), microwave imager, multi-viewing multi-channel multiple-polarisation imaging (3MI) instruments as well as the instruments building on the EPS heritage.\r\n \r\nThe data from each of these instruments offers potential for use in atmospheric process studies (e.g., aerosols, clouds, precipitation-related), weather and climate modelling, and environmental systems research; in addition, the co-location of instruments on the same platform also offers research and development potential.\r\n \r\nIn order to work with data as soon as operationally available, approaches will need to be made now to national and European science agencies to establish priorities and support frameworks.\r\n \r\nBojan Bojkov presents the new programme capabilities and answers questions to support interest by the science community.\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Identify research opportunities and priorities; activate research support frameworks","summary":"As part of the EGU2020 Sharing Geoscience Online Townhall events, Bojan Bojkov (Head of Remote Sensing and Products Division, EUMETSAT) presents on \"European research opportunities","content":"The presentation is followed by Q&A. Questions can also be asked using the Slido tool - please use #nextgen-metsat\r\n\r\n\r\nIn addition, the Townhall event will complemented by a livechat session on this topic, organised as part of EGU2020 Sharing Geosciences Online, on 5 May 2020 at 19.00-20.00 CEST (join at https://meetingorganizer.copernicus.org/EGU2020/session/38159).\r\n\r\nThe annual European Geophysical Union (EGU) General Assembly normally attracts >10 000 participants from all geosciences. This years conference, originally scheduled 4-8 May 2020 in Vienna, has been cancelled due to COVID-19. It is partly replaced by a series of online events termed EGU2020 Sharing Geosciences Online, which are openly accessible at www.egu2020.eu . These include several online training sessions organised by EUMETSAT and partners.","targetAudience":"The main audience for this Townhall are researchers and academia interested in using innovative satellite data for meteorological and related environmental research. ","registrationHowto":"Join the event on Zoom videoconferencing tool at https://tinyurl.com/egu2020. For questions, joins us on Sli.do #nextgen-metsat\r\n ","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2830,"name":"Madalina Ungur as sysadmin","email":"Madalina.Ungur@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions"