pril 14 UTC session will be: \r\n- Use of 1-min resolution satellite imagery coupled to ground-based observations for cloud process determination:John Mecikalski,Atmospheric Science Department, University of Alabama in Huntsville, USA\r\n- Hail Storm Risk Assessment Using Space-Borne Remote Sensing Observations and Reanalysis DataKris Bedka, NASA Langley Research Center, USA","targetAudience":"Operational meteorologists and convection researchers","registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":1587474000000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":80,"value":"Other","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1884,"created":1586957148000,"updated":1593000123000,"title":"Convection Working Group Online Session 1","titleNative":"Convection Working Group Online Session 1","startDate":1587456000000,"endDate":1587459600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"Convection Working Group","contactPerson":"CWG Co-chairs","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://eumetsat.zoom.us/meeting/register/tJUlcuyprTosEte5-oSAlZIWrAqB7DG1UDco","description":"Convection Working Group will host two online sessions on 21 April 2020. At each session two convection experts from the group will make a 20 minutes presentation followed by a general discussion. \r\nThe presentations at 21 April 08 UTC session will be: \r\n- New products for MTG  and NWC SAF Xavier Calbet AEMET, Spain\r\n- NWCSAF Convection ProductsJ.-M. Moisselin, F. Autones, M. Claudon,Météo-France\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"The presentations at 21 April 08 UTC session will be: \r\n- New products for MTG  and NWC SAF Xavier Calbet AEMET, Spain\r\n- NWCSAF Convection ProductsJ.-M. Moisselin, F. Autones, M. Claudon,Météo-France\r\n","targetAudience":"Operational meteorologists and convection researchers","registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":1587459600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":80,"value":"Other","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1875,"created":1583868082000,"updated":1583868402000,"title":"Satellite Remote Sensing for Agricultural Applications","titleNative":"Satellite Remote Sensing for Agricultural Applications","startDate":1586872800000,"endDate":1588714200000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Online","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/water/webinars/remote-sensing-for-agriculture-20?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSTAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=AG-RS-2020","description":"Since the launch of NASAs first Landsat mission in 1972, satellite imagery has been used for global agricultural monitoring, providing one of the longest operational applications for the Landsat program. Although satellite observations of land began with agricultural monitoring, only in recent years has agricultural remote sensing seen reinvigoration among space agencies, national ministries of agriculture, and global initiatives. To monitor agricultural systems, NASA utilizes satellite observations to assess a wide variety of geophysical and biophysical parameters, including: precipitation, temperature, evapotranspiration, soil moisture, and vegetation health.\r\n\r\nPast ARSET webinars on land and water resources covered remote sensing-derived parameters relevant to agriculture within a broader scope. This 4-part introductory webinar will focus on data products, data access, and case-studies demonstrating how remote sensing data can be used for decision-making among the agriculture and food security communities.\r\n\r\nThis training will address how to use remote sensing data for agriculture monitoring, specifically drought and crop monitoring. The webinar will also provide end-users the ability to evaluate which regions of the world agricultural productivity is above or below long-term trends. This informs decisions pertaining to market stability and humanitarian relief.\r\n\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\r\n\r\nGoal 2: End hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition and promote sustainable agriculture\r\n\r\nTarget 2.4: By 2030, ensure sustainable food production systems and implement resilient agricultural practices that increase productivity and production, that help maintain ecosystems, that strengthen capacity for adaptation to climate change, extreme weather, drought, flooding and other disasters and that progressively improve land and soil quality. \r\n\r\n\r\nCourse Dates: April 14, 21, 28, and May 5, 2020\r\n\r\nTimes: 10:00-11:30 & 16:00-17:30 EDT (UTC-4); There will be identical sessions at two different times of the day\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to identify which satellites and sensors can be used for agricultural applications, understand the limitations of remote sensing and modeled data for agriculture and food security, acquire specific remote sensing data products that are appropriate for their work, and apply remote sensing techniques to crop monitoring, drought, and humanitarian relief.","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Four online, 1.5-hour parts with sessions offered twice a day.","targetAudience":"Local, state, federal, private, and non-governmental organizations involved in agricultural monitoring, agricultural policy, food security, agricultural insurance, and humanitarian relief.","registrationHowto":"Registration is free but you must register: https://go.nasa.gov/2v0RtGN","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":2,"value":"II Asia"},{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":6,"value":"V South-West Pacific"},{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"},{"id":1,"value":"I Africa"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1876,"created":1585122305000,"updated":1585128547000,"title":"How to Be Alone Together - Teaching Geosciences in Quarantine","titleNative":"How to Be Alone Together - Teaching Geosciences in Quarantine","startDate":1585576800000,"endDate":1585580400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"COMET","contactPerson":"Tsvetomir Ross-Lazarov","contactEmail":"training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"https://zoom.us/j/548374481","description":"With the coronavirus pandemic impacting everyone at many levels, meteorological training institutions are turning to distance learning to fulfill their mission.  \r\n\r\nThe COMET Program would like to assist you in this transition by sharing best practices and tools for connecting and engaging learners online.  COMET will host a webinar entitled \"How to Be Alone TogetherTeaching Geosciences in Quarantine\" on March 30th, 2020 at 14 UTC.  ","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"","registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5842,"name":"Vesa Nietosvaara","email":"vesa.nietosvaara@eumetsat.int","agency":"","country":"EM","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":103,"value":"Workshop"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":80,"value":"Other","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":15,"value":"Training: Education and Training Providers "}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1872,"created":1582742037000,"updated":1582801528000,"title":"Using the UN Biodiversity Lab to Support National Conservation and Sustainable Development Goals","titleNative":"Utilizando el UN Biodiversity Lab para Apoyar los Objetivos Nacionales de Conservacion y Desarrollo Sostenible","startDate":1585072800000,"endDate":1586287800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/land/webinars/un-biodiversity-2020/es?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSTAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=UN-BIO-ES","description":"This 3-part series, offered in English, Spanish and French, will introduce participants to UN Biodiversity Lab and how it can be used to access and analyze global data in order to provide key information on the Convention on Biological Diversity's (CBD) Aichi Biodiversity Targets and on the nature-based Sustainable Development Goals.\r\n\r\nOffered in partnership with the UN Development Programme (UNDP), this series will teach participants about global biodiversity-based uses of remote sensing. As we enter the fourth industrial revolution, technology is revolutionizing our ability to map nature. Satellite data provide a birds eye, yet incredibly detailed view of the Earths surface in real-time, while drones and mobile apps enable local communities and indigenous peoples to map their knowledge of local ecosystems. To support policymakers to develop data-driven sustainable development solutions, UNDP, the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), and the Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) launched UN Biodiversity Lab, with funding from the GEF and support from MapX, UNEP World Conservation Monitoring Centre, Global Resource Information Database - Geneva, and NASA. The UN Biodiversity Lab is an online platform that allows policymakers and other stakeholders to access global data layers, upload national datasets, and analyze these datasets in combination to provide key information on the CBDs Aichi Biodiversity Targets and on the nature-based Sustainable Development Goals. Already in use by over 50 countries, as well as utilized as the key decision support system for two NASA-funded applied science projects, the UN Biodiversity Lab has high potential to be scaled up to reach new ministries and countries and stakeholder groups.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\r\n\r\nGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts\r\n\r\nGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\r\n\r\nGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, attendees will:\r\n\r\n Understand key global biodiversity and sustainable development policy instruments (CBD, UN Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development) as they relate to conservation efforts\r\n\r\n Have knowledge of spatial data on biodiversity and sustainable development, including data generated by NASA projects\r\n\r\n Be familiar with the UN Biodiversity Lab structure, data, and tools\r\n\r\n Have the ability to apply UN Biodiversity Lab tools to their region of interest\r\n\r\n Utilize case study examples from multiple partner countries as context for their work","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Three, one and half-hour parts that include lectures, demonstrations, and question & answer sessions.","targetAudience":"National policymakers involved in conservation and sustainable development commitments to the CBD, UNFCCC, and 2030 Agenda.","registrationHowto":"Haga clic aquí para registrarse para la sesión en español: https://go.nasa.gov/2OzmKHd","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":false,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"},{"id":20,"value":"Other (see description)"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1873,"created":1582742666000,"updated":1582801491000,"title":"Using the UN Biodiversity Lab to Support National Conservation and Sustainable Development Goals","titleNative":"Utiliser le UN Biodiversity Lab pour soutenir les objectifs nationaux de conservation et de développement durable","startDate":1585062000000,"endDate":1586277000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","contactUrl":"https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/land/webinars/un-biodiversity-2020/fr?utm_source=direct&utm_medium=EUMETSTAT-VLAB&utm_campaign=UN-BIO-FR","description":"This 3-part series, offered in English, Spanish and French, will introduce participants to UN Biodiversity Lab and how it can be used to access and analyze global data in order to provide key information on the Convention on Biological Diversity's (CBD) Aichi Biodiversity Targets and on the nature-based Sustainable Development Goals.\r\n\r\nOffered in partnership with the UN Development Programme (UNDP), this series will teach participants about global biodiversity-based uses of remote sensing. As we enter the fourth industrial revolution, technology is revolutionizing our ability to map nature. Satellite data provide a birds eye, yet incredibly detailed view of the Earths surface in real-time, while drones and mobile apps enable local communities and indigenous peoples to map their knowledge of local ecosystems. To support policymakers to develop data-driven sustainable development solutions, UNDP, the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), and the Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) launched UN Biodiversity Lab, with funding from the GEF and support from MapX, UNEP World Conservation Monitoring Centre, Global Resource Information Database - Geneva, and NASA. The UN Biodiversity Lab is an online platform that allows policymakers and other stakeholders to access global data layers, upload national datasets, and analyze these datasets in combination to provide key information on the CBDs Aichi Biodiversity Targets and on the nature-based Sustainable Development Goals. Already in use by over 50 countries, as well as utilized as the key decision support system for two NASA-funded applied science projects, the UN Biodiversity Lab has high potential to be scaled up to reach new ministries and countries and stakeholder groups.\r\n\r\nRelevant UN Sustainable Development Goals:\r\n\r\nGoal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts\r\n\r\nGoal 14: Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development\r\n\r\nGoal 15: Protect, restore and promote sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems, sustainably manage forests, combat desertification, and halt and reverse land degradation and halt biodiversity loss","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Learning Objectives: By the end of this training, attendees will:\r\n\r\n Understand key global biodiversity and sustainable development policy instruments (CBD, UN Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development) as they relate to conservation efforts\r\n\r\n Have knowledge of spatial data on biodiversity and sustainable development, including data generated by NASA projects\r\n\r\n Be familiar with the UN Biodiversity Lab structure, data, and tools\r\n\r\n Have the ability to apply UN Biodiversity Lab tools to their region of interest\r\n\r\n Utilize case study examples from multiple partner countries as context for their work","summary":null,"content":"Course Format: Three, one and half-hour parts that include lectures, demonstrations, and question & answer sessions.","targetAudience":"National policymakers involved in conservation and sustainable development commitments to the CBD, UNFCCC, and 2030 Agenda.","registrationHowto":"Inscription français: https://go.nasa.gov/3biP9v2","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"",