ning@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"A 1 week course on advanced Satellite Meteorology for South Eastern European countries, covering advanced topics of satellite meteorology and satellite image interpretation and their application in operational weather forecasting.","country":{"id":"HR","name":"CROATIA","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"The course is expected to improve the skills of operational meteorologists in interpretation of satellite images and products. ","summary":"A 1 week course on advanced Satellite Meteorology for South Eastern European countries, covering advanced topics of satellite meteorology and satellite image interpretation and the","content":"","targetAudience":"The course is addressed to weather forecasters working in national hydro-meteorological institutes of South Eastern European countries. Primary countries: Montenegro, Albania, Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina, Republic of Srpska, FYROM, Kosovo, Serbia. Secondary countries: Slovenia, Croatia, Bulgaria, Romania, Hungary and Greece.\r\n","registrationHowto":"Applications closed!\r\n\r\nLink for application: http://training.eumetsat.int/mod/questionnaire/view.php?id=10205 .","registrationEndDate":1520553600000,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":2,"value":"By invitation only"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":50,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":true},{"id":70,"value":"NHMS","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":16,"value":"Eumetcal","visible":false},{"id":17,"value":"EuMeTrain","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":101,"value":"Serbo-Croatian","iso639":"sh"},{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1682,"created":1523892496000,"updated":1523960812000,"title":"Monitoring Tropical Storms for Emergency Preparedness ","titleNative":"Monitoring Tropical Storms for Emergency Preparedness ","startDate":1525305600000,"endDate":1525910400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brockbl1@umbc.edu","contactUrl":"https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/disasters/webinars/18-tropical-storms","description":"Tropical storms have major impacts, including loss of life and destruction of property. In 2017 alone, the United States experienced three tropical storms with more than 1 billion dollar in losses. Open source satellite data can be used before, during, and after a storm for monitoring and response. A storms intensity, path, wind, precipitation, storm surge, and flooding can be derived from historical and near real-time satellite observations. In this introductory webinar, participants will learn about the NASA data and tools they can use to monitor tropical storms.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"By the end of the training, attendees will be able to identify remote sensing data and tools relevant to tropical storms, monitor conditions before, during, and after a storm using remote sensing data, and understand how remote sensing data can be used in decision-making activities","summary":null,"content":"Course Date and Time: Thursdays, May 3, 2018 and May 10, 2018. There are 2 identical sessions provided twice per day to accommodate an international audience.  09:00  11:00 and 18:00  20:00 EDT (UTC-4). ","targetAudience":"This training is primarily intended for individuals and organizations engaged in emergency management, such as relief organizations, transportation and utility providers, public health professionals, insurance providers.","registrationHowto":"This webinar is free and open but you must register. Please only register for one of the daily sessions: You can check your local time to select your session preference.","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":25,"value":"NASA","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1627,"created":1510128011000,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Basic Satellite Course","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Basic Satellite Course","startDate":1525219200000,"endDate":1529193600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"","contactPerson":"EUMeTrain TSO","contactEmail":"tso@eumetrain.org","contactUrl":"","description":"Mainly asynchronous course with 3 (4) synchronous playground sessions where participants will be able to interact with the material and each other. The total length of the event is 7 weeks starting from 2 May to 17 June, 2018. In that time period there are going to be three main blocks, each in the duration of 2 weeks with a 'Refresher' block at the beginning of the course.\r\nAt the end, if all requirements met, participants will receive a certificate of successfully passing the course.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"After completing the course, participants are expected to be able to:\r\n\r\n1) Identify surface features:\r\n a) Identify terrain and geographical features in satellite data\r\n b) Identify surface characteristics and conditions, including dry/wet, different vegetation types, sand and desert\r\n2) Identify cloud types and their characteristics:\r\n a) Identify fronts, baroclinic zones and areas with cyclogenesis\r\n b) Identify Cumulonimbus clouds\r\n c) Identify fog and low stratus cloudiness\r\n3) Locate, identify and determine the strength, the characteristics of atmospheric phenomena:\r\n a) Dust and sand storms, plumes and areas of raised dust\r\n b) Fires and smoke\r\n c) Precipitation types and amounts\r\n d) Volcanic ash particulates and chemical emissions\r\n e) Aerosol and particulate pollution\r\n f) Features indicating regions of clear air turbulence ","summary":"Mainly asynchronous course with 3 (4) synchronous playground sessions where participants will be able to interact with the material and each other. The total length of the event is","content":"This course is considered to be competency-based and primarily tailored for the weather forecasters and meteorologists, so they can fulfill their everyday duties easier and with more accuracy. It covers: Identification of different surface features, cloud types and their characteristics and weather phenomena in satellite imagery and products. The course will contain quizzes, playground sessions and exercises for self-assessment.","targetAudience":"The main target audience are forecasters working in shifts. The secondary audience are met