arningOutcome":"Satellite imagery and products real-time applications","summary":null,"content":"","targetAudience":"Forecasters, Students, Researchers, Trainers","registrationHowto":"","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5859,"name":"Jose Galvez","email":"jose.galvez@colostate.edu","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":18,"value":"NOAA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":12,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Costa Rica","visible":false},{"id":18,"value":"NOAA","visible":false},{"id":2,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Barbados","visible":false},{"id":3,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Argentina","visible":false},{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"},{"id":7,"value":"Spanish","iso639":"es"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"},{"id":4,"value":"IV North America, Central America and Caribbean"}],"qualification":[{"id":17,"value":"Satellite Skills and Knowledge"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1618,"created":1509109390000,"updated":1509114752000,"title":"Fire detection and monitoring by satellite","titleNative":"Portuguese","startDate":1505736000000,"endDate":1505937600000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"São José dos Campos, SP, Brazil","host":"INPE","contactPerson":"Alberto Setzer/SELPLER","contactEmail":"cursos@selperbrasil.org.br","contactUrl":"http://www.selperbrasil.org.br/cursos/curso_queimadas_sjc.php","description":"This course is focused on the operational monitoring of fire spots detected by satellite, with estimates of burning locations and areas, and use of forecast models for fire risk.   \r\nThe course is open to participants from different locations. Fees apply. For more information, please consult the website.","country":{"id":"BR","name":"BRAZIL","region":"RA-III","type":"Rest of the World"},"learningOutcome":"The trainees are expected to develop skills on the remote sensing technology developed by INPE for fire detection using multiplatform polar orbiting and geostationary satellites, identifying surface features, hot spots, and burnt areas on satellite imagery. They should also integrate information obtained from satellite imagery with meteorological data and cartographic information using Geographic Information System (GIS). The trainees are also expected to learn how to disseminate the information through internet webservices. ","summary":null,"content":"The course will provide lectures on the operational data sources for vegetation fire detection and monitoring, and the use of the various products e.g., hotspots, risk and fire meteorology, as well as burnt area estimation. The products that will be presented are the ones developed by INPEs fire monitoring program as well as from other well-known institutions such as NASA, NOAA and ESA, with emphasis on: the different products obtained from polar orbiting and geostationary satellites, the choice of the best spectral channels to use for each application; the different spatial and temporal resolutions of the meteorological satellites (NOAA, AQUA, TERRA, GOES and METEOSAT) and environmental satellites (LANDSAT, CBERS, RESOURCESAT); building historical temporal series; spatial analyses for data integration; and information extraction techniques from medium resolution satellite imagery. Real case studies and practical exercises will also be provided during the course.  \r\n","targetAudience":"Environmental agents, researchers and disaster management operational services interested in satellite technology for fire detection and monitoring.  ","registrationHowto":"Registration at: http://www.selperbrasil.org.br/cursos/curso_queimadas_sjc.php","registrationEndDate":1505692800000,"fees":"Yes","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2842,"name":"Natalia Rudorff","email":"natalia.rudorff@inpe.br","agency":"INPE","country":"BR","programme":"VLab","trainingPartner":{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":90,"value":"VLab","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":4,"value":"VLab Centre of Excellence Brazil","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":5,"value":"Portuguese","iso639":"pt"}],"regions":[{"id":3,"value":"III South America"}],"qualification":[{"id":10,"value":"PWS: Advisors Supporting Disaster Prevention and Mitigation and Other User Activities"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1583,"created":null,"updated":null,"title":"9th European Conference on Severe Storms (ECSS) 2017","titleNative":"9th European Conference on Severe Storms (ECSS) 2017","startDate":1505692800000,"endDate":1506038400000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Pula","host":"Meteorological and Hydrological Service of Croatia (DHMZ)","contactPerson":"ECSS Team","contactEmail":"ecss@essl.org","contactUrl":"https://www.essl.org/cms/european-conferences-on-severe-storms/ecss-2017/","description":"The scope of the conference covers all aspects of severe convective storms. Researchers, operational forecasters, and risk and emergency managers are invited to submit contributions. In light of the global relevance of the conference themes, participants from all over the world are encouraged to attend. ","country":{"id":"HR","name":"CROATIA","region":"RA-VI","type":"Member State"},"learningOutcome":"","summary":null,"content":"These are the conference topics (and tentative session titles):\r\n- Convective storm and tornado dynamics;\r\n- Numerical modelling of storms, storm-scale data assimilation;\r\n- Impact of storms on society, impact mitigation and early warning systems;\r\n- Convective storms within extratropical, Mediterranean and tropical cyclones;\r\n- Floods and flash floods;\r\n- Forecasting and nowcasting of severe weather;\r\n- Severe weather forecast training;\r\n- Satellite studies of storms and their environment;\r\n- Radar studies of storms;\r\n- Storm electrification, lightning, microphysics and hail;\r\n- Storm climatologies, risk assessments and climate change;\r\n- Collection of storm data, historical events and damage assessment.\r\n\r\n","targetAudience":"Researchers, operational forecasters, and risk and emergency managers are invited to submit contributions. ESSL wholeheartedly invites participants from all over the world to attend.","registrationHowto":"Abstract submission and registration for the conference are now possible on the ECSS website http://meetings.copernicus.org/ecss2017/registration.html . \r\nThere, you will find further details. The deadline for abstract submission is 20 March 2017. Early-bird registration rates are available until 15 June 2017.\r\n","registrationEndDate":1497484800000,"fees":"Yes","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":2829,"name":"Joana Betencourt ","email":"Joana.Betencourt@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"DE","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":null,"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":102,"value":"Conference"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":80,"value":"Other","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT","visible":false}],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[{"id":21,"value":"Not Applicable"}],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1595,"created":null,"updated":1693586463000,"title":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","titleNative":"EUMeTrain Weather Briefing","startDate":1505131200000,"endDate":1505134800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"EUMeTrain","contactPerson":"Marko Blaskovic","contactEmail":"blaskovic@cirus.dhz.hr","contactUrl":"https://met-learning.webex.com/met-learning/k2/j.php?MTID=t3b37a9a8a2c70e625805ae6ac0ca8a01","description":"Online weather discussion about the phenomena that occurred in the last month, after the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations. Every month another country, another presenter presents the weather events. The Weather Briefing is hosted by EUMeTrain. Any additional questions can be asked via the provided email.","country":{"id":"--","name":"ONLINE","region":null,"type":"Other"},"learningOutcome":"Gaining additional knowledge about analyzing weather situations using satellite data. Being able to analyze satellite imagery by yourself.","summary":"Online weather discussion about the phenomena that occurred in the last month, after the last weather briefing. Satellite imagery is used to discuss interesting situations. Every m","content":"Every weather briefing concentrates on some new events that occurred in the last month.","targetAudience":"Any meteorological audience is welcome to these sessions, though these briefings will be of most use to forecasters.","registrationHowto":"https://met-learning.webex.com/met-learning/k2/j.php?MTID=t3b37a9a8a2c70e625805ae6ac0ca8a01","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5837,"name":"Mark Higgins","email":"mark.higgins@eumetsat.int","agency":"EUMETSAT","country":"EM","programme":"EUMETSAT","trainingPartner":{"id":15,"value":"EUMETSAT"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":104,"value":"Discussion"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":40,"value":"EUMeTrain","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[{"id":7,"value":"VI Europe"}],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1596,"created":null,"updated":null,"title":"Introduction to Remote Sensing for Scenario-Based Ecoforecasting","titleNative":"Introduction to Remote Sensing for Scenario-Based Ecoforecasting","startDate":1504800000000,"endDate":1506618000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brockbl1@umbc.edu","contactUrl":"https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/land/webinars/scenario-based-ecoforecasting-17","description":"As climates shift and change, ecosystems respond in a variety of ways. Assessing the ecological impacts of a changing climate is crucial for natural resource management decision-making. NASA Earth observations can be used to help make these decisions. This four-week webinar series, in collaboration with the USGS North Central Climate Center, will include an overview of the satellites, sensors, and tools relevant to ecological forecasting; available climate products and data for ecological modeling; scenario planning; and the application of modeling for species distribution and state-and-transition simulations.\r\nCourse Format: \r\nFour, one-hour sessions \r\nSessions will be held on Thursdays in September, at 12:00-1:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)","country":null,"learningOutcome":"By the end of this training, attendees will be able to understand what ecoforecasting is and its applications, understand how climate data supports future projections of management targets, articulate how scenario planning can be utilized for decision-making with multiple sources of uncertainty, and integrate qualitative and quantitative tools to support resource management decision-making in situations with high uncertainty.","summary":null,"content":"A general description of each of the four sessions is available in the course website.","targetAudience":"Local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations charged with maintaining healthy ecosystems, and interested in ecological forecasting using satellite imagery and climate data. ","registrationHowto":"There is no cost for the webinar, but you must register through the training's webpage","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1597,"created":null,"updated":null,"title":"Introduction to Remote Sensing of Harmful Algal Blooms","titleNative":"Introduction to Remote Sensing of Harmful Algal Blooms","startDate":1504569600000,"endDate":1506384000000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"","host":"NASA Applied Remote Sensing Training Program (ARSET)","contactPerson":"Brock Blevins","contactEmail":"brockbl1@umbc.edu","contactUrl":"https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/water/webinars/HABs17","description":"Harmful algal blooms (HABs) can have a negative impact on the ecosystem and human health. Satellite remote sensing is able to collect data frequently and over a large area to identify impaired water quality from HABs. This data can inform decision-makers on where best to put their resources for taking water samples, determine what toxins are in the water, whether they need to change or move drinking water intakes, and whether a fishery needs to be closed. Remote sensing data enables individuals and organizations to have more flexible plans for water sampling. It also leads to a more efficient and appropriate allocation of resources for protecting human health.\r\nCourse Format: \r\nFour, one hour sessions\r\nSessions will be held on Tuesdays in September: September 5, 12, 19, and 26 at 11:00 a.m. -12:00 p.m. or 21:00-22:00 p.m. EDT (UTC-4)","country":null,"learningOutcome":"By the end of the training, attendees will be able to identify NASAs Earth Science remote sensing data products for the identification and monitoring of HABs, describe how coupled remote sensing and modeling approaches are used in decision support tools, and use a selection of NASA Earth Science data tools to monitor HABs.","summary":null,"content":"A general description of each of the four sessions is available in the course website.","targetAudience":"Local, regional, state, federal, and international organizations interested in using satellite imagery for coastal and ocean applications.","registrationHowto":"There is no cost for the webinar, but you must register through the training's webpage: https://arset.gsfc.nasa.gov/water/webinars/HABs17","registrationEndDate":null,"fees":"No","awardGranted":null,"creator":{"id":5861,"name":"Brock Blevins","email":"brock.blevins@nasa.gov","agency":"","country":"US","programme":"","trainingPartner":{"id":25,"value":"NASA"},"userIdentifier":null,"blocked":null,"lastAccess":null,"webGroup":null,"profile":null,"aclRoleIds":null,"aclGroupIds":null},"approver":null,"leadTrainer":null,"aclGroup":{"id":2,"name":"EUMETSAT"},"statusUpdate":null,"eventType":{"id":101,"value":"Course"},"attendance":{"id":1,"value":"Open"},"status":{"id":2,"value":"Confirmed"},"condition":"DEFINITE","format":null,"attendeeCount":0,"external":false,"previouslyConfirmed":true,"initiative":[{"id":120,"value":"NASA-ARSET","visible":true}],"trainingPartner":[],"programmes":[],"language":[{"id":3,"value":"English","iso639":"en"}],"regions":[],"qualification":[],"eumDataApplications":[],"eumDataAccessTypes":[],"eumSatelliteMissions":[],"eumSensors":[]},{"id":1598,"created":null,"updated":null,"title":"Using the Copernicus Marine Data Stream for Ocean Applications","titleNative":"Using the Copernicus Marine Data Stream for Ocean Applications","startDate":1504483200000,"endDate":1504828800000,"tbdDate":null,"city":"Ostende","host":"IODE","contactPerson":"Copernicus Training Team","contactEmail":"copernicus.training@eumetsat.int","contactUrl":"","description":"The purpose of the event is to develop skills in using data from EUMETSAT's Copernicus Marine Data Stream for oceanographic applications. We will focus on the application of data from Sentinel-3. ","country":{"id